0 and 1. If we can find a nice expression for the area of one such ring, and if we find a nice way to add them all up, it might lead us to an understanding of the full circle\'s area.\n\nèšç„¦å…¶ä¸­ä¸€ä¸ªåŒå¿ƒåœ†çŽ¯ï¼Œç§°å…¶åŠå¾„ä¸ºrï¼ˆåœ¨0åˆ°1ä¹‹é—´å–å€¼ï¼‰ã€‚å¦‚æžœèƒ½æ‰¾åˆ°å•çŽ¯é¢ç§¯çš„æ°å½“è¡¨è¾¾å¼ï¼Œå¹¶æ‰¾åˆ°ç›¸åŠ æ±‚å’Œçš„æœ‰æ•ˆæ–¹æ³•ï¼Œæˆ‘ä»¬å°±èƒ½ç†è§£æ•´åœ†çš„é¢ç§¯ã€‚\n\nThis figure highlights a band of area formed from dividing the circle into concentric rings.\n\nè¿™å¼ å›¾ç¤ºçªå‡ºäº†é€šè¿‡åŒå¿ƒåœ†åˆ†å‰²å½¢æˆçš„çŽ¯çŠ¶åŒºåŸŸã€‚\n\nIn the spirit of using what will come to be standard calculus notations, let\'s call the thickness of one of these rings â€œdrâ€, which you can read as meaning â€œthe tiny difference in radius from one ring to the nextâ€. In the drawing above, for example, dr = 0.1.\n\néµå¾ªæœªæ¥å°†æˆä¸ºæ ‡å‡†å¾®ç§¯åˆ†ç¬¦å·çš„ç²¾ç¥žï¼Œæˆ‘ä»¬ç§°çŽ¯çš„åŽšåº¦ä¸º\"dr\"ï¼ˆå¯ç†è§£ä¸º\"ç›¸é‚»åœ†çŽ¯çš„å¾®å°åŠå¾„å·®\"ï¼‰ã€‚ä¾‹å¦‚ä¸Šå›¾æ‰€ç¤ºæ¡ˆä¾‹ä¸­ï¼Œdr = 0.1ã€‚\n\nYou should know that some people, perhaps even most people, would object to our using the notation dr to represent a specific not-infinitely-small size like this, suggesting instead we use some other notation, like Î”r. Well, it\'s my house and my rules, and I have my reasons, which we\'ll get into later. For now, let\'s think about dr as simply being some number without worrying about what phrases like â€œinfinitely smallâ€ would mean.\n\néœ€è¦è¯´æ˜Žï¼šéƒ¨åˆ†äººï¼ˆæˆ–è®¸æ˜¯å¤šæ•°äººï¼‰ä¼šåå¯¹ç”¨drè¡¨ç¤ºéžæ— é™å°çš„å…·ä½“åŽšåº¦ï¼Œå»ºè®®æ”¹ç”¨Î”rç­‰ç¬¦å·ã€‚ä¸è¿‡è¿™æ˜¯æˆ‘çš„åœ°ç›˜æˆ‘åšä¸»ï¼Œæˆ‘æœ‰è‡ªå·±çš„ç†ç”±ï¼ˆç¨åŽè¯¦è¿°ï¼‰ã€‚çŽ°åœ¨åªéœ€å°†drè§†ä¸ºå…·ä½“æ•°å­—ï¼Œæš‚ä¸æ·±ç©¶\"æ— é™å°\"çš„å«ä¹‰ã€‚\n\nWhich of the following is closest to the area of one of these rings with inner radius r and thickness dr?\n\nä»¥ä¸‹å“ªä¸ªé€‰é¡¹æœ€æŽ¥è¿‘å†…åŠå¾„ä¸ºrã€åŽšåº¦ä¸ºdrçš„åœ†çŽ¯é¢ç§¯ï¼Ÿ\n\n- Ï€rÂ²Â·dr\n\n- 2Ï€rÂ²Â·dr\n\n- 2Ï€rÂ·dr\n\n- Ï€rÂ·dr\n\n- Ï€rÂ²Â·dr\n\n- 2Ï€rÂ²Â·dr\n\n- 2Ï€rÂ·dr\n\n- Ï€rÂ·dr','PLAIN_TEXT','PLAIN_TEXT','PLAIN_TEXT','PLAIN_TEXT','PLAIN_TEXT'),(528,1008,'333','','My goal is for you to come away from this series feeling like you could have invented calculus. That is, cover these core ideas, but in a way that makes clear where they actually come from, and what they really mean, using an all-around visual approach.\nInventing math is no joke, and there is a difference between being told why something makes sense and actually generating it from scratch. But at all points I want you to think to yourself if you were an early mathematician pondering these ideas and drawing the right diagrams, does it feel reasonable that you could have stumbled upon these truths yourself?\n\nIn this chapter, I want to show how you might stumble into the core ideas of calculus by thinking deeply about one very specific bit of geometry: The area of a circle. Maybe you know that the area is Ï€ multiplied by the radius R squared, but why? Is there a way to think about where this formula comes from?\n\nThis figure illustrates the area of a circle with radius R and the formula that calculates its area.\n![æˆªå›¾ 2026-01-30 21-44-12.png](/api/media/9/download)\n\nContemplating this problem and leaving yourself open to generalizing the tools you use along the way can actually lead you to a glimpse of three big ideas in calculus: Integrals, derivatives, and the fact that they are opposites.\n\nBut the story of finding the area starts more simply: Just you, and a circle. To be concrete, let\'s say the radius is 1 unit. If you weren\'t worried about the exact area, and just wanted to estimate it, one way you might go about it is to chop the circle into smaller pieces whose areas are easier to approximate, then add up the results.\n\nThere are many ways you might go about this, each of which may lead to its own interesting line of reasoning.\n\nThis figure illustrates different ways to divide the area of the circle with a radius 1.\n\n![æˆªå›¾ 2026-01-29 23-42-55.png](/api/media/8/download)\n\nMath has a tendency to reward you when you respect symmetry, so among the divisions shown above, either the bottom left one dividing into pizza slices or the bottom right which divides into concentric rings will be likely to lead us down a fruitful path. For now, let\'s think about the division into concentric rings.\n\nFocus on just one of those concentric rings, and let\'s call its radius r, which will be some number between 0 and 1. If we can find a nice expression for the area of one such ring, and if we find a nice way to add them all up, it might lead us to an understanding of the full circle\'s area.\n\nThis figure highlights a band of area formed from dividing the circle into concentric rings.\n\nIn the spirit of using what will come to be standard calculus notations, let\'s call the thickness of one of these rings â€œdrâ€, which you can read as meaning â€œthe tiny difference in radius from one ring to the nextâ€. In the drawing above, for example, dr = 0.1.\n\nYou should know that some people, perhaps even most people, would object to our using the notation dr to represent a specific not-infinitely-small size like this, suggesting instead we use some other notation, like Î”r. Well, it\'s my house and my rules, and I have my reasons, which we\'ll get into later. For now, let\'s think about dr as simply being some number without worrying about what phrases like â€œinfinitely smallâ€ would mean.\n\nWhich of the following is closest to the area of one of these rings with inner radius r and thickness dr?\n\n- Ï€rÂ²Â·dr\n\n- 2Ï€rÂ²Â·dr\n\n- 2Ï€rÂ·dr\n\n- Ï€rÂ·dr\n','MARKDOWN','\n\nUrbanization means more people are moving from rural areas to cities. This trend can bring many benefits, but also causes some problems.  \n\nCities offer better job opportunities and higher incomes for many people. Economic growth in cities helps improve public services like hospitals, schools, and transportation systems. Additionally, living in cities often gives people easier access to cultural activities and modern technology.  \n\nHowever, rapid urbanization creates challenges. As cities become more crowded, housing shortages often occur, making living costs rise. Traffic jams and air pollution become worse with increasing populations. Public transportation systems often struggle to serve everyone properly.  \n\nTo make cities better for living, governments need good urban planning. Important steps include building affordable housing, developing efficient public transport, and creating more green spaces like parks. Solving these problems will help cities continue growing while ensuring a good quality of life for their residents.','**Rewritten Article:**\n\nIn recent decades, mounting evidence has underscored the profound impact of human activities on global ecosystems. The escalating exploitation of natural resources, coupled with unchecked industrial expansion, has precipitated widespread environmental degradation. Notably, deforestation and fossil fuel combustion have been identified as primary catalysts for climate change, manifesting in rising global temperatures and erratic weather patterns. Concurrently, the proliferation of plastic waste has engendered a crisis in marine environments, with microplastics infiltrating food chains and posing health risks to both wildlife and humans.\n\nEfforts to mitigate these challenges have gained international momentum, albeit with disparities in implementation across nations. Developed countries have pioneered renewable energy technologies, such as solar and wind power, to reduce carbon emissions. Conversely, many developing regions continue to rely heavily on non-renewable resources due to economic constraints. Environmental advocacy groups emphasize that systemic change necessitates not only technological innovation but also policy reform and public education. The transition toward sustainability demands collaborative frameworks that reconcile economic growth with ecological preservation, highlighting the imperative for global cooperation in addressing this multifaceted crisis.','\n\nI notice your request appears to be incomplete - the \"Original article\" section currently contains no text to rewrite. To properly fulfill your request for an academic rewrite, could you please provide the full original article text? This will enable me to apply appropriate scholarly language and structure while maintaining fidelity to the source material. I\'m ready to proceed once I receive the complete content.',NULL,'','MEDIUM',0,NULL,'',0,0,1,'APPROVED',1,'2026-02-04 21:26:47',NULL,'I\'ll create the requested Chinese-English parallel text as soon as you provide the original article. Please paste the English text you want translated and formatted in parallel format.','\n\n```markdown\nMy goal in this series is for you to finish feeling like you could have created calculus yourself. We\'ll explain key ideas, but show clearly where they come from and what they really mean, using pictures to help.\n\nCreating math isn\'t easy. Knowing why something works is different from building it from nothing. But as you read, imagine you\'re an early mathematician. By drawing pictures and thinking carefully, could you have discovered these ideas?\n\nThis chapter shows how you might find calculus ideas by studying one geometry problem: **the area of a circle**. You may know the formula is Ï€ times radius R squared, but why? Where does this come from?\n\nThis picture shows a circle with radius R and its area formula:\n![æˆªå›¾ 2026-01-30 21-44-12.png](/api/media/9/download)\n\nThinking deeply about this problem can lead to three big calculus ideas: integrals, derivatives, and how they connect.\n\nLet\'s find the circle\'s area simply. Imagine a circle with radius 1. To estimate its area, we can cut the circle into small pieces that are easier to measure, then add those areas.\n\nThere are many ways to cut a circle. Each way can teach us different things.\n\nThis image shows different ways to divide a radius-1 circle:\n![æˆªå›¾ 2026-01-29 23-42-55.png](/api/media/8/download)\n\nMath often rewards those who use balance. For us, cutting like pizza slices (bottom left) or into rings (bottom right) will help most. For now, let\'s use rings.\n\nLook at one ring with radius r (between 0 and 1). If we find its area and add all rings together, we can find the whole circle\'s area.\n\nThis shows a ring\'s area when you cut the circle into rings:\n![å›¾ç‰‡å ä½ç¬¦è¯´æ˜Ž]\n\nWe\'ll use standard calculus signs. Let the ring\'s thickness be **dr** (meaning \"tiny radius change between rings\"). In our picture, dr = 0.1.\n\n*Note: Some people prefer different signs (like Î”r), but we\'ll use **dr**. We won\'t worry about \"infinitely small\" size nowâ€”think of **dr** as a small number.*\n\n**Question:** Which answer best gives the area of a ring with inner radius r and thickness dr?\n\n- Ï€rÂ²Â·dr  \n- 2Ï€rÂ²Â·dr  \n- 2Ï€rÂ·dr  \n- Ï€rÂ·dr\n```','\n# Understanding Calculus Through the Area of a Circle\n\nThroughout this series, I aim to empower you with the conviction that you could have independently developed calculus. Our approach will clarify foundational concepts by revealing their origins and true significance through comprehensive visual explanations. While mathematical innovation demands rigorous thinking, we will distinguish between passive acceptance and genuine reconstruction of ideas. Continuously ask yourself: If you were an early scholar contemplating these principles through geometric diagrams, might you have naturally uncovered these truths?\n\n## The Origin of the Circle Area Formula\nThis chapter explores how profound reflection on circular geometryâ€”specifically, calculating its areaâ€”can illuminate essential calculus concepts. Most recognize that a circle\'s area equals Ï€ multiplied by the square of its radius (Ï€RÂ²), but how might one derive this relationship? Can we comprehend the origin of this formula?\n\n![Circle with radius R and area formula](</api/media/9/download>)  \n*Figure: Circle of radius R with its area formula.*\n\nBy thoroughly investigating this problem and generalizing our methodological tools, we encounter three fundamental calculus principles: **integrals**, **derivatives**, and their **inverse relationship**. Our journey begins humbly with a single circleâ€”assume a radius of 1 unit. To approximate its area, we can partition the circle into simpler, estimable components and aggregate their areas.\n\n## Partitioning Strategies and Symmetry\nMultiple dissection approaches offer unique insights, as shown below. Mathematics often rewards symmetry, making either the pizza-slice divisions (bottom left) or concentric rings (bottom right) particularly promising pathways.\n\n![Various circle divisions](</api/media/8/download>)  \n*Figure: Alternative methods for subdividing a unit-radius circle.*\n\nWe will focus on concentric rings. Consider one ring with radius *r* (where 0 â‰¤ *r* â‰¤ 1) and thickness **dr**â€”denoting the minute radial increment between adjacent rings. Here, **dr** might be 0.1 (as illustrated), though its finite size may invite semantic debates. For our purposes, **dr** represents a measurable thickness; deeper philosophical discussions about infinitesimals will follow later.\n\n## Area Approximation of a Single Ring\nWhich expression best approximates the area of a thin ring with inner radius *r* and thickness *dr*?  \n- Ï€rÂ²Â·dr  \n- 2Ï€rÂ²Â·dr  \n- 2Ï€rÂ·dr  \n- Ï€rÂ·dr  \n\n*(Retain this question while we develop the reasoning in subsequent sections.)*\n\nBy analyzing these rings and summing their contributions, we will formulate an intuitive understanding of the circle\'s total area. This methodology establishes a foundational framework for integral calculus.','```markdown\n# Methodological Foundations of Calculus Through Geometric Analysis\n\nThe pedagogical objective of this treatise is to facilitate readers\' conceptual grasp of calculus fundamentals such that they might plausibly reconstruct its theoretical framework through first principles. We shall elucidate core concepts while rigorously demonstrating their epistemological origins through predominantly visual methodologies.  \n\nMathematical discovery constitutes a non-trivial endeavor, with substantive distinction existing between comprehending established theorems and formulating them ab initio. Throughout this exposition, readers are encouraged to adopt the epistemological perspective of early mathematicians: Given appropriate diagrammatic representations and sustained analytical contemplation, might plausible pathways emerge for autonomous discovery of these fundamental truths?\n\n## Geometric Motivation: The Circle Area Problem\n\nThis chapter examines how foundational calculus concepts may emerge organically from systematic investigation of elementary geometry - specifically, the area computation of circular domains. While the formulation $A = Ï€R^2$ remains universally familiar, its ontological justification warrants deeper examination. What fundamental reasoning might yield this principal relationship?\n\n![æˆªå›¾ 2026-01-30 21-44-12.png](/api/media/9/download)\n\nDiagram 1 illustrates the circular domain with radius $R$ and its corresponding area formula.\n\nThorough interrogation of this geometric problem, when coupled with systematic generalization of analytical techniques, reveals profound connections to three central calculus concepts: integral sums, differential quotients, and their inverse operational relationship (The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus).\n\n## Estimation Through Domain Decomposition\n\nOur analytical journey commences with elementary decomposition: Consider a unit circle ($R=1$). Initial approximation strategies involve partitioning the domain into constituent elements whose areas prove more computationally tractable, subsequently aggregated through summation.\n\nMultiple decomposition methodologies present themselves, each potentially yielding distinct but valuable analytical insights:\n\n![æˆªå›¾ 2026-01-29 23-42-55.png](/api/media/8/download)\n\nDiagram 2 demonstrates varied approaches to circular domain subdivision.\n\nMathematical analysis frequently rewards symmetrical consideration. Among the illustrated techniques, the sectorial partition (lower left) and annular decomposition (lower right) prove particularly productive. We shall focus our present examination on the concentric annular approach.\n\n## Analytical Framework for Annular Elements\n\nConsider an individual concentric ring with arbitrary radius $r \\in [0,1]$. Our methodological objective becomes twofold: Establish an accurate expression for the differential area $dA$ of said annular element, then develop summation techniques for complete domain integration.\n\nFollowing emergent calculus notation conventions, let $dr$ represent the radial thickness of each annular element (interpreted as the infinitesimal radial increment between consecutive rings). The illustrative example employs $dr = 0.1$.\n\nWhile traditional calculus pedagogy might advocate alternative notations (e.g., $Î”r$) for non-infinitesimal quantities, we intentionally adopt the $dr$ convention here - with forthcoming justification for this notational choice. For present purposes, consider $dr$ as a finite real number, temporarily prescinding from metaphysical considerations regarding infinitesimals.\n\n## Formal Problem Statement\n\nGiven an annular element with inner radius $r$ and thickness $dr$, which of the following expressions most accurately approximates its area $dA$?  \n\n1. $Ï€rÂ²Â·dr$  \n2. $2Ï€rÂ²Â·dr$  \n3. $2Ï€rÂ·dr$  \n4. $Ï€rÂ·dr$\n```','My goal is for you to come away from this series feeling like you could have invented calculus. That is, cover these core ideas, but in a way that makes clear where they actually come from, and what they really mean, using an all-around visual approach.\næˆ‘çš„ç›®æ ‡æ˜¯è®©ä½ è¯»å®Œè¿™ä¸ªç³»åˆ—åŽï¼Œæ„Ÿè§‰åƒæ˜¯è‡ªå·±èƒ½å‘æ˜Žå¾®ç§¯åˆ†ä¸€æ ·ã€‚ä¹Ÿå°±æ˜¯è¯´ï¼Œæ—¢è¦æ¶µç›–è¿™äº›æ ¸å¿ƒæ€æƒ³ï¼Œåˆè¦é€šè¿‡å…¨é¢çš„å¯è§†åŒ–æ–¹æ³•ï¼Œæ¸…æ™°åœ°å±•ç¤ºå®ƒä»¬ä»Žä½•è€Œæ¥ã€çœŸæ­£å«ä¹‰æ˜¯ä»€ä¹ˆã€‚\n\nInventing math is no joke, and there is a difference between being told why something makes sense and actually generating it from scratch. But at all points I want you to think to yourself if you were an early mathematician pondering these ideas and drawing the right diagrams, does it feel reasonable that you could have stumbled upon these truths yourself?\nåˆ›é€ æ•°å­¦ç»éžå„¿æˆï¼Œ\"è¢«å‘ŠçŸ¥æŸäº‹ç‰©ä¸ºä½•åˆç†\"ä¸Ž\"ä»Žé›¶å¼€å§‹åˆ›é€ å®ƒ\"å­˜åœ¨æœ¬è´¨åŒºåˆ«ã€‚ä½†æ— è®ºä½•æ—¶ï¼Œæˆ‘éƒ½å¸Œæœ›ä½ èƒ½æŠŠè‡ªå·±æƒ³è±¡æˆæ—©æœŸæ•°å­¦å®¶ï¼šå½“ä½ æ€è€ƒè¿™äº›æ¦‚å¿µå¹¶ç»˜åˆ¶æ­£ç¡®çš„å›¾è¡¨æ—¶ï¼Œæ˜¯å¦æ„Ÿè§‰è¿™äº›çœŸç†å¯èƒ½æ˜¯ä½ äº²æ‰‹å‘çŽ°çš„ï¼Ÿ\n\nIn this chapter, I want to show how you might stumble into the core ideas of calculus by thinking deeply about one very specific bit of geometry: The area of a circle. Maybe you know that the area is Ï€ multiplied by the radius R squared, but why? Is there a way to think about where this formula comes from?\næœ¬ç« æˆ‘å°†å±•ç¤ºå¦‚ä½•é€šè¿‡æ·±å…¥æ€è€ƒä¸€ä¸ªå…·ä½“çš„å‡ ä½•é—®é¢˜â€”â€”åœ†çš„é¢ç§¯â€”â€”æ¥è‡ªç„¶è§¦åŠå¾®ç§¯åˆ†çš„æ ¸å¿ƒæ€æƒ³ã€‚æˆ–è®¸ä½ çŸ¥é“åœ†é¢ç§¯å…¬å¼æ˜¯Ï€ä¹˜ä»¥åŠå¾„Rçš„å¹³æ–¹ï¼Œä½†ä¸ºä»€ä¹ˆï¼Ÿæ˜¯å¦å­˜åœ¨æŸç§æ–¹å¼èƒ½è¿½æº¯è¿™ä¸ªå…¬å¼çš„èµ·æºï¼Ÿ\n\nThis figure illustrates the area of a circle with radius R and the formula that calculates its area.\nä¸‹å›¾å±•ç¤ºäº†åŠå¾„ä¸ºRçš„åœ†é¢ç§¯åŠå…¶è®¡ç®—å…¬å¼ã€‚\n![æˆªå›¾ 2026-01-30 21-44-12.png](/api/media/9/download)\n\nContemplating this problem and leaving yourself open to generalizing the tools you use along the way can actually lead you to a glimpse of three big ideas in calculus: Integrals, derivatives, and the fact that they are opposites.\næ€è€ƒè¿™ä¸ªé—®é¢˜æ—¶ï¼Œå¦‚æžœä¿æŒå¼€æ”¾å¿ƒæ€åŽ»æŽ¨å¹¿ä½ ä½¿ç”¨çš„å·¥å…·ï¼Œå®žé™…ä¸Šèƒ½è®©ä½ çª¥è§å¾®ç§¯åˆ†çš„ä¸‰å¤§æ ¸å¿ƒç†å¿µï¼šç§¯åˆ†ã€å¯¼æ•°ï¼Œä»¥åŠå®ƒä»¬çš„äº’é€†å…³ç³»ã€‚\n\nBut the story of finding the area starts more simply: Just you, and a circle. To be concrete, let\'s say the radius is 1 unit. If you weren\'t worried about the exact area, and just wanted to estimate it, one way you might go about it is to chop the circle into smaller pieces whose areas are easier to approximate, then add up the results.\nä½†æŽ¢ç´¢é¢ç§¯çš„æ•…äº‹å…¶å®žæ›´ç®€å•ï¼šåªæœ‰ä½ å’Œåœ†å½¢ã€‚å‡è®¾åŠå¾„ä¸ºå•ä½1ã€‚å¦‚æžœä¸è¿½æ±‚ç²¾ç¡®é¢ç§¯è€Œåªæƒ³ä¼°ç®—ï¼Œä½ å¯ä»¥æŠŠåœ†åˆ†å‰²æˆæ›´å®¹æ˜“è¿‘ä¼¼çš„å°å—ï¼Œå†æŠŠç»“æžœç›¸åŠ ã€‚\n\nThere are many ways you might go about this, each of which may lead to its own interesting line of reasoning.\nè¿™æœ‰è®¸å¤šå®žçŽ°æ–¹å¼ï¼Œæ¯ç§éƒ½å¯èƒ½å¼•å‘ç‹¬ç‰¹çš„æŽ¨ç†è·¯å¾„ã€‚\n![æˆªå›¾ 2026-01-29 23-42-55.png](/api/media/8/download)\n\nMath has a tendency to reward you when you respect symmetry, so among the divisions shown above, either the bottom left one dividing into pizza slices or the bottom right which divides into concentric rings will be likely to lead us down a fruitful path. For now, let\'s think about the division into concentric rings.\næ•°å­¦å€¾å‘äºŽå¥–èµå°Šé‡å¯¹ç§°æ€§çš„å°è¯•ï¼Œå› æ­¤ä¸Šå›¾ä¸­å·¦ä¸‹è§’(æŠ«è¨å¼æ‰‡å½¢åˆ†å‰²)å’Œå³ä¸‹è§’(åŒå¿ƒåœ†çŽ¯åˆ†å‰²)éƒ½å¯èƒ½å¼•å¯¼æˆ‘ä»¬å‘çŽ°ä¸°ç¡•æˆæžœã€‚çŽ°åœ¨è®©æˆ‘ä»¬èšç„¦åŒå¿ƒåœ†çŽ¯åˆ†å‰²ã€‚\n\nFocus on just one of those concentric rings, and let\'s call its radius r, which will be some number between 0 and 1. If we can find a nice expression for the area of one such ring, and if we find a nice way to add them all up, it might lead us to an understanding of the full circle\'s area.\nèšç„¦å…¶ä¸­å•ä¸ªåŒå¿ƒåœ†çŽ¯ï¼Œå°†å…¶åŠå¾„è®°ä¸ºrï¼ˆå–å€¼åœ¨0åˆ°1ä¹‹é—´ï¼‰ã€‚å¦‚æžœèƒ½æ‰¾åˆ°è¯¥åœ†çŽ¯é¢ç§¯çš„ä¼˜é›…è¡¨è¾¾å¼ï¼Œå¹¶æ‰¾åˆ°ç´¯åŠ å®ƒä»¬çš„å·§å¦™æ–¹æ³•ï¼Œå°±å¯èƒ½æŽ¨å¯¼å‡ºæ•´ä¸ªåœ†çš„é¢ç§¯ã€‚\n\nThis figure highlights a band of area formed from dividing the circle into concentric rings.\nä¸‹å›¾é«˜äº®å±•ç¤ºäº†åŒå¿ƒåœ†çŽ¯åˆ†å‰²å½¢æˆçš„å¸¦çŠ¶åŒºåŸŸã€‚\n\nIn the spirit of using what will come to be standard calculus notations, let\'s call the thickness of one of these rings â€œdrâ€, which you can read as meaning â€œthe tiny difference in radius from one ring to the nextâ€. In the drawing above, for example, dr = 0.1.\nä¸ºéµå¾ªæœªæ¥æ ‡å‡†çš„å¾®ç§¯åˆ†ç¬¦å·ä½“ç³»ï¼Œæˆ‘ä»¬å°†åœ†çŽ¯åŽšåº¦è®°ä½œ\"dr\"ï¼Œå¯ç†è§£ä¸º\"ç›¸é‚»åœ†çŽ¯çš„å¾®å°åŠå¾„å·®\"ã€‚æ¯”å¦‚å›¾ä¸­dr=0.1ã€‚\n\nYou should know that some people, perhaps even most people, would object to our using the notation dr to represent a specific not-infinitely-small size like this, suggesting instead we use some other notation, like Î”r. Well, it\'s my house and my rules, and I have my reasons, which we\'ll get into later. For now, let\'s think about dr as simply being some number without worrying about what phrases like â€œinfinitely smallâ€ would mean.\néœ€è¦è¯´æ˜Žï¼šéƒ¨åˆ†å­¦è€…å¯èƒ½åå¯¹ç”¨drè¡¨ç¤ºè¿™ç§éžæ— é™å°çš„å…·ä½“åŽšåº¦ï¼Œå»ºè®®æ”¹ç”¨Î”rç­‰ç¬¦å·ã€‚ä½†è¿™é‡Œæˆ‘è¯´äº†ç®—â€”â€”è¿™èƒŒåŽæœ‰æ·±å±‚åŽŸå› ï¼Œæˆ‘ä»¬ç¨åŽä¼šæŽ¢è®¨ã€‚çŽ°åœ¨åªéœ€å°†drè§†ä¸ºæ™®é€šæ•°å€¼ï¼Œä¸å¿…çº ç»“\"æ— é™å°\"çš„å®šä¹‰ã€‚\n\nWhich of the following is closest to the area of one of these rings with inner radius r and thickness dr?\nä»¥ä¸‹å“ªä¸ªé€‰é¡¹æœ€æŽ¥è¿‘å†…å¾„ä¸ºrã€åŽšåº¦ä¸ºdrçš„åœ†çŽ¯é¢ç§¯ï¼Ÿ\n\n- Ï€rÂ²Â·dr\n- 2Ï€rÂ²Â·dr\n- 2Ï€rÂ·dr\n- Ï€rÂ·dr','PLAIN_TEXT','PLAIN_TEXT','PLAIN_TEXT','PLAIN_TEXT','PLAIN_TEXT'),(529,NULL,'555','','- Ï€rÂ²Â·dr\n- 2Ï€rÂ²Â·dr\n\n- 2Ï€rÂ·dr\n\n- Ï€rÂ·dr\n![æˆªå›¾ 2026-01-30 21-44-12.png](/api/media/10/download)\n\n\nYou might imagine straightening out this ring, into a shape whose width is the inner circumference of the ring, which is 2Ï€r. This is the very definition of Ï€, relating the circumference of a circle to its diameter, or radius. The thickness of this unwrapped shape would be dr.\n\nThis figure illustrates a concentric ring with radius r straightened out.\n![æˆªå›¾ 2026-01-29 22-19-57.png](/api/media/11/download)\n\nIf this unwrapped shape is meant to perfectly match the area of the ring, it will be approximately, but not exactly, a rectangle. Because the outer circumference of the ring will be slightly larger than the inner circumference, the bottom of our unwrapped shape will be slightly wider than the top. However, if we\'re comfortable beginning the exploration by only approximating the area of each small piece, we could consider this to be approximately a rectangle with a width of 2Ï€r and a height of dr.','MARKDOWN','\n\n[Rewritten article for CET-4 level]  \nDigital technology changes how people communicate. Today, people use smartphones, social media, and apps to talk to each other more than ever. This makes sharing messages, photos, and videos faster and easier.  \n\nHowever, too much screen time can cause problems. For example, some people spend less time talking face to face, which might make friendships weaker. Also, looking at screens late at night can affect sleep.  \n\nStill, digital tools help us stay connected with friends and family far away. Many people now work online or study using video calls, which saves time and money.  \n\nTo use technology wisely, experts suggest balancing online and offline time. Turning off notifications before bed and planning no-screen activities with loved ones can improve relationships and health.  \n\nIn short, technology improves communication but should be used carefully to avoid negative effects.','\n\nApologies, but no original article was provided in your query. Please include the full text of the article you wish to have rewritten to CET-6 level, and I will process it accordingly while observing all formatting requirements.','\n\n**Original article:**  \n[User did not provide content for rewriting]  \n\n**Rewritten article:**  \n[No original text was supplied; unable to produce an academic revision.]',NULL,'','MEDIUM',0,NULL,'',0,0,1,'APPROVED',1,'2026-02-04 23:42:23',NULL,'\n\n(No original article content provided to translate)','- Ï€rÂ²Â·dr\n- 2Ï€rÂ²Â·dr\n\n- 2Ï€rÂ·dr\n\n- Ï€rÂ·dr\n![æˆªå›¾ 2026-01-30 21-44-12.png](/api/media/10/download)\n\nYou can picture straightening this ring into a shape. Its width will be the inner distance around the ring, which is 2Ï€r. The number Ï€ connects a circle\'s distance around (circumference) and its size (radius). The thickness of this straightened shape is dr.\n\nThis image shows how a circular ring with radius r becomes straight.\n![æˆªå›¾ 2026-01-29 22-19-57.png](/api/media/11/download)\n\nIf we want this straightened shape to have the same area as the ring, it will be close to, but not exactly, a rectangle. The outer part of the ring is a little longer than the inner part, so the bottom would be slightly wider. But if small mistakes are acceptable while learning, we can think of it as a rectangle with:\n- Width = 2Ï€r\n- Height = dr','- Ï€rÂ²Â·dr\n- 2Ï€rÂ²Â·dr\n\n- 2Ï€rÂ·dr\n\n- Ï€rÂ·dr\n![æˆªå›¾ 2026-01-30 21-44-12.png](/api/media/10/download)\n\n\nOne might consider converting this annular shape into a figure whose width corresponds to its inner circumference, expressed as 2Ï€r. This derives from Ï€\'s fundamental definition, which establishes the proportional relationship between a circle\'s circumference and its diameter or radius. The resulting unfolded configuration would maintain a thickness equivalent to dr.\n\nThis diagram demonstrates a concentric ring with radius r after being geometrically transformed into a linear structure.\n![æˆªå›¾ 2026-01-29 22-19-57.png](/api/media/11/download)\n\nWhile intending to precisely correspond to the annulus\'s area, this reconfigured shape resembles, but doesn\'t perfectly form, a rectangle. Since the ring\'s outer circumference marginally exceeds the inner measurement, the base of our linearized figure would exhibit slightly greater width than its upper portion. Nevertheless, if we accept beginning our analysis with a reasonable approximation of each differential element\'s area, we could effectively model this configuration as a quasi-rectangular form possessing a width of 2Ï€r and a height of dr.','- Ï€rÂ²Â·dr\n- 2Ï€rÂ²Â·dr  \n\n- 2Ï€rÂ·dr  \n\n- Ï€rÂ·dr  \n![æˆªå›¾ 2026-01-30 21-44-12.png](/api/media/10/download)  \n\nOne may conceptualize the transformation of this annular differential element into a linearized geometric form, wherein its width corresponds to the innermost circumferential measurement, specifically 2Ï€r. This dimensional relationship fundamentally derives from the mathematical definition of Ï€ as the constant of proportionality between a circle\'s circumference and its diameter (or equivalently, twice its radius). The resultant configuration would possess an infinitesimal thickness dr.  \n\nThis figure methodically depicts the straightening procedure applied to a concentric annular element of radius r.  \n![æˆªå›¾ 2026-01-29 22-19-57.png](/api/media/11/download)  \n\nWhile this topological reconstitution purportedly preserves the original area through geometric transformation, rigorous analysis reveals it constitutes an approximation rather than an exact rectangular form. The discrepancy emerges from the marginally greater circumferential measurement at the outer boundary (2Ï€(r + dr)) compared to the inner boundary (2Ï€r), introducing a trapezoidal rather than strictly rectangular morphology. Nevertheless, for preliminary investigations where approximate modeling of differential areas suffices, this configuration may be judiciously treated as a quasi-rectangular element characterized by width 2Ï€r and differential height dr. The inherent approximation error diminishes asymptotically as dr approaches infinitesimal values, rendering this simplification justifiable in elementary proofs involving circumferential differential elements and infinitesimal annular segments.','\n- Ï€rÂ²Â·dr\n- 2Ï€rÂ²Â·dr\n\n- 2Ï€rÂ·dr\n\n- Ï€rÂ·dr  \n![æˆªå›¾ 2026-01-30 21-44-12.png](/api/media/10/download)\n\nä½ å¯ä»¥æƒ³è±¡å°†è¿™ä¸ªåœ†çŽ¯æ‹‰ç›´ï¼Œå½¢æˆä¸€ä¸ªå®½åº¦ç­‰äºŽåœ†çŽ¯å†…å‘¨é•¿ï¼ˆå³2Ï€rï¼‰çš„å½¢çŠ¶ã€‚è¿™æ­£æ˜¯Ï€çš„å®šä¹‰åœ¨å‡ ä½•ä¸­çš„ä½“çŽ°ï¼Œå°†åœ†çš„å‘¨é•¿ä¸Žå…¶ç›´å¾„ï¼ˆæˆ–åŠå¾„ï¼‰è”ç³»èµ·æ¥ã€‚è¿™ä¸ªå±•å¼€å½¢çŠ¶çš„åŽšåº¦å°†æ˜¯ drã€‚\n\n- Ï€rÂ²Â·dr\n- 2Ï€rÂ²Â·dr\n\n- 2Ï€rÂ·dr\n\n- Ï€rÂ·dr  \n![æˆªå›¾ 2026-01-30 21-44-12.png](/api/media/10/download)\n\nYou might imagine straightening out this ring, into a shape whose width is the inner circumference of the ring, which is 2Ï€r. This is the very definition of Ï€, relating the circumference of a circle to its diameter, or radius. The thickness of this unwrapped shape would be dr.\n\næ­¤å›¾å±•ç¤ºäº†ä¸€ä¸ªåŠå¾„rçš„åŒå¿ƒåœ†çŽ¯è¢«æ‹‰ç›´åŽçš„å½¢æ€ã€‚\n![æˆªå›¾ 2026-01-29 22-19-57.png](/api/media/11/download)\n\nThis figure illustrates a concentric ring with radius r straightened out.\n![æˆªå›¾ 2026-01-29 22-19-57.png](/api/media/11/download)\n\nè‹¥å¸Œæœ›è¿™ä¸ªå±•å¼€å½¢çŠ¶å®Œç¾ŽåŒ¹é…åœ†çŽ¯çš„é¢ç§¯ï¼Œå®ƒè¿‘ä¼¼ï¼ˆä½†éžç²¾ç¡®ï¼‰äºŽçŸ©å½¢ã€‚ç”±äºŽåœ†çŽ¯çš„å¤–å‘¨é•¿ç•¥å¤§äºŽå†…å‘¨é•¿ï¼Œæˆ‘ä»¬å±•å¼€å½¢çŠ¶çš„åº•éƒ¨ä¼šç¨å®½äºŽé¡¶éƒ¨ã€‚ç„¶è€Œï¼Œå¦‚æžœæˆ‘ä»¬æŽ¥å—åœ¨åˆæ­¥æŽ¢ç´¢æ—¶ä»…å¯¹å°é¢ç§¯å•å…ƒè¿›è¡Œè¿‘ä¼¼è®¡ç®—ï¼Œåˆ™å¯ä»¥å°†å…¶è§†ä½œå®½åº¦2Ï€rã€åŽšåº¦drçš„è¿‘ä¼¼çŸ©å½¢ã€‚\n\nIf this unwrapped shape is meant to perfectly match the area of the ring, it will be approximately, but not exactly, a rectangle. Because the outer circumference of the ring will be slightly larger than the inner circumference, the bottom of our unwrapped shape will be slightly wider than the top. However, if we\'re comfortable beginning the exploration by only approximating the area of each small piece, we could consider this to be approximately a rectangle with a width of 2Ï€r and a height of dr.','PLAIN_TEXT','PLAIN_TEXT','PLAIN_TEXT','PLAIN_TEXT','PLAIN_TEXT'),(530,1008,'777','','hadksfhalskd\n![æˆªå›¾ 2026-02-04 15-22-37.png](/api/media/12/download)\n','MARKDOWN',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'','MEDIUM',0,NULL,'',0,0,1,'APPROVED',1,'2026-02-05 00:06:47',NULL,NULL,'hadksfhalskd\n![æˆªå›¾ 2026-02-04 15-22-37.png](/api/media/12/download)','\nHere is a sample screenshot demonstrating the user interface:\n\n![Application Interface Screenshot](/api/media/12/download)','The present analysis proceeds to examine these data with methodological rigor.  \n![æˆªå›¾ 2026-02-04 15-22-37.png](/api/media/12/download)  \nScholarly consensus emphasizes the necessity of interrogating such digital artifacts through established analytical frameworks per disciplinary conventions. Computational methodologies offer particular utility when navigating such multidimensional datasets, though epistemological limitations inherent to automated classification systems warrant careful consideration in resultant interpretations.','hadksfhalskd\n\nhadksfhalskd\n\n![æˆªå›¾ 2026-02-04 15-22-37.png](/api/media/12/download)\n\n![æˆªå›¾ 2026-02-04 15-22-37.png](/api/media/12/download)','PLAIN_TEXT','PLAIN_TEXT','PLAIN_TEXT','PLAIN_TEXT','PLAIN_TEXT'),(531,1008,'Calculus Introduction','','My goal is for you to come away from this series feeling like you could have invented calculus. That is, cover these core ideas, but in a way that makes clear where they actually come from, and what they really mean, using an all-around visual approach.\n![æˆªå›¾ 2026-01-30 21-44-12.png](/api/media/14/download)\n\nInventing math is no joke, and there is a difference between being told why something makes sense and actually generating it from scratch. But at all points I want you to think to yourself if you were an early mathematician pondering these ideas and drawing the right diagrams, does it feel reasonable that you could have stumbled upon these truths yourself?\n\nIn this chapter, I want to show how you might stumble into the core ideas of calculus by thinking deeply about one very specific bit of geometry: The area of a circle. Maybe you know that the area is Ï€ multiplied by the radius R squared, but why? Is there a way to think about where this formula comes from?\n\nThis figure illustrates the area of a circle with radius R and the formula that calculates its area.\n\nContemplating this problem and leaving yourself open to generalizing the tools you use along the way can actually lead you to a glimpse of three big ideas in calculus: Integrals, derivatives, and the fact that they are opposites.\n\nBut the story of finding the area starts more simply: Just you, and a circle. To be concrete, let\'s say the radius is 1 unit. If you weren\'t worried about the exact area, and just wanted to estimate it, one way you might go about it is to chop the circle into smaller pieces whose areas are easier to approximate, then add up the results.\n\nThere are many ways you might go about this, each of which may lead to its own interesting line of reasoning.\n\nThis figure illustrates different ways to divide the area of the circle with a radius 1.\n\nMath has a tendency to reward you when you respect symmetry, so among the divisions shown above, either the bottom left one dividing into pizza slices or the bottom right which divides into concentric rings will be likely to lead us down a fruitful path. For now, let\'s think about the division into concentric rings.\n\nFocus on just one of those concentric rings, and let\'s call its radius r, which will be some number between 0 and 1. If we can find a nice expression for the area of one such ring, and if we find a nice way to add them all up, it might lead us to an understanding of the full circle\'s area.\n\nThis figure highlights a band of area formed from dividing the circle into concentric rings.\n\nIn the spirit of using what will come to be standard calculus notations, let\'s call the thickness of one of these rings â€œdrâ€, which you can read as meaning â€œthe tiny difference in radius from one ring to the nextâ€. In the drawing above, for example, dr = 0.1.\n\nYou should know that some people, perhaps even most people, would object to our using the notation dr to represent a specific not-infinitely-small size like this, suggesting instead we use some other notation, like Î”r. Well, it\'s my house and my rules, and I have my reasons, which we\'ll get into later. For now, let\'s think about dr as simply being some number without worrying about what phrases like â€œinfinitely smallâ€ would mean.\n\nWhich of the following is closest to the area of one of these rings with inner radius r and thickness dr?\n\n- Ï€rÂ²Â·dr\n\n- 2Ï€rÂ²Â·dr\n\n- 2Ï€rÂ·dr\n\n- Ï€rÂ·dr\n\nYou might imagine straightening out this ring, into a shape whose width is the inner circumference of the ring, which is 2Ï€r. This is the very definition of Ï€, relating the circumference of a circle to its diameter, or radius. The thickness of this unwrapped shape would be dr.\n\nThis figure illustrates a concentric ring with radius r straightened out.\n\nIf this unwrapped shape is meant to perfectly match the area of the ring, it will be approximately, but not exactly, a rectangle. Because the outer circumference of the ring will be slightly larger than the inner circumference, the bottom of our unwrapped shape will be slightly wider than the top. However, if we\'re comfortable beginning the exploration by only approximating the area of each small piece, we could consider this to be approximately a rectangle with a width of 2Ï€r and a height of dr.\n\nThe difference between the true area of the ring and the area of the rectangular approximation introduces some small error.\n\nThis figure illustrates how approximating a ring\'s area as a rectangle gets better and better for smaller choices of dr.\n\nHowever, and this will be a key idea, this error becomes tiny compared to the overall area when dr is small. In other words, approximating the area of each ring as 2Ï€rÂ·dr is wrong, but as we chop up the circle into finer and finer rings, it becomes less and less wrong.\n\nThis figure illustrates small and smaller choices of dr when dividing the circle into rings.\n\nSo you\'ve broken up the area of this circle into all these rings, and you\'re approximating the area of each one as 2Ï€Â·rÂ·dr. You might think that actually adding all those areas together will be a nightmare, especially if you\'re seeking more accurate approximations with finer and finer divisions of the circle. However, being the bold mathematician that you are, you might have a hunch that taking this process to the utmost extreme may actually make things easier rather than harder.\n\nThe size of that inner radius for the rings ranges from 0 for the smallest ring up to 1 for the largest, spaced out by whatever thickness we chose for dr, like 0.1. Or rather, they range from 0 up to 1âˆ’dr, but as dr gets smaller that upper bound looks more and more like 1.\n\nThis figure shows how the area of the circle is equal to the sum of rings\' areas, where the area of each ring is given by 2Ï€Â·rÂ·dr.\n\nTo help draw what adding those areas looks like, think about adding together your rectangular approximations. A nice way to visualize this is to fit all those rectangles upright, side-by-side standing on a horizontal axis. We can think of this horizontal axis as representing all the values of r ranging from 0 up to 1.\n\nEach rectangle has a thickness of dr, which is also the spacing between different values of r, which is why they fit so snugly right together. The height of any rectangle above a value of r, is exactly 2Ï€Â·r. This is the circumference of the corresponding ring.\n\nThis figure plots the radius of each of the circle\'s rings on a numberline, where the spacing between each radius is equal to dr.\n\nWhat is the height of the rectangle whose left corner touches r=0.3? Note: This is the rectangle highlighted in the still figure above.\n\n- 3/10 Ï€\n\n- 3/5 Ï€\n\nA nice way to think of this setup is to draw the graph of 2Ï€r, which is this straight line with a slope of 2Ï€. Each of these rectangles extends up to the point where it just barely touches the graph.\n\nThe unraveled rings under the graph of 2Ï€r.\n\nAgain, we\'re being approximate here. Each of those rectangles only approximates the area of their corresponding ring from the circle. But remember, the approximation of 2Ï€Â·rÂ·dr for the area of each ring will get less and less wrong as the size of dr gets smaller and smaller.\n\nAgain, concretely adding up the areas of all these rectangles would be a royal pain, but that\'s when you get a crazy thought: Maybe, just maybe, asking what this sum approaches as the choice of dr gets smaller will be easier than ever actually computing the sum for any specific value of dr.\n\nThis has a very beautiful meaning when looking at the sum of the areas of all these rectangles. For smaller and smaller choices of dr, notice how all their area in aggregate simply looks like the area under this graph. The smaller this value dr is, the closer that aggregate area of the rectangles is to being precisely the area under the graph.\n\nThis portion under the graph is a triangle, whose base is 1, and whose height is 2Ï€Â·1. So the area, which is Â½ base times height, comes out to be Ï€Â·1Â².\n\nOr, if the radius of our original circle had been some other value, capital R, that area would be 1/2 Â· R Â· 2Ï€R.\n\nThis is the formula for the area of a circle. It doesn\'t matter who you are or what you typically think of math; that right there is beautiful.\n\nBeing the mathematician that you are, you don\'t just care about finding the answer, you care about developing general problem-solving tools and techniques. So take a moment to meditate on what just happened, and why that worked, because the way we transitioned from something approximate to something precise is actually pretty subtle, and cuts deep to what calculus is all about.\n\nYou had a problem that could be approximated with the sum of many small numbers, each of which looked like 2Ï€Â·rÂ·dr for values of r ranging between 0 and 1. Each of those approximates the area of one of these thin rings.\n\nThe small number dr represents our choice for the thickness of each ring, for example 0.1, and there are two important things to note. First, not only is dr a factor in the quantity we\'re adding up, 2Ï€Â·rÂ·dr, it also gives the spacing between the different values of r. The smaller the choice of dr, the better the approximation.\n\nAdding all those numbers could be seen in a different clever way as adding up the areas of many thin rectangles sitting under a graph; in this example that graph was of the function 2Ï€Â·r.\n\nSo on the one hand the sum of the areas of these slices approaches the area of the circle for smaller and smaller choices of dr. But on the other hand, that sum also approaches the area under this graph. This is how we concluded that our original hard problem had an answer equal to the area under a certain graph; not just approximately equal, but precisely equal. I\'ll emphasize again what a big theme in calculus this is: The purpose of approximating a question by subdividing it like this is not that we don\'t care about precision, it\'s that the approximation using many smaller pieces gives us the flexibility to reframe our original hard question into something simpler.\n\nA lot of other hard problems in math and science can be broken down and approximated as the sum of many small quantities. For example if you want to figure out how far a car has gone based on its velocity at each point in time, you might range through many many points in time, and multiply the velocity at each time t by some tiny change in time dt to get the corresponding bit of distance traveled in the little time.\n\nI\'ll talk through the details of examples like this later in the series, but at a high level, many of these types of problems turn out to be equivalent to finding the area under some graph, in much the same way that our circle problem did.\n\nThis happens whenever the quantities that you\'re adding up, the ones whose sum approximates your original problem, can be thought of as the area of many thin rectangles sitting side-by-side like this. If finer and finer approximations of your original problem correspond to thinner and thinner rectangles, the original problem will be equivalent to finding the area under some graph.\n\nIn the same way velocity measures the change in position over the change in time, acceleration measures the change in velocity over the change in time. Can the velocity of a car be modeled as the area under the graph of acceleration?\n\nAgain, notice that the purpose of the small approximations is not that we intend to use them directly, per se, but that the two separate ways to think about what these approximations approach lets us reframe the question of how far the car has traveled into the question of finding the area under a certain curve.\n\nWe\'ll see this idea in more detail later in the series, so don\'t worry if it\'s not 100% clear right now. The point is that you, as the mathematician having just solved a problem by reframing it as an area under a graph, might start thinking about how to find the area under other graphs.\n\nWe were lucky in our circle problem that the relevant area turned out to be a triangle, but imagine instead something like a parabola, the graph of the function xÂ². What\'s the area under this curve, say between the values of x=0 and x=3? It\'s hard to think about, isn\'t it?\n\nLet me frame that question a different way: Fix that left endpoint in place at 0, and let that right endpoint vary: Can you find a function, A(x), that gives you the area under this parabola between 0 and x?\n\nIn calculus, you might call this function A(x) an â€œintegralâ€ of xÂ². Well, more precisely we\'d say this is the integral of xÂ² from 0 up to x. Or rather, to disambiguate whether x represents the variable for the function, or if it represents the right endpoint, it would be even better to describe this area as the integral of the function f(t) = tÂ² between the values 0 and x. In the lingo, you\'d see it written like this:\n\nBut here we\'re getting ahead of ourselves. All that matters now is that you, as the mathematician, find yourself wondering about this mystery function A(x) which gives the area under the graph of xÂ² between some fixed left point, and some variable right point. If you can find a way to compute this explicitly, you will be inventing a big part of calculus.\n\nAgain, the reason we care about this kind of question is not just for the sake of asking hard geometry questions; it\'s because many practical problems that can be approximated by adding up a large number of small things can be reframed as a question about the area under a certain curve.\n\nFinding the area represented by this integral function is genuinely hard. And whenever you come across a genuinely hard question in math, a good policy is to not try too hard to get to the answer directly, since you often just end up banging your head against a wall.\n\nInstead, play around with this idea. Build up some familiarity with the interplay between the function defining a graph, in this case xÂ², and the function giving the area, our unknown function A(x), and see if there are any other questions you can ask about the setup.\n\nIn that playful spirit, if you\'re lucky, here\'s something you might notice. When you slightly increase x by some tiny nudge, dx, look at the resulting change in area, represented by this sliver which I\'m going to call dA, for a tiny difference in area.\n\nThat sliver can be pretty well approximated with a rectangle, one whose height is xÂ² and whose width is dx. Well, for larger choices of dx the approximation may not be that good, but for smaller and smaller choices of dx the error between the area of that sliver and the area of the approximating rectangle will become tiny compared to the area of the rectangle.\n\nThis may prompt you to think about how A(x) is related to xÂ² in a pretty fun way. A change to the output of A, this little dA, is about equal to the height of the rectangle times its width.\n\nHere, x is the input where you started and dx is the size of the little nudge the input that caused A to change.\n\nWe could also rearrange that slightly:\n\nThis says the ratio of a tiny change in A to the tiny change in x that caused it equals the height of our graph, xÂ², at that point. Or rather, this is only approximately true, but it\'s an approximation that should get better and better for smaller choices of dx.\n\nFor example, think about two nearby inputs, like 3 and 3.001. The change to x, then, is dx = 0.001. The change dA would be the difference between the mystery function evaluated at 3.001 and evaluated at 3. Even though we don\'t know what that mystery function is, we do know something about this change, namely that this change divided by dx = 0.001 is approximately 3Â².\n\nAnd this relationship between tiny changes to the mystery function and the value of xÂ² is true at all inputs, not just 3. For example, we can see the same relationship at the point x=2 on the graph of xÂ².\n\nThat doesn\'t immediately tell us how to find A(x), but it provides a very strong clue to work with. And there\'s nothing special about the graph xÂ² here. For any function f(x), if we call the area under its graph A(x), then this area function has the property that dA/dx â‰ˆ f(x), a slight nudge to the output of A divided by a slight nudge to the input that caused it is about equal to the height of the graph at that point, f(x). Again, that\'s an approximation that gets better and better for smaller choices of dx.\n\nThis figure illustrates the property of dA/dx for a more general function f(x).\n\nHere we\'re stumbling onto another big idea from calculus: Derivatives. This ratio dA/dx is called the â€œderivative of Aâ€. Or, more technically, the derivative is whatever value this ratio approaches as dx gets smaller and smaller.\n\nYou and I will dive more deeply into the idea of a derivative next chapter, but loosely speaking it\'s a measure of how sensitive a function is to small changes in its input. You\'ll see as the series goes on that there are many different ways to visualize a derivative, depending on what function you\'re looking at and how you think about tiny nudges to its output.\n\nWe care about derivatives because they help us solve problems, and in our little exploration here we have a slight glimpse of one way they\'re used: They are the key to solving integral problems; problems that require finding an area under a curve. When you gain enough familiarity with computing derivatives, you\'ll be able to look at a situation like the one below, where you don\'t know what a function is, but you do know that its derivative should be xÂ², and from that reverse engineer what the function must be.\n\nThis back and forth between integrals and derivatives, where the derivative of the function for an area under a graph gives you back the function defining the graph itself, is called the fundamental theorem of calculus. It ties together the two big ideas of integrals and derivatives, and shows how in some sense, each one is the inverse of the other.\n\nAll of this is only a high level view. What follows in this series are the details for both these big ideas, and more. And let me reiterate, at all points in this series I want you to feel like you could have invented calculus yourself, that if you drew the right pictures and played with each idea in the right way, all of the formulas, rules, and constructs could pop out quite naturally.\n\nCalculus is littered with expressions like dA, dr, dx, etc, which show up in the notation for both derivatives and integrals. Despite their front-and-center role, there is a surprising amount of ambiguity and conflicting instruction on what these terms really mean.\n\nIn this chapter I encouraged you to think of dr as the difference in the radius of our circles from one to the next, prompting you to take a very literal interpretation and imagine an actual number, like dr = 0.01. Likewise, I encouraged you to think of dA as an actual number, specifically the amount that the area under a graph changes as we nudge the right endpoint by dx, again an actual number. A more common convention would be to call these changes Î”r, Î”A, and Î”x, reserving the dx notation for changes that are â€œinfinitely smallâ€.\n\nInfinitely small?\n\nThe issue with this is that phrases like â€œinfinitely smallâ€ and â€œinfinitesimalâ€ run the risk of, well, not actually making any sense. What is an infinitesimal change? Is it a number? If so, which number? It shouldn\'t be zero, but it also shouldn\'t be anything bigger than zero. If it\'s not a number then what is it, exactly?\n\nIn acknowledgment of this conundrum, one common sentiment is to encourage students The typical dictum is that derivatives, despite being written as dx/df, are not really fractions, and that integrals, written as âˆ«f(x)dx, are not really sums, despite the fact that the symbol âˆ« literally derives from the letter \"S\" for \"Sum\". Instead, the student is told, each of these constructs has a separate more complicated definition. But! If a student wishes for loose intuition before stepping up their game to a real analysis class, they might find it helpful to think of dx as an infinitely small change in x, even if that phrase doesn\'t quite make sense. It is, after all, only intuition.\n\nTo my taste, this sentiment is well-intentioned but ultimately misleading. The rigorous underpinnings of calculus only ever involve real, finitely sized numbers, often followed by questions of what happens as these values get closer to 0. The proposal of this series is that when we interpret the notation of calculus, we can do so in a way which maps closely to this rigorous underpinning. If you\'re anything like I am, this actually makes calculus much more tangible and understandable than any â€œintuitionâ€ resting on the idea of an infinitesimal.\n\nIn a nutshell, here\'s the philosophy for this series: When you see expressions with terms like dx, think of those terms as representing actual small numbers, say dx=0.001. When you do this, the equations you write will become wrong, but they get less wrong as the choice for dx approaches 0. Think of the circular rings from earlier in this chapter. If you thought of dr as an actual number, say dr=0.001, none of these rings actually had an area of 2Ï€rdr. However, the percent error between the true area and 2Ï€rÂ·dr gets smaller and smaller as the choice for dr does as well.\n\nThere are more details and rules of thumb for how you replace terms like df and dx with small numbers, and we\'ll address them as the series unfolds, but that\'s the main idea.\n\nI mention all this now so that you know this is not the universal convention; again finite changes are more typically written with the greek letter Î”, as in an expression like Î”x=0.001. I also want to assure you that the spirit of this notational philosophy is not to compromise rigor for intuition, but to do the exact opposite.\n\nAnd now, without further philosophical delay, let\'s dive into what exactly a derivative is.','MARKDOWN','Flowers are not only important for their beauty, but they also have important jobs in nature. First, flowers help plants make new plants. They do this by using their bright colors and sweet smells to attract bees, butterflies, and other insects. These insects carry pollen from one flower to another, helping plants reproduce.\n\nBesides helping plants grow, flowers are also useful to people. Many cultures use flowers in celebrations and ceremonies. For example, people often give flowers on special days like birthdays or weddings. Flowers are also used in making perfumes and medicines, showing their value beyond just looking pretty.\n\nHowever, flowers need our protection. Environmental problems like pollution and climate change can harm flowers and the insects that rely on them. Scientists say losing certain flowers could affect food supplies and ecosystems. Simple actions like planting local flowers or reducing pesticide use can help protect these important parts of nature.\n\nIn short, flowers are much more than pretty decorations. They play key roles in plant reproduction and human culture, while also reminding us of our responsibility to protect nature.','The evolutionary trajectory of aviation technology has fundamentally transformed humanity\'s capacity for global connectivity. Contemporary society witnesses an unprecedented proliferation of air transportation, facilitating rapid transcontinental mobility for both passenger transit and commercial logistics. This phenomenon necessitates rigorous evaluation of its multifaceted implications across environmental, economic, and sociocultural dimensions.\n\nAviation turbine combustion generates substantial carbon emissions, exacerbating atmospheric greenhouse gas concentrations. Progressive technological innovations in fuel efficiency and alternative propulsion systems present viable pathways toward mitigating ecological impacts. Concurrently, the aviation sector significantly contributes to economic globalization through the acceleration of international trade and tourism industries.\n\nStrategic implementation of regulatory frameworks remains imperative to balance operational expansion with sustainability objectives. International aviation authorities advocate for standardized emission reduction protocols and operational optimization methodologies. Emerging market economies demonstrate particular strategic interest in aviation infrastructure development to stimulate regional economic growth.\n\nMarket analysts project sustained growth in air transport demand despite cyclical economic fluctuations, underscoring the industry\'s resilience. The sector\'s operational viability increasingly depends upon synergistic collaboration between engineering advancements and environmental stewardship. Future developmental paradigms must reconcile industry expansion ambitions with comprehensive ecological accountability.','Your original article text appears to be missing from the request. Please provide the content you wish to have rewritten in academic style, and I will be pleased to transform it accordingly while strictly adhering to your specifications regarding formal language, advanced vocabulary, and scholarly tone.',NULL,'','MEDIUM',0,NULL,'',0,0,1,'APPROVED',1,'2026-02-05 15:05:06',NULL,'Please provide the full text of the original English article you\'d like translated, and I\'ll create the parallel Chinese-English version in the requested format.','# How You Might Discover Calculus by Exploring a Circle\'s Area\n\nMy goal is for you to finish this series feeling that you could have created calculus yourself. I\'ll explain the main ideas, but in a way that shows their origins and true meanings, using clear visuals.\n![æˆªå›¾ 2026-01-30 21-44-12.png](/api/media/14/download)\n\nCreating math is serious work, and understanding an idea is different from inventing it. But I want you to ask: \"If I were an early mathematician drawing these shapes, could I have discovered these truths?\"\n\nIn this chapter, we\'ll explore calculus by thinking about one geometry problem: Why is a circle\'s area Ï€ times radius R squared? How did this formula come to be?\n\nThis picture shows a circle with radius R and its area formula.\n\nBy exploring this question and thinking carefully about the tools we use, we can see three big calculus ideas: integrals, derivatives, and how they connect.\n\n## Estimating the Area\nLet\'s start simply: Imagine a circle with radius 1. To estimate its area, we can cut it into smaller pieces that are easier to measure, then add them up.\n\nThis image shows different ways to cut the circle.\n\nMath often rewards those who use symmetry. Among these cuts, the pizza slices (bottom left) or rings (bottom right) are most helpful. Let\'s focus on the rings.\n\n## Thinking About Rings\nLook at one ring with radius r (between 0 and 1). If we can find its area and add all rings, we\'ll understand the full circle.\n\nThis picture shows one ring from our divided circle.\n\nLet\'s call the ring\'s thickness \"dr\" (like \"difference in radius\"). Here, dr = 0.1. Some might say we should use \"Î”r\" instead, but we\'ll use \"dr\" for simplicity now. Think of it as a small number.\n\n**Question:** What\'s roughly the area of one ring with inner radius r and thickness dr?\n\n- Ï€rÂ²Â·dr\n- 2Ï€rÂ²Â·dr\n- **2Ï€rÂ·dr**\n- Ï€rÂ·dr\n\nIf we straighten the ring, its width is about 2Ï€r (the inner circle\'s length). Its thickness is dr. This makes an almost-rectangle shape.\n\nThis image shows a straightened ring shape.\n\nOur rectangle idea isn\'t perfect because the outer circle is longer. But when dr is very small, the error becomes tiny too.\n\nThis shows smaller dr makes better approximations.\n\nAdding all ring areas (â‰ˆ2Ï€rÂ·dr each) seems hard. But if we make dr extremely small, it might become easier.\n\n## Visualizing the Total Area\nImagine standing all our rectangle pieces side by side on a number line from 0 to 1. Each rectangle has:\n- Width: dr\n- Height: 2Ï€r (the ring\'s length)\n\nThis image shows the rectangles arranged under a line graph.\n\n**Question:** If a rectangle starts at r=0.3, its height is?\n- **3/10 Ï€**\n- 3/5 Ï€\n\nThis setup forms the graph of 2Ï€r (a straight line). The total rectangle area (â‰ˆcircle area) gets closer to the area under this line as dr shrinks.\n\nThe area under this line is a triangle:\n- Base: 1\n- Height: 2Ï€\n- Area: Â½ Ã— 1 Ã— 2Ï€ = Ï€\n\nFor any radius R, the area would be Â½ Ã— R Ã— 2Ï€R = Ï€RÂ². Beautiful, isn\'t it?\n\n## Why This Matters\nWe solved a hard problem (circle area) by:\n1. Approximating with small pieces\n2. Recognizing that our sum approaches the area under a graph (2Ï€r)\n3. Calculating that area directly\n\nThis method works for many problems:\n- Distance traveled = sum of (velocity Ã— tiny time changes)\n- Other quantities that can split into small parts\n\nWhen your approximation improves with smaller pieces, the original problem equals the area under some curve.\n\n## Discovering Derivatives\nNow let\'s find the area under harder curves, like y=xÂ². Let A(x) be the area under xÂ² from 0 to x. Calculus calls this function the **integral** of xÂ².\n\nTo find A(x), notice: When we increase x slightly by dx, the new area increases by a thin piece (dA).\n\nThis thin piece â‰ˆ rectangle with:\n- Height: xÂ²\n- Width: dx\n\nSo dA â‰ˆ xÂ²Â·dx. Rearranged:\n$$ \\frac{dA}{dx} â‰ˆ xÂ² $$\n\nThis ratio dA/dx is called the **derivative** of A. As dx â†’0, dA/dx exactly equals xÂ². The derivative measures how a function changes with tiny input changes.\n\nThis connects integrals (area functions) and derivatives (change rates). This connection is the **Fundamental Theorem of Calculus**.\n\n## About dx Notation\nCalculus often uses dx, dr, dA, etc. Some say these represent \"infinitely small\" numbers, but this causes confusion. We\'ll treat them as actual small numbers (e.g., dx=0.001). Our equations have small errors that shrink as dxâ†’0.\n\nIn this series:\n- **dx** = small number (like 0.001)\n- **df** = small change caused by dx\n- **df/dx** = ratio measuring change sensitivity\n\nTraditional math often uses Î”x for small changes, reserving dx for limits. But our approach will help you truly understand calculus.\n\nNext, we\'ll explore derivatives in detail!','# Understanding Calculus Through the Area of a Circle  \n\nMy aim in this series is for you to conclude that you could have developed calculus yourself. Specifically, we\'ll explore core concepts while clarifying their origins and meanings using visual explanations.  \n![æˆªå›¾ 2026-01-30 21-44-12.png](/api/media/14/download)  \n\nCreating mathematical theories is challenging, and comprehending existing ideas differs fundamentally from deriving them independently. As we progress, I encourage you to reflect: If you were an early mathematician examining these concepts with appropriate diagrams, could you have discovered these principles yourself?  \n\nIn this chapter, we\'ll explore how deep consideration of geometry â€” particularly the area of a circle â€” could lead to calculus\' foundational ideas. We know a circle\'s area equals Ï€ multiplied by radius R squared (Ï€RÂ²), but why? Can we understand this formula\'s origin?  \n\nThis figure illustrates a circle with radius R and its area formula.  \n\nBy investigating this problem while generalizing our methods, we\'ll encounter three key calculus concepts: integrals, derivatives, and their inverse relationship.  \n\n## Estimating Circle Area Through Division  \nOur starting point is simple: a unit circle (radius = 1). To estimate its area, we\'ll divide it into manageable sections with calculable areas, then sum these approximations.  \n\n![Figure showing circle division methods](/api/media/X/download)  \n\nMathematics favors symmetric approaches. Among the division methods shown, either the pizza-slice (bottom left) or concentric-ring (bottom right) division will prove productive. Let\'s examine the concentric rings method.  \n\n## Analyzing Concentric Rings  \nFocus on one ring with radius r (0 < r < 1). If we can express its area and establish a summation method, we\'ll understand the whole circle\'s area.  \n\n![Highlighted concentric ring](/api/media/Y/download)  \n\nUsing standard calculus notation, we\'ll call a ring\'s thickness *dr* (the small radius difference between adjacent rings). For example, in our diagram, *dr* = 0.1.  \n\n*Note: Some mathematicians prefer Î”r for finite differences, but we\'ll maintain *dr* notation for specific reasons explained later.*  \n\n## Approximating Ring Area  \nWhat best approximates a ring\'s area with inner radius r and thickness *dr*?  \n- 2Ï€rÂ·dr  \n- Ï€rÂ·dr  \n- Ï€rÂ²Â·dr  \n- 2Ï€rÂ²Â·dr  \n\nImagine straightening the ring into a shape with width equal to its inner circumference (2Ï€r). The resulting shape\'s thickness would be *dr*.  \n\n![Unwrapped ring visualization](/api/media/Z/download)  \n\nThis unwrapped shape approximatesâ€”but doesn\'t precisely equalâ€”a rectangle. The outer circumference slightly exceeds the inner, making the unwrapped shape\'s bottom slightly wider than its top. Nevertheless, we\'ll treat it as a rectangle with width 2Ï€r and height *dr*.  \n\n## Error Analysis and Improved Approximation  \nOur rectangular approximation introduces a small error.  \n\nHoweverâ€”and this is crucialâ€”this error diminishes as *dr* decreases. Though 2Ï€rÂ·dr isn\'t exactly correct, the approximation improves as we divide the circle into thinner rings.  \n\n![Error reduction visualization](/api/media/A/download)  \n\n## Summation Strategy  \nWe\'ve divided the circle\'s area into ring approximations of 2Ï€rÂ·dr. While summing these directly seems difficult for small *dr*, this extreme subdivision paradoxically simplifies the problem.  \n\nThe rings\' radii range from 0 to 1 (or more precisely, 0 to 1 - *dr*), spaced by *dr*.  \n\n![Summation visualization](/api/media/B/download)  \n\nVisualize stacking these rectangular approximations upright along a horizontal axis representing r-values from 0 to 1. Each rectangle has:  \n- Width: *dr*  \n- Height: 2Ï€r (the circumference at radius r)  \n\n## From Approximation to Exact Solution  \nRather than calculating sums for specific *dr*, consider what happens as *dr* approaches zero. The total rectangular area approaches the area under 2Ï€r\'s graphâ€”a triangle with:  \n- Base: 1  \n- Height: 2Ï€(1)  \n- Area: Â½ Ã— 1 Ã— 2Ï€ = Ï€  \n\nFor radius R (not 1), the area would be:  \nÂ½ Ã— R Ã— 2Ï€R = Ï€RÂ²  \n\nThis is the circle area formula. Regardless of mathematical background, this derivation demonstrates elegant reasoning.  \n\n## General Problem-Solving Principles  \nOur solution method reveals broader problem-solving tools:  \n1. **Approximation**: Break complex problems into manageable parts  \n2. **Reframing**: Convert challenging questions into equivalent graphical interpretations  \n\nConsider other applications:  \n- Calculating distance from velocity-time data  \n- Determining velocity from acceleration  \nBoth can be reframed as finding areas under curves.  \n\n## Introducing Derivatives  \nReturning to our parabola example (graph of xÂ²), let\'s define A(x) as the area under the curve from 0 to xâ€”an \"integral\" of xÂ². Small input changes (*dx*) yield output changes (*dA*) approximated by:  \ndA â‰ˆ xÂ² Ã— dx  \n\nRearranged:  \ndA/dx â‰ˆ xÂ²  \n\nThis ratioâ€”termed the **derivative** of Aâ€”measures how A(x) responds to input changes. Remarkably, this relationship holds for any function f(x):  \ndA/dx â‰ˆ f(x)  \n\n![Derivative visualization](/api/media/C/download)  \n\n## Fundamental Theorem of Calculus  \nThe inverse relationship between derivatives and integrals constitutes calculus\' fundamental theorem:  \n- Differentiation calculates instantaneous change  \n- Integration sums cumulative change  \n\nThis profound connection means:  \n1. Area functions\' derivatives yield the original curve\'s equation  \n2. Knowledge of derivatives enables solving integral problems  \n\n## Understanding Calculus Notation  \nTraditional calculus uses *dx*, *dr*, *dA* for \"infinitely small\" quantitiesâ€”a concept some find problematic. Our approach:  \n- Treat *dx* as small finite values (e.g., 0.001)  \n- Acknowledge approximations become exact as *dx* â†’ 0  \n\nFor instance, our *dr* = 0.001 ring areas weren\'t precisely 2Ï€rÂ·dr, but errors diminished as *dr* decreased.  \n\n## Conclusion  \nThis perspectiveâ€”using concrete finite differencesâ€”maintains rigor while enhancing understanding. We\'ve established:  \n1. Area calculations through refined approximation  \n2. Derivative definitions through incremental ratios  \n3. The foundational derivative-integral relationship  \n\nIn subsequent chapters, we\'ll delve deeper into derivatives\' nature and applications, always maintaining this accessible approach. Remember: With appropriate visualizations and reasoning, these concepts emerge naturally.','# Foundational Perspectives on Calculus: Geometric Intuition and Methodological Innovation\n\nThe overarching objective of this instructional sequence is to engender within the learner the epistemological conviction that the foundational principles of calculus represent natural cognitive developments rather than arbitrary formalisms. Through systematic examination of core concepts elucidated via geometric visualization, we shall illuminate their ontological origins and substantive significance.\n\n![æˆªå›¾ 2026-01-30 21-44-12.png](/api/media/14/download)\n\nThe formulation of mathematical theory constitutes a non-trivial intellectual endeavor, with a substantive distinction existing between passive comprehension of established results and active generation of novel insights. Nevertheless, throughout our exposition, we shall adopt the heuristic posture of early mathematical pioneers. By methodically constructing appropriate diagrams and engaging in sustained analytical reflection, we shall demonstrate the conceptual accessibility of these truths to an inquiring intellect. \n\n## Preliminaries: The Area of a Circular Domain as Provisional Case Study\n\nOur initial investigation concerns a quintessential geometric problem: establishing the area formula for a circular region. While the canonical expression $A = \\pi R^2$ represents common knowledge, its ontological justification warrants rigorous examination. Through conscientious study of this specific geometric question, employing iterative refinement of methodological tools, we shall arrive incidentally at three cardinal calculus concepts: integral summation, differential analysis, and their profound reciprocity via the Fundamental Theorem.\n\nFigure 1 illustrates a circular domain of radius $R$ accompanied by its standard area formula.\n\nBefore proceeding to advanced techniques, let us first establish the problem domain with parametric specificity. Consider a unit circle where $R = 1$. Initial approximation strategies suggest partitioning the circular region into elementary subdomains with tractable area estimation, subsequently aggregated to produce a global approximation.\n\nFigure 2 exhibits multiple potential decomposition strategies for the unit circle.\n\nMathematical analysis frequently rewards symmetry-preserving approaches. Among the illustrated partition schemata, those maintaining rotational symmetryâ€”specifically, sectoral (lower left) or annular (lower right) subdivisionsâ€”provide particularly productive analytical pathways. We shall focus henceforth on the concentric annular decomposition.\n\n## Analytic Framework: Annular Subdivision and Differential Approximation\n\nConsider a constituent annular element with radial coordinate $r \\in [0, 1]$. Let the annular thickness be denoted $dr$, interpreted pragmatically as a finite radial increment (e.g., $dr = 0.1$ in our illustration). While conventional pedagogy might designate such finite differences with $\\Delta r$ notation, reserving $dr$ for infinitesimal constructs, we intentionally adopt the latter convention for anticipatory conceptual reasons that will subsequently be justified.  \n\nFigure 3 highlights an annular element generated via concentric subdivision.\n\nThe geometric area of such an annular element with inner radius $r$ and thickness $dr$ may be approximated through rectilinear transformation. Mentally rectifying the annular region produces a quasi-rectangular strip with width approximating the inner circumference $2\\pi r$ and height $dr$.  \n\nFigure 4 demonstrates the rectification of an annular element.\n\nThis approximation introduces measurable geometric error since the outer circumference exceeds the inner by $2\\pi(r + dr) - 2\\pi r = 2\\pi dr$. However, crucially, the relative error diminishes as $dr \\to 0$. Formally:\n\n$$\\lim_{dr \\to 0} \\frac{\\text{Absolute Error}}{\\text{Annular Area}} = \\lim_{dr \\to 0} \\frac{\\pi dr^2}{\\pi(2rdr + dr^2)} = 0$$\n\nThus, while $2\\pi r \\cdot dr$ constitutes an inaccurate area representation for finite $dr$, it achieves asymptotic validity under progressive refinement of the partition.\n\nFigure 5 illustrates error diminution with decreasing annular thickness.\n\n## From Discrete Approximation to Continuous Integration\n\nThe aggregate circular area therefore comprises the summation of annular areas across $r \\in [0, 1]$. While explicit computation for finite $dr$ proves combinatorially intensive, consideration of the limit process yields profound analytical simplification.\n\nVisualization of the approximating areas reveals their correspondence with Riemann sums for the function $f(r) = 2\\pi r$. Specifically:\n\n1. Domain discretization: $r_k = k \\cdot dr$ for $k = 0, 1, \\ldots, N-1$ where $N = \\frac{1}{dr}$\n2. Riemann sum: $\\sum_{k=0}^{N-1} 2\\pi r_k \\cdot dr$\n3. Graphical interpretation: Area under $f(r) = 2\\pi r$ via left-endpoint rectangles\n\nFigure 6 depicts the summation as rectangular areas under a linear function.\n\nUnder limit refinement ($dr \\to 0$), the Riemann sum converges to the exact integral:\n\n$$\\lim_{dr \\to 0} \\sum_{k=0}^{1/dr - 1} 2\\pi r_k dr = \\int_{0}^{1} 2\\pi r dr = \\pi(1)^2$$\n\nGeneralizing for arbitrary radius $R$:\n\n$$\\text{Area} = \\int_{0}^{R} 2\\pi r dr = \\pi R^2$$\n\nThis elegant resolutionâ€”transmuting a complex geometric problem into equivalent area computation under a linear functionâ€”epitomizes calculus\' operational power. The conceptual transition from approximate finite summation to exact continuous integration requires meticulous examination, as it constitutes the essence of integral calculus.\n\n## Generalization: From Geometric Specificity to Analytic Universality\n\nBeyond its inherent geometric significance, this solution exemplifies a ubiquitous mathematical strategy: \n\n1. **Problem Discretization**: Approximate a continuous quantity via summation of manageable finite elements\n2. **Reframing via Functional Correspondence**: Recognize the summation as a Riemann sum associated with a particular function\n3. **Limit Analysis**: Establish equality between the original quantity and the definite integral of said function\n\nThis paradigm extends to numerous scientific domains. Consider kinematic exemplars:\n\n**Problem**: Determine displacement from time-dependent velocity $v(t)$  \n**Approach**:\n$$\\Delta x \\approx \\sum v(t_k) \\Delta t \\longrightarrow \\int_{t_0}^{t_1} v(t) dt$$\n\n**Problem**: Determine velocity from time-dependent acceleration $a(t)$  \n**Approach**:\n$$\\Delta v \\approx \\sum a(t_k) \\Delta t \\longrightarrow \\int_{t_0}^{t_1} a(t) dt$$\n\nFigure 7 illustrates acceleration-velocity-area correspondence.\n\nCentral to these formulations is the recognition that when cumulative approximations correspond to Riemann sums, the original problem becomes equivalent to definite integration.\n\n## Integral Functions and Differential Relationships\n\nExtending beyond definite integrals, we consider the function $A(x) = \\int_{0}^{x} t^2 dt$ representing the area under $f(t) = t^2$ from $0$ to $x$. Determination of $A(x)$ represents a prototypical integration problem fundamental to calculus.\n\nPivotal insight emerges upon examining $A(x)$\'s response to marginal input variation $dx$. The resultant area increment $dA$ satisfies:\n\n$$dA \\approx f(x) dx = x^2 dx \\implies \\frac{dA}{dx} \\approx x^2$$\n\nAs $dx \\to 0$, the approximation achieves exactitude:\n\n$$\\frac{dA}{dx} = \\lim_{dx \\to 0} \\frac{A(x + dx) - A(x)}{dx} = x^2$$\n\nThus, we discern that the derivative of the area accumulation function $A(x)$ yields the original integrand $x^2$. This constitutes a particular manifestation of the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, establishing inverse operations between differentiation and integration.\n\nFigure 8 demonstrates the differential relationship $dA/dx = f(x)$.\n\nThis derivative-integral reciprocity permeates calculus applications. Knowledge that an unknown function $A(x)$ satisfies $A\'(x) = f(x)$ enables reverse-engineering of $A(x)$ through antidifferentiationâ€”the quintessential solution strategy for integral problems.\n\n## Philosophical Considerations on Notational Semantics\n\nThe differential notation $dr$, $dx$, $dA$ etc. permeates calculus formalism while provoking persistent ontological debate. Traditional interpretations reference \"infinitesimal quantities\"â€”mathematical entities simultaneously non-zero yet smaller than any positive real number. However, such conceptions introduce metaphysical complications absent from standard real analysis.  \n\nOur pedagogical approach embraces an alternative epistemology: \n\n1. Interpret differentials as finite real increments (e.g., $dr = 0.001$)\n2. Acknowledge resultant approximations as inexact for finite differences \n3. Recognize asymptotic exactitude under limit processes ($dr \\to 0$)  \n\nWhile convention often reserves $\\Delta r$ for finite differences and $dr$ for derivatives, our intentional notation emphasizes conceptual continuity between discrete approximation and continuous limit. Crucially, thisframework preserves rigorous real analysis foundations while enhancing cognitive accessibility.  \n\n## Concluding Synthesis and Forward Orientation  \n\nThrough systematic examination of a specific geometric problem, we have discerned the conceptual underpinnings of integral summation, differential analysis, and their profound reciprocity. The methodical progression from finite approximation to infinite refinement reveals calculus not as an arcane formalism, but as a natural extension of spatial reasoning validated by limit processes.  \n\nSubsequent exposition will formalize derivative concepts, explore differentiation techniques, generalize integral formulations, and rigorously establish the Fundamental Theorem. Throughout, we shall maintain the orienting perspective that these constructs emerge inevitably from disciplined geometric intuition and analytic reflection.','# My goal is for you to come away from this series feeling like you could have invented calculus. That is, cover these core ideas, but in a way that makes clear where they actually come from, and what they really mean, using an all-around visual approach.\næˆ‘çš„ç›®æ ‡æ˜¯è®©ä½ åœ¨å­¦ä¹ å®Œè¿™ä¸ªç³»åˆ—åŽï¼Œæ„Ÿè§‰è‡ªå·±åŽŸæœ¬å¯ä»¥å‘æ˜Žå¾®ç§¯åˆ†ã€‚ä¹Ÿå°±æ˜¯è¯´ï¼Œæˆ‘ä»¬è¦æ¶µç›–è¿™äº›æ ¸å¿ƒæ¦‚å¿µï¼Œä½†è¦ä»¥ä¸€ç§æ¸…æ™°å±•ç¤ºå®ƒä»¬å®žé™…æ¥æºå’ŒçœŸæ­£å«ä¹‰çš„æ–¹å¼å‘ˆçŽ°ï¼Œå…¨ç¨‹é‡‡ç”¨å¯è§†åŒ–æ–¹æ³•ã€‚  \n![æˆªå›¾ 2026-01-30 21-44-12.png](/api/media/14/download)\n\n# Inventing math is no joke, and there is a difference between being told why something makes sense and actually generating it from scratch. But at all points I want you to think to yourself if you were an early mathematician pondering these ideas and drawing the right diagrams, does it feel reasonable that you could have stumbled upon these truths yourself?\nå‘æ˜Žæ•°å­¦ç»éžå„¿æˆï¼Œç†è§£æŸäº‹ç‰©çš„åˆç†æ€§ä¸Žå…¶ä»Žé›¶å¼€å§‹åˆ›é€ å­˜åœ¨æœ¬è´¨åŒºåˆ«ã€‚ä½†åœ¨æ­¤è¿‡ç¨‹ä¸­ï¼Œæˆ‘å¸Œæœ›ä½ æ—¶åˆ»æ€è€ƒï¼šå¦‚æžœä½ æ˜¯æ—©æœŸæ•°å­¦å®¶ï¼Œå¸¦ç€è¿™äº›é—®é¢˜å¹¶ç»˜åˆ¶æ­£ç¡®å›¾è¡¨ï¼Œæ˜¯å¦ä¼šæ„Ÿè§‰è‡ªå·±æœ¬å¯ä»¥ç‹¬ç«‹å‘çŽ°è¿™äº›çœŸç†ï¼Ÿ  \n\n# In this chapter, I want to show how you might stumble into the core ideas of calculus by thinking deeply about one very specific bit of geometry: The area of a circle. Maybe you know that the area is Ï€ multiplied by the radius R squared, but why? Is there a way to think about where this formula comes from?\næœ¬ç« ä¸­ï¼Œæˆ‘å°†å±•ç¤ºå¦‚ä½•é€šè¿‡å¯¹ä¸€ä¸ªå…·ä½“å‡ ä½•é—®é¢˜â€”â€”åœ†çš„é¢ç§¯â€”â€”è¿›è¡Œæ·±å…¥æ€è€ƒæ—¶ï¼Œæ„å¤–è§¦åŠå¾®ç§¯åˆ†çš„æ ¸å¿ƒç†å¿µã€‚æˆ–è®¸ä½ çŸ¥é“é¢ç§¯ç­‰äºŽÏ€ä¹˜ä»¥åŠå¾„Rçš„å¹³æ–¹ï¼Œä½†ä¸ºä½•å¦‚æ­¤ï¼Ÿæ˜¯å¦å­˜åœ¨è¿½æº¯è¿™ä¸ªå…¬å¼æ¥æºçš„æ–¹å¼ï¼Ÿ  \n\n# This figure illustrates the area of a circle with radius R and the formula that calculates its area.\nè¯¥å›¾å±•ç¤ºäº†åŠå¾„ä¸ºRçš„åœ†åŠå…¶é¢ç§¯è®¡ç®—å…¬å¼ã€‚  \n![alt text](/api/media/X/download)\n\n# Contemplating this problem and leaving yourself open to generalizing the tools you use along the way can actually lead you to a glimpse of three big ideas in calculus: Integrals, derivatives, and the fact that they are opposites.\næ€è€ƒè¿™ä¸ªé—®é¢˜å¹¶å¼€æ”¾å¿ƒæ€æ¥æŽ¨å¹¿ä½¿ç”¨è¿‡ç¨‹ä¸­çš„å·¥å…·ï¼Œå®žé™…ä¸Šèƒ½è®©ä½ çª¥è§å¾®ç§¯åˆ†çš„ä¸‰å¤§æ ¸å¿ƒç†å¿µï¼šç§¯åˆ†ã€å¯¼æ•°ä»¥åŠå®ƒä»¬äº’ä¸ºé€†è¿ç®—çš„ç‰¹æ€§ã€‚  \n\n# But the story of finding the area starts more simply: Just you, and a circle. To be concrete, let\'s say the radius is 1 unit. If you weren\'t worried about the exact area, and just wanted to estimate it, one way you might go about it is to chop the circle into smaller pieces whose areas are easier to approximate, then add up the results.\nä½†æ±‚é¢ç§¯çš„æ•…äº‹èµ·ç‚¹æ›´ç®€å•ï¼šåªæœ‰ä½ å’Œåœ†ã€‚å…·ä½“è€Œè¨€ï¼Œè®¾åŠå¾„ä¸º1å•ä½ã€‚è‹¥ä½ æ— éœ€ç²¾ç¡®é¢ç§¯è€Œä»…éœ€ä¼°ç®—ï¼Œå¯å°†åœ†åˆ†å‰²ä¸ºæ›´æ˜“è¿‘ä¼¼è®¡ç®—é¢ç§¯çš„å°å—ï¼Œå†å°†ç»“æžœç›¸åŠ ã€‚  \n\n# There are many ways you might go about this, each of which may lead to its own interesting line of reasoning.\nå®žçŽ°æ­¤ç›®æ ‡æœ‰å¤šç§æ–¹å¼ï¼Œæ¯æ¡è·¯å¾„éƒ½å¯èƒ½å¼•å‘ç‹¬ç‰¹è€Œæœ‰è¶£çš„æŽ¨ç†é“¾æ¡ã€‚  \n![alt text](/api/media/X/download)\n\n# Math has a tendency to reward you when you respect symmetry, so among the divisions shown above, either the bottom left one dividing into pizza slices or the bottom right which divides into concentric rings will be likely to lead us down a fruitful path. For now, let\'s think about the division into concentric rings.\nå½“ä½ å°Šé‡å¯¹ç§°æ€§æ—¶ï¼Œæ•°å­¦å¾€å¾€ä¼šç»™äºˆå›žæŠ¥ã€‚å› æ­¤åœ¨ä¸Šå›¾æ‰€ç¤ºçš„åˆ†å‰²æ–¹å¼ä¸­ï¼Œå·¦ä¸‹è§’æŠ«è¨åˆ‡ç‰‡å¼æˆ–å³ä¸‹è§’åŒå¿ƒçŽ¯å¼åˆ†å‰²æ›´å¯èƒ½å¼•å‘å¯Œæœ‰æˆæ•ˆçš„è·¯å¾„ã€‚çŽ°è®©æˆ‘ä»¬å…ˆæ€è€ƒåŒå¿ƒçŽ¯åˆ†å‰²æ³•ã€‚  \n\n# Focus on just one of those concentric rings, and let\'s call its radius r, which will be some number between 0 and 1. If we can find a nice expression for the area of one such ring, and if we find a nice way to add them all up, it might lead us to an understanding of the full circle\'s area.\nèšç„¦å…¶ä¸­æŸä¸ªåŒå¿ƒçŽ¯ï¼Œè®¾å…¶åŠå¾„ä¸ºrï¼ˆå€¼åœ¨0åˆ°1ä¹‹é—´ï¼‰ã€‚è‹¥èƒ½æ‰¾åˆ°è¡¨è¾¾å•çŽ¯é¢ç§¯çš„ä¼˜é›…å…¬å¼ï¼Œå¹¶æ‰¾åˆ°æ±‚å’Œè‰¯æ–¹ï¼Œä¾¿å¯é€šå‘ç†è§£æ•´ä¸ªåœ†é¢ç§¯ä¹‹è·¯ã€‚  \n![alt text](/api/media/X/download)\n\n# In the spirit of using what will come to be standard calculus notations, let\'s call the thickness of one of these rings â€œdrâ€, which you can read as meaning â€œthe tiny difference in radius from one ring to the nextâ€. In the drawing above, for example, dr = 0.1.\næœ¬ç€ä½¿ç”¨æœªæ¥æ ‡å‡†å¾®ç§¯åˆ†ç¬¦å·çš„ç²¾ç¥žï¼Œæˆ‘ä»¬å°†çŽ¯çš„åŽšåº¦è®°ä¸º\"dr\"ï¼Œå¯ç†è§£ä¸º\"ç›¸é‚»çŽ¯é—´å¾®å°çš„åŠå¾„å·®\"ã€‚ä¾‹å¦‚ä¸Šå›¾ä¸­ï¼Œdr=0.1ã€‚  \n\n# You should know that some people, perhaps even most people, would object to our using the notation dr to represent a specific not-infinitely-small size like this, suggesting instead we use some other notation, like Î”r. Well, it\'s my house and my rules, and I have my reasons, which we\'ll get into later. For now, let\'s think about dr as simply being some number without worrying about what phrases like â€œinfinitely smallâ€ would mean.\néœ€çŸ¥æœ‰äº›äººï¼ˆç”šè‡³å¤šæ•°äººï¼‰ä¼šåå¯¹æˆ‘ä»¬ç”¨drè¡¨ç¤ºè¿™ç§éžæ— ç©·å°çš„å…·ä½“å°ºå¯¸ï¼Œå»ºè®®æ”¹ç”¨å¦‚Î”rç­‰ç¬¦å·ã€‚ä½†è¿™æ˜¯æˆ‘çš„é¢†åŸŸæˆ‘çš„è§„åˆ™ï¼ŒåŽç»­å°†è§£é‡ŠåŽŸå› ã€‚ç›®å‰ä»…éœ€å°†drè§†ä¸ºæ™®é€šæ•°å€¼ï¼Œæš‚ä¸æ·±ç©¶\"æ— ç©·å°\"ä¹‹å«ä¹‰ã€‚  \n\n# Which of the following is closest to the area of one of these rings with inner radius r and thickness dr?\nå¯¹äºŽå†…åŠå¾„rã€åŽšåº¦drçš„çŽ¯ï¼Œä»¥ä¸‹å“ªé¡¹æœ€æŽ¥è¿‘å…¶é¢ç§¯ï¼Ÿ  \n- Ï€rÂ²Â·dr  \n- 2Ï€rÂ²Â·dr  \n- 2Ï€rÂ·dr  \n- Ï€rÂ·dr  \n\n# You might imagine straightening out this ring, into a shape whose width is the inner circumference of the ring, which is 2Ï€r. This is the very definition of Ï€, relating the circumference of a circle to its diameter, or radius. The thickness of this unwrapped shape would be dr.\nä½ å¯æƒ³è±¡å°†æ­¤çŽ¯å±•å¼€â€”â€”å…¶å®½åº¦ä¸ºå†…å‘¨é•¿2Ï€rï¼ˆè¿™æ­£æ˜¯Ï€çš„å®šä¹‰ï¼šå‘¨é•¿ä¸Žç›´å¾„æˆ–åŠå¾„çš„å…³ç³»ï¼‰ã€‚å±•å¼€å›¾å½¢çš„åŽšåº¦å³ä¸ºdrã€‚  \n![alt text](/api/media/X/download)\n\n# If this unwrapped shape is meant to perfectly match the area of the ring, it will be approximately, but not exactly, a rectangle. Because the outer circumference of the ring will be slightly larger than the inner circumference, the bottom of our unwrapped shape will be slightly wider than the top. However, if we\'re comfortable beginning the exploration by only approximating the area of each small piece, we could consider this to be approximately a rectangle with a width of 2Ï€r and a height of dr.\nè‹¥å±•å¹³å›¾å½¢éœ€å®Œç¾Žå¯¹åº”çŽ¯é¢ç§¯ï¼Œåˆ™è¿‘ä¼¼ä¸ºçŸ©å½¢ï¼ˆä½†éžç²¾ç¡®ï¼‰ã€‚ç”±äºŽå¤–å‘¨é•¿ç•¥å¤§äºŽå†…å‘¨é•¿ï¼Œå±•å¹³å›¾å½¢åº•éƒ¨ç•¥å®½äºŽé¡¶éƒ¨ã€‚ä½†è‹¥å¯æŽ¥å—åˆå§‹é˜¶æ®µè¿›è¡Œè¿‘ä¼¼ï¼Œåˆ™å¯è§†å…¶ä¸ºå®½2Ï€rã€é«˜drçš„è¿‘ä¼¼çŸ©å½¢ã€‚  \n\n# The difference between the true area of the ring and the area of the rectangular approximation introduces some small error.\nçŽ¯çš„çœŸå®žé¢ç§¯ä¸ŽçŸ©å½¢è¿‘ä¼¼ä¹‹é—´å­˜åœ¨å¾®å°è¯¯å·®ã€‚  \n![alt text](/api/media/X/download)\n\n# However, and this will be a key idea, this error becomes tiny compared to the overall area when dr is small. In other words, approximating the area of each ring as 2Ï€rÂ·dr is wrong, but as we chop up the circle into finer and finer rings, it becomes less and less wrong.\nç„¶è€Œï¼ˆå…³é”®ç†å¿µåœ¨æ­¤ï¼‰ï¼šå½“drè¶³å¤Ÿå°æ—¶ï¼Œè¯¯å·®ç›¸æ¯”æ€»é¢ç§¯å°†å˜å¾—å¾®ä¸è¶³é“ã€‚æ¢è¨€ä¹‹ï¼Œç”¨2Ï€rÂ·drè¿‘ä¼¼å•ä¸ªçŽ¯é¢ç§¯è™½æœ‰è¯¯å·®ï¼Œä½†éšç€åˆ†å‰²è¶Šæ¥è¶Šç»†ï¼Œè¯¯å·®è¶Šæ¥è¶Šå°ã€‚  \n![alt text](/api/media/X/download)\n\n# So you\'ve broken up the area of this circle into all these rings, and you\'re approximating the area of each one as 2Ï€Â·rÂ·dr. You might think that actually adding all those areas together will be a nightmare, especially if you\'re seeking more accurate approximations with finer and finer divisions of the circle. However, being the bold mathematician that you are, you might have a hunch that taking this process to the utmost extreme may actually make things easier rather than harder.\näºŽæ˜¯ä½ å°†åœ†é¢ç§¯åˆ†è§£ä¸ºä¼—å¤šçŽ¯ï¼Œæ¯çŽ¯é¢ç§¯è¿‘ä¼¼ä¸º2Ï€Â·rÂ·drã€‚ä½ å¯èƒ½è®¤ä¸ºå®žé™…æ±‚å’Œè¿‡ç¨‹å°†æ˜¯å™©æ¢¦â€”â€”å°¤å…¶è¿½æ±‚æ›´ç²¾ç¡®è¿‘ä¼¼æ—¶åˆ†å‰²è¶Šæ¥è¶Šç»†ã€‚ä½†ä½œä¸ºå‹‡æ•¢çš„æ•°å­¦å®¶ï¼Œä½ æˆ–è®¸ç›´è§‰æ„Ÿåˆ°ï¼šå°†è¿‡ç¨‹æŽ¨å‘æžè‡´åè€Œä¼šç®€åŒ–é—®é¢˜ã€‚  \n\n# The size of that inner radius for the rings ranges from 0 for the smallest ring up to 1 for the largest, spaced out by whatever thickness we chose for dr, like 0.1. Or rather, they range from 0 up to 1âˆ’dr, but as dr gets smaller that upper bound looks more and more like 1.\nçŽ¯çš„å†…å¾„èŒƒå›´ä»Žæœ€ä¸­å¿ƒçš„0åˆ°æœ€å¤–å±‚çš„1ï¼Œé—´éš”ä¸ºæ‰€é€‰drå€¼ï¼ˆå¦‚0.1ï¼‰ã€‚å‡†ç¡®è¯´æ˜¯ä»Ž0åˆ°1-drèŒƒå›´ï¼Œä½†éšç€drå˜å°ï¼Œä¸Šé™è¶Šæ¥è¶ŠæŽ¥è¿‘1ã€‚  \n![alt text](/api/media/X/download)\n\n# To help draw what adding those areas looks like, think about adding together your rectangular approximations. A nice way to visualize this is to fit all those rectangles upright, side-by-side standing on a horizontal axis. We can think of this horizontal axis as representing all the values of r ranging from 0 up to 1.\nä¸ºç›´è§‚å±•çŽ°æ±‚å’Œè¿‡ç¨‹ï¼Œæƒ³è±¡å°†æ‰€æœ‰çŸ©å½¢è¿‘ä¼¼ç›´ç«‹æŽ’åˆ—äºŽæ°´å¹³è½´æ—ã€‚æ­¤è½´å¯è§†ä¸ºrä»Ž0åˆ°1çš„æ‰€æœ‰å–å€¼ã€‚  \n\n# Each rectangle has a thickness of dr, which is also the spacing between different values of r, which is why they fit so snugly right together. The height of any rectangle above a value of r, is exactly 2Ï€Â·r. This is the circumference of the corresponding ring.\næ¯ä¸ªçŸ©å½¢åŽšåº¦drå³rå€¼é—´éš”â€”â€”æ•…å®ƒä»¬ç´§å¯†ç›¸é‚»ã€‚çŸ©å½¢åœ¨rå¤„çš„é«˜åº¦ç²¾ç¡®ä¸º2Ï€rï¼ˆå¯¹åº”çŽ¯çš„å‘¨é•¿ï¼‰ã€‚  \n![alt text](/api/media/X/download)\n\n# What is the height of the rectangle whose left corner touches r=0.3? Note: This is the rectangle highlighted in the still figure above.\nå·¦è§’ä½äºŽr=0.3çš„çŸ©å½¢é«˜åº¦æ˜¯å¤šå°‘ï¼Ÿ æ³¨ï¼šå³ä¸Šå›¾çªå‡ºæ˜¾ç¤ºçš„çŸ©å½¢ã€‚  \n- 3/10 Ï€  \n- 3/5 Ï€  \n\n# A nice way to think of this setup is to draw the graph of 2Ï€r, which is this straight line with a slope of 2Ï€. Each of these rectangles extends up to the point where it just barely touches the graph.\nè¿™ç§è®¾ç½®çš„ç†è§£æ–¹å¼ä¹‹ä¸€ï¼šç»˜åˆ¶æ–œçŽ‡ä¸º2Ï€çš„ç›´çº¿å›¾2Ï€rã€‚æ¯ä¸ªçŸ©å½¢æ°å¥½å»¶ä¼¸è‡³è§¦è¾¾æ­¤æ›²çº¿å¤„ã€‚  \n\n# The unraveled rings under the graph of 2Ï€r.\n2Ï€rå›¾åƒä¸‹å±•å¹³çš„çŽ¯å›¾ç¤ºã€‚  \n\n# Again, we\'re being approximate here. Each of those rectangles only approximates the area of their corresponding ring from the circle. But remember, the approximation of 2Ï€Â·rÂ·dr for the area of each ring will get less and less wrong as the size of dr gets smaller and smaller.\nå†æ¬¡å¼ºè°ƒï¼Œæ­¤å¤„ä»…æ˜¯è¿‘ä¼¼ã€‚å„çŸ©å½¢ä»…è¿‘ä¼¼åœ†çš„å¯¹åº”çŽ¯é¢ç§¯ã€‚ä½†è¯·ç‰¢è®°ï¼šéšç€drå‡å°ï¼Œç”¨2Ï€Â·rÂ·drè¿‘ä¼¼çŽ¯é¢ç§¯çš„è¯¯å·®å°†è¶Šæ¥è¶Šå°ã€‚  \n\n# Again, concretely adding up the areas of all these rectangles would be a royal pain, but that\'s when you get a crazy thought: Maybe, just maybe, asking what this sum approaches as the choice of dr gets smaller will be easier than ever actually computing the sum for any specific value of dr.\nå…·ä½“æ±‚æ‰€æœ‰çŸ©å½¢é¢ç§¯ç¡®å®žç¹çï¼Œä½†ä½ çªç„¶äº§ç”Ÿç–¯ç‹‚æƒ³æ³•ï¼šæˆ–è®¸å½“drè¶‹è¿‘äºŽ0æ—¶æ±‚å’Œçš„æžé™å€¼ï¼Œä¼šæ¯”å¯¹ä»»ä½•å…·ä½“dræ±‚å’Œæ›´å®¹æ˜“ã€‚  \n\n# This has a very beautiful meaning when looking at the sum of the areas of all these rectangles. For smaller and smaller choices of dr, notice how all their area in aggregate simply looks like the area under this graph. The smaller this value dr is, the closer that aggregate area of the rectangles is to being precisely the area under the graph.\nè¿™åœ¨è§‚å¯ŸçŸ©å½¢æ€»é¢ç§¯æ—¶å‘ˆçŽ°ç¾Žå¦™å«ä¹‰ï¼šéšdrå‡å°ï¼Œæ€»é¢ç§¯é€æ¸è¶‹å‘å›¾åƒä¸‹æ–¹åŒºåŸŸã€‚drè¶Šå°ï¼ŒçŸ©å½¢æ€»é¢ç§¯è¶Šç²¾ç¡®é€¼è¿‘æ›²çº¿ä¸‹é¢ç§¯ã€‚  \n\n# This portion under the graph is a triangle, whose base is 1, and whose height is 2Ï€Â·1. So the area, which is Â½ base times height, comes out to be Ï€Â·1Â².\nè¯¥åŒºåŸŸä¸ºåº•1ã€é«˜2Ï€Â·1çš„ä¸‰è§’å½¢ã€‚é¢ç§¯Â½åº•Ã—é«˜=Ï€Â·1Â²ã€‚  \n\n# Or, if the radius of our original circle had been some other value, capital R, that area would be 1/2 Â· R Â· 2Ï€R.\nè‹¥åœ†åŠå¾„ä¸ºæŸå€¼Rï¼Œåˆ™é¢ç§¯ä¸ºÂ½Â·RÂ·2Ï€Rã€‚  \n\n# This is the formula for the area of a circle. It doesn\'t matter who you are or what you typically think of math; that right there is beautiful.\nè¿™æ­£æ˜¯åœ†çš„é¢ç§¯å…¬å¼ã€‚æ— è®ºä½ å¯¹æ•°å­¦æŒä½•çœ‹æ³•ï¼Œæ­¤è¿‡ç¨‹æ— ç–‘å±•çŽ°äº†æ•°å­¦ä¹‹ç¾Žã€‚  \n\n# Being the mathematician that you are, you don\'t just care about finding the answer, you care about developing general problem-solving tools and techniques. So take a moment to meditate on what just happened, and why that worked, because the way we transitioned from something approximate to something precise is actually pretty subtle, and cuts deep to what calculus is all about.\nä½œä¸ºæ•°å­¦å®¶ï¼Œä½ ä¸ä»…å…³å¿ƒç­”æ¡ˆï¼Œæ›´å…³æ³¨é€šç”¨è§£é¢˜å·¥å…·çš„æž„å»ºã€‚è¯·ç¨ä½œæ€è€ƒï¼šæˆ‘ä»¬å¦‚ä½•ä»Žè¿‘ä¼¼è¿‡æ¸¡åˆ°ç²¾ç¡®ï¼Ÿæ­¤è¿‡ç¨‹ç²¾å¦™ç»ä¼¦ï¼Œç›´å‡»å¾®ç§¯åˆ†æœ¬è´¨ã€‚  \n\n# You had a problem that could be approximated with the sum of many small numbers, each of which looked like 2Ï€Â·rÂ·dr for values of r ranging between 0 and 1. Each of those approximates the area of one of these thin rings.\nä½ é‡åˆ°ä¸€ä¸ªé—®é¢˜ï¼šå®ƒå¯é€šè¿‡è¯¸å¤šå°æ•°å€¼çš„å’Œè¿‘ä¼¼ï¼Œæ¯ä¸ªå€¼å½¢ä¼¼2Ï€Â·rÂ·drï¼ˆrå–å€¼0åˆ°1ï¼‰ã€‚æ¯ä¸ªæ•°å€¼è¿‘ä¼¼è–„çŽ¯é¢ç§¯ã€‚  \n\n# The small number dr represents our choice for the thickness of each ring, for example 0.1, and there are two important things to note. First, not only is dr a factor in the quantity we\'re adding up, 2Ï€Â·rÂ·dr, it also gives the spacing between the different values of r. The smaller the choice of dr, the better the approximation.\nå¾®å°å€¼drè¡¨ç¤ºçŽ¯åŽšåº¦ï¼ˆå¦‚0.1ï¼‰ï¼Œéœ€æ³¨æ„ä¸¤ç‚¹ï¼šdrä¸ä»…æ˜¯æ±‚å’Œé¡¹2Ï€Â·rÂ·drä¸­çš„å› å­ï¼ŒåŒæ—¶ä¹Ÿå†³å®šrå€¼é—´éš”ã€‚drè¶Šå°ï¼Œè¿‘ä¼¼è¶Šä½³ã€‚  \n\n# Adding all those numbers could be seen in a different clever way as adding up the areas of many thin rectangles sitting under a graph; in this example that graph was of the function 2Ï€Â·r.\nè¿™äº›æ•°å€¼ä¹‹å’Œè¿˜å¯ç†è§£ä¸ºï¼šæ±‚å‡½æ•°2Ï€ræ›²çº¿ä¸‹ä¼—å¤šç»†é•¿çŸ©å½¢é¢ç§¯ä¹‹å’Œã€‚  \n\n# So on the one hand the sum of the areas of these slices approaches the area of the circle for smaller and smaller choices of dr. But on the other hand, that sum also approaches the area under this graph. This is how we concluded that our original hard problem had an answer equal to the area under a certain graph; not just approximately equal, but precisely equal. I\'ll emphasize again what a big theme in calculus this is: The purpose of approximating a question by subdividing it like this is not that we don\'t care about precision, it\'s that the approximation using many smaller pieces gives us the flexibility to reframe our original hard question into something simpler.\nå› æ­¤ï¼šè¿™äº›è–„ç‰‡é¢ç§¯ä¹‹å’Œéšdrå‡å°è¶‹è¿‘åœ†é¢ç§¯ï¼›å¦ä¸€æ–¹é¢ï¼Œè¯¥å’Œä¹Ÿè¶‹è¿‘äºŽæ›²çº¿ä¸‹é¢ç§¯ã€‚å¦‚æ­¤æˆ‘ä»¬æ–­å®šåŽŸéš¾é¢˜çš„ç­”æ¡ˆç­‰äºŽç‰¹å®šæ›²çº¿ä¸‹æ–¹åŒºåŸŸâ€”â€”ä¸ä»…æ˜¯è¿‘ä¼¼ï¼Œè€Œç²¾ç¡®ç­‰äºŽã€‚å†æ¬¡å¼ºè°ƒå¾®ç§¯åˆ†æ ¸å¿ƒä¸»é¢˜ï¼šé€šè¿‡ç»†åˆ†é€¼è¿‘é—®é¢˜ç›®çš„å¹¶éžèˆå¼ƒç²¾åº¦ï¼Œè€Œæ˜¯é€šè¿‡å¾®å°åˆ†å‰²çš„è¿‘ä¼¼èŽ·å¾—çµæ´»æ€§ï¼Œå°†åŽŸå§‹éš¾é¢˜é‡æž„ä¸ºæ›´ç®€å½¢å¼ã€‚  \n\n# A lot of other hard problems in math and science can be broken down and approximated as the sum of many small quantities. For example if you want to figure out how far a car has gone based on its velocity at each point in time, you might range through many many points in time, and multiply the velocity at each time t by some tiny change in time dt to get the corresponding bit of distance traveled in the little time.\nä¼—å¤šæ•°å­¦ä¸Žç§‘å­¦éš¾é¢˜çš†å¯åˆ†è§£ä¸ºè¯¸å¤šå¾®å°é‡ä¹‹å’Œã€‚ä¾‹å¦‚è‹¥æƒ³æ ¹æ®æ¯æ—¶åˆ»é€Ÿåº¦è®¡ç®—æ±½è½¦è¡Œé©¶è·ç¦»ï¼Œå¯å°†æ—¶é—´åˆ†ä¸ºä¼—å¤šç‚¹ï¼Œå„æ—¶åˆ»tçš„é€Ÿåº¦ä¹˜ä»¥æ—¶é—´å¾®å°å˜åŒ–dtï¼Œå¾—dtæ—¶é—´å†…è¡Œé©¶çš„å¾®è·ç¦»ã€‚  \n\n# I\'ll talk through the details of examples like this later in the series, but at a high level, many of these types of problems turn out to be equivalent to finding the area under some graph, in much the same way that our circle problem did.\nåŽç»­ç« èŠ‚å°†è¯¦è¿°æ­¤ç±»å®žä¾‹ï¼Œä½†é«˜å±‚æ¬¡çœ‹ï¼šå¤§é‡æ­¤ç±»é—®é¢˜ç­‰ä»·äºŽæ±‚æŸæ›²çº¿ä¸‹é¢ç§¯â€”â€”æ­£å¦‚æˆ‘ä»¬çš„åœ†é¢ç§¯é—®é¢˜ã€‚  \n\n# This happens whenever the quantities that you\'re adding up, the ones whose sum approximates your original problem, can be thought of as the area of many thin rectangles sitting side-by-side like this. If finer and finer approximations of your original problem correspond to thinner and thinner rectangles, the original problem will be equivalent to finding the area under some graph.\nåªè¦æ±‚å’Œçš„é‡æž„å¯è§†ä¸ºå¹¶åˆ—ç»†é•¿çŸ©å½¢é¢ç§¯ä¹‹å’Œï¼Œä¸”æ›´ç²¾è¿‘ä¼¼å¯¹åº”æ›´è–„çŸ©å½¢ï¼Œåˆ™åŽŸé—®é¢˜ç­‰ä»·äºŽæ±‚æŸæ›²çº¿ä¸‹é¢ç§¯ã€‚  \n\n# In the same way velocity measures the change in position over the change in time, acceleration measures the change in velocity over the change in time. Can the velocity of a car be modeled as the area under the graph of acceleration?\né€Ÿåº¦æµ‹é‡ä½ç§»ç›¸å¯¹æ—¶é—´å˜åŒ–çŽ‡ï¼ŒåŠ é€Ÿåº¦åˆ™æµ‹é‡é€Ÿåº¦ç›¸å¯¹æ—¶é—´å˜åŒ–çŽ‡ã€‚æ±½è½¦é€Ÿåº¦èƒ½å¦å»ºæ¨¡ä¸ºåŠ é€Ÿåº¦æ›²çº¿ä¸‹é¢ç§¯ï¼Ÿ  \n\n# Again, notice that the purpose of the small approximations is not that we intend to use them directly, per se, but that the two separate ways to think about what these approximations approach lets us reframe the question of how far the car has traveled into the question of finding the area under a certain curve.\nå†æ¬¡æ³¨æ„ï¼šå¾®å°è¿‘ä¼¼çš„ç›®çš„å¹¶éžç›´æŽ¥ä½¿ç”¨å®ƒä»¬ï¼Œè€Œæ˜¯é€šè¿‡ä¸¤ç§è§†è§’è§‚å¯Ÿè¿‘ä¼¼è¶‹è¿‘è¿‡ç¨‹ï¼Œå°†\"æ±½è½¦è¡Œé©¶è·ç¦»\"é—®é¢˜é‡æž„ä¸º\"æŸæ›²çº¿ä¸‹é¢ç§¯\"é—®é¢˜ã€‚  \n\n# We\'ll see this idea in more detail later in the series, so don\'t worry if it\'s not 100% clear right now. The point is that you, as the mathematician having just solved a problem by reframing it as an area under a graph, might start thinking about how to find the area under other graphs.\nåŽç»­å°†æ›´è¯¦ç»†é˜è¿°æ­¤æ¦‚å¿µâ€”â€”çŽ°ä¸å¿…è‹›æ±‚å®Œå…¨ç†è§£ã€‚é‡ç‚¹åœ¨äºŽï¼šå½“ä½ é€šè¿‡é‡æž„ä¸ºæ›²çº¿ä¸‹é¢ç§¯è§£å†³ä¸€ä¸ªé—®é¢˜åŽï¼Œå¯èƒ½å¼€å§‹æ€è€ƒå¦‚ä½•æ±‚å…¶ä»–æ›²çº¿ä¸‹é¢ç§¯ã€‚  \n\n# We were lucky in our circle problem that the relevant area turned out to be a triangle, but imagine instead something like a parabola, the graph of the function xÂ². What\'s the area under this curve, say between the values of x=0 and x=3? It\'s hard to think about, isn\'t it?\nåœ†çš„é¢ç§¯æ¡ˆä¾‹ä¸­ç›¸å…³åŒºåŸŸæ˜¯ä¸‰è§’å½¢å®žå±žå¹¸è¿ã€‚çŽ°åœ¨æƒ³è±¡æŠ›ç‰©çº¿â€”â€”å‡½æ•°xÂ²å›¾åƒã€‚å…¶åœ¨x=0åˆ°x=3ä¹‹é—´çš„æ›²çº¿ä¸‹é¢ç§¯å¦‚ä½•æ±‚ï¼Ÿè¿™æ˜¾ç„¶æ›´å…·æŒ‘æˆ˜æ€§ã€‚  \n\n# Let me frame that question a different way: Fix that left endpoint in place at 0, and let that right endpoint vary: Can you find a function, A(x), that gives you the area under this parabola between 0 and x?\næ¢ç§æé—®æ–¹å¼ï¼šå›ºå®šå·¦ç«¯ç‚¹äºŽ0ï¼Œè®©å³ç«¯ç‚¹å˜åŠ¨ã€‚èƒ½å¦æ‰¾åˆ°ä¸€ä¸ªå‡½æ•°A(x)è¡¨ç¤ºæŠ›ç‰©çº¿åœ¨0åˆ°xä¹‹é—´çš„ä¸‹æ–¹é¢ç§¯ï¼Ÿ  \n\n# In calculus, you might call this function A(x) an â€œintegralâ€ of xÂ². Well, more precisely we\'d say this is the integral of xÂ² from 0 up to x. Or rather, to disambiguate whether x represents the variable for the function, or if it represents the right endpoint, it would be even better to describe this area as the integral of the function f(t) = tÂ² between the values 0 and x. In the lingo, you\'d see it written like this:\nå¾®ç§¯åˆ†ä¸­ï¼Œä½ å¯èƒ½ç§°A(x)ä¸ºxÂ²çš„\"ç§¯åˆ†\"ã€‚æ›´å‡†ç¡®è¯´ï¼Œè¿™æ˜¯xÂ²ä»Ž0åˆ°xçš„ç§¯åˆ†ã€‚ä¸ºåŒºåˆ†xæ˜¯å‡½æ•°å˜é‡è¿˜æ˜¯å³ç«¯ç‚¹ï¼Œå®œæè¿°ä¸ºå‡½æ•°f(t)=tÂ²åœ¨0åˆ°xé—´çš„ç§¯åˆ†ã€‚æœ¯è¯­ä¸­å¸¸å†™ä½œï¼š  \n$$ \\int_0^x t^2 \\, dt $$  \n\n# But here we\'re getting ahead of ourselves. All that matters now is that you, as the mathematician, find yourself wondering about this mystery function A(x) which gives the area under the graph of xÂ² between some fixed left point, and some variable right point. If you can find a way to compute this explicitly, you will be inventing a big part of calculus.\nä½†æˆ‘ä»¬æš‚ä¸”æ‰“ä½ã€‚å…³é”®åœ¨äºŽï¼šä½œä¸ºæ•°å­¦å®¶çš„ä½ å¼€å§‹å¥½å¥‡è¿™ä¸ªç¥žç§˜å‡½æ•°A(x)â€”â€”å®ƒç»™å‡ºxÂ²å›¾åƒä»Žå›ºå®šå·¦ç«¯ç‚¹åˆ°å¯å˜å³ç«¯ç‚¹é—´åŒºåŸŸçš„é¢ç§¯ã€‚è‹¥ä½ èƒ½æ‰¾åˆ°æ˜¾å¼è®¡ç®—A(x)çš„æ–¹æ³•ï¼Œä¾¿å‘æ˜Žäº†å¾®ç§¯åˆ†çš„é‡è¦éƒ¨åˆ†ã€‚  \n\n# Again, the reason we care about this kind of question is not just for the sake of asking hard geometry questions; it\'s because many practical problems that can be approximated by adding up a large number of small things can be reframed as a question about the area under a certain curve.\nå†æ¬¡å¼ºè°ƒï¼šæˆ‘ä»¬å…³æ³¨æ­¤ç±»é—®é¢˜ä¸ä»…å› å‡ ä½•éš¾é¢˜æœ¬èº«ï¼Œæ›´å› ä¼—å¤šå¯é€šè¿‡ç´¯åŠ å¾®å°é‡è¿‘ä¼¼çš„å®žé™…é—®é¢˜çš†å¯é‡æž„ä¸ºç‰¹å®šæ›²çº¿ä¸‹é¢ç§¯çš„æ±‚è§£ã€‚  \n\n# Finding the area represented by this integral function is genuinely hard. And whenever you come across a genuinely hard question in math, a good policy is to not try too hard to get to the answer directly, since you often just end up banging your head against a wall.\næ±‚æ­¤ç±»ç§¯åˆ†å‡½æ•°çš„é¢ç§¯å®žå±žä¸æ˜“ã€‚æ•°å­¦ä¸­é‡åˆ°çœŸæ­£éš¾é¢˜æ—¶ï¼Œåº”å¯¹è‰¯ç­–å¹¶éžå¼ºè¡Œæ±‚è§£ï¼ˆå¸¸å¾’åŠ³æ— åŠŸï¼‰ã€‚  \n\n# Instead, play around with this idea. Build up some familiarity with the interplay between the function defining a graph, in this case xÂ², and the function giving the area, our unknown function A(x), and see if there are any other questions you can ask about the setup.\nè€Œåº”æŽ¢ç´¢æ­¤æ¦‚å¿µï¼šç†Ÿæ‚‰å‡½æ•°xÂ²ï¼ˆå®šä¹‰å›¾åƒï¼‰ä¸ŽæœªçŸ¥é¢ç§¯å‡½æ•°A(x)é—´çš„ç›¸äº’ä½œç”¨ï¼Œå¹¶æ€è€ƒå¯å¯¹è®¾ç½®æå‡ºçš„å…¶ä»–é—®é¢˜ã€‚  \n\n# In that playful spirit, if you\'re lucky, here\'s something you might notice. When you slightly increase x by some tiny nudge, dx, look at the resulting change in area, represented by this sliver which I\'m going to call dA, for a tiny difference in area.\nä»¥æ¸¸æˆç²¾ç¥žæŽ¢ç´¢æ—¶ï¼Œä½ æˆ–è®¸æ³¨æ„åˆ°ï¼šå½“xå¾®å°å¢žé‡dxæ—¶ï¼Œè§‚å¯Ÿé¢ç§¯å˜åŒ–â€”â€”æˆ‘ä»¬å°†æ­¤è–„ç‰‡é¢ç§¯å˜åŒ–è®°ä¸ºdAã€‚  \n\n# That sliver can be pretty well approximated with a rectangle, one whose height is xÂ² and whose width is dx. Well, for larger choices of dx the approximation may not be that good, but for smaller and smaller choices of dx the error between the area of that sliver and the area of the approximating rectangle will become tiny compared to the area of the rectangle.\næ­¤è–„ç‰‡å¯ç”¨çŸ©å½¢è‰¯å¥½è¿‘ä¼¼ï¼šé«˜xÂ²ã€å®½dxã€‚å¯¹è¾ƒå¤§dxè¿‘ä¼¼æ•ˆæžœæ¬ ä½³ï¼Œä½†éšç€dxå‡å°ï¼Œè–„ç‰‡é¢ç§¯ä¸ŽçŸ©å½¢é¢ç§¯é—´çš„è¯¯å·®ç›¸æ¯”çŸ©å½¢è‡ªèº«å°†å¾®ä¸è¶³é“ã€‚  \n\n# This may prompt you to think about how A(x) is related to xÂ² in a pretty fun way. A change to the output of A, this little dA, is about equal to the height of the rectangle times its width.\nè¿™ä¿ƒä½¿ä½ ä»¥è¶£å‘³æ–¹å¼æ€è€ƒA(x)ä¸ŽxÂ²çš„å…³è”ï¼šAçš„è¾“å‡ºå˜åŒ–dAçº¦ç­‰äºŽçŸ©å½¢é«˜ä¹˜å®½ã€‚  \n$$ dA \\approx x^2 \\cdot dx $$  \n\n# Here, x is the input where you started and dx is the size of the little nudge the input that caused A to change.\næ­¤å¤„xä¸ºåˆå§‹è¾“å…¥ç‚¹ï¼Œdxæ˜¯å¯¼è‡´Aå˜åŒ–çš„è¾“å…¥å¾®å°å˜åŠ¨é‡ã€‚  \n\n# We could also rearrange that slightly:\nå¯æ”¹å†™ä¸ºï¼š  \n$$ \\frac{dA}{dx} \\approx x^2 $$  \n\n# This says the ratio of a tiny change in A to the tiny change in x that caused it equals the height of our graph, xÂ², at that point. Or rather, this is only approximately true, but it\'s an approximation that should get better and better for smaller choices of dx.\nè¿™æ„å‘³ç€ï¼šAçš„å¾®å°å˜åŒ–ä¸Žå¼•å‘å®ƒçš„xå¾®å°å˜åŒ–ä¹‹æ¯”ç­‰äºŽè¯¥ç‚¹å›¾åƒé«˜åº¦xÂ²ã€‚è™½ç„¶åªæ˜¯è¿‘ä¼¼æˆç«‹ï¼Œä½†éšç€dxå‡å°è¿‘ä¼¼ä¼šè¶Šæ¥è¶Šå¥½ã€‚  \n\n# For example, think about two nearby inputs, like 3 and 3.001. The change to x, then, is dx = 0.001. The change dA would be the difference between the mystery function evaluated at 3.001 and evaluated at 3. Even though we don\'t know what that mystery function is, we do know something about this change, namely that this change divided by dx = 0.001 is approximately 3Â².\nä¾‹å¦‚è€ƒè™‘ç›¸é‚»è¾“å…¥3ä¸Ž3.001ï¼šxå˜åŒ–dx=0.001ï¼ŒdAä¸ºA(3.001)ä¸ŽA(3)ä¹‹å·®ã€‚å°½ç®¡æœªçŸ¥Açš„å…·ä½“å½¢å¼ï¼Œä½†æˆ‘ä»¬çŸ¥dA/dx â‰ˆ 3Â²ã€‚  \n\n# And this relationship between tiny changes to the mystery function and the value of xÂ² is true at all inputs, not just 3. For example, we can see the same relationship at the point x=2 on the graph of xÂ².\næ­¤å…³ç³»åœ¨ä»»æ„è¾“å…¥ç‚¹å‡æˆç«‹ã€‚ä¾‹å¦‚x=2å¤„åŒæ ·æœ‰dA/dx â‰ˆ 2Â²ã€‚  \n\n# That doesn\'t immediately tell us how to find A(x), but it provides a very strong clue to work with. And there\'s nothing special about the graph xÂ² here. For any function f(x), if we call the area under its graph A(x), then this area function has the property that dA/dx â‰ˆ f(x), a slight nudge to the output of A divided by a slight nudge to the input that caused it is about equal to the height of the graph at that point, f(x). Again, that\'s an approximation that gets better and better for smaller choices of dx.\nè¿™è™½æœªç›´æŽ¥ç»™å‡ºA(x)æ±‚æ³•ï¼Œä½†æä¾›äº†å…³é”®çº¿ç´¢ã€‚ä¸”æ­¤å…³ç³»ä¸é™äºŽxÂ²â€”â€”å¯¹ä»»æ„å‡½æ•°f(x)ï¼Œè‹¥å°†æ›²çº¿ä¸‹é¢ç§¯å‡½æ•°è®°ä½œA(x)ï¼Œåˆ™Aå…·æœ‰æ€§è´¨dA/dx â‰ˆ f(x)ã€‚å†æ¬¡å¼ºè°ƒï¼šdxè¶Šå°è¿‘ä¼¼è¶Šç²¾ç¡®ã€‚  \n![alt text](/api/media/X/download)\n\n# Here we\'re stumbling onto another big idea from calculus: Derivatives. This ratio dA/dx is called the â€œderivative of Aâ€. Or, more technically, the derivative is whatever value this ratio approaches as dx gets smaller and smaller.\næˆ‘ä»¬åœ¨æ­¤å¶ç„¶è§¦åŠå¾®ç§¯åˆ†å¦ä¸€æ ¸å¿ƒç†å¿µï¼šå¯¼æ•°ã€‚æ¯”çŽ‡dA/dxç§°ä¸º\"Açš„å¯¼æ•°\"ã€‚ä¸¥æ ¼æ¥è¯´ï¼Œå¯¼æ•°æ˜¯è¯¥æ¯”çŽ‡éšdxè¶‹è¿‘äºŽ0æ—¶æ‰€é€¼è¿‘çš„å€¼ã€‚  \n\n# You and I will dive more deeply into the idea of a derivative next chapter, but loosely speaking it\'s a measure of how sensitive a function is to small changes in its input. You\'ll see as the series goes on that there are many different ways to visualize a derivative, depending on what function you\'re looking at and how you think about tiny nudges to its output.\nä¸‹ç« å°†æ·±å…¥å¯¼æ•°æ¦‚å¿µã€‚ç²—ç•¥è®²ï¼Œå¯¼æ•°è¡¡é‡å‡½æ•°å¯¹è¾“å…¥å¾®å°å˜åŒ–çš„æ•æ„Ÿåº¦ã€‚éšç€ç³»åˆ—å±•å¼€ï¼Œä½ ä¼šçœ‹åˆ°æ ¹æ®å‡½æ•°ç±»åž‹åŠè¾“å‡ºå¾®æ‰°æ€è€ƒæ–¹å¼çš„ä¸åŒï¼Œå¯¼æ•°æœ‰å„ç§å¯è§†åŒ–å‘ˆçŽ°ã€‚  \n\n# We care about derivatives because they help us solve problems, and in our little exploration here we have a slight glimpse of one way they\'re used: They are the key to solving integral problems; problems that require finding an area under a curve. When you gain enough familiarity with computing derivatives, you\'ll be able to look at a situation like the one below, where you don\'t know what a function is, but you do know that its derivative should be xÂ², and from that reverse engineer what the function must be.\næˆ‘ä»¬å…³æ³¨å¯¼æ•°å› å®ƒä»¬è§£å†³éš¾é¢˜ã€‚å½“å‰æŽ¢ç´¢ä¸­æˆ‘ä»¬çž¥è§å…¶ä¸€ç§ç”¨é€”ï¼šä½œä¸ºè§£å†³ç§¯åˆ†é—®é¢˜ï¼ˆæ±‚æ›²çº¿ä¸‹é¢ç§¯ï¼‰çš„é’¥åŒ™ã€‚å½“ä½ æŽŒæ¡å¯¼æ•°è®¡ç®—åŽï¼Œä¾¿èƒ½å¤„ç†ä¸‹è¿°æƒ…å¢ƒï¼šæœªçŸ¥æŸå‡½æ•°å½¢å¼ï¼Œä½†çŸ¥å…¶å¯¼æ•°åº”ä¸ºxÂ²ï¼Œç”±æ­¤é€†å‘æŽ¨å¯¼åŽŸå‡½æ•°ã€‚  \n\n# This back and forth between integrals and derivatives, where the derivative of the function for an area under a graph gives you back the function defining the graph itself, is called the fundamental theorem of calculus. It ties together the two big ideas of integrals and derivatives, and shows how in some sense, each one is the inverse of the other.\nç§¯åˆ†ä¸Žå¯¼æ•°é—´çš„è¿™ç§æ¥å›žå…³ç³»â€”â€”æ›²çº¿ä¸‹é¢ç§¯å‡½æ•°çš„å¯¼æ•°ç»™å‡ºåŽŸå‡½æ•°â€”â€”ç§°ä¸ºå¾®ç§¯åˆ†åŸºæœ¬å®šç†ã€‚å®ƒå°†ç§¯åˆ†ä¸Žå¯¼æ•°ä¸¤å¤§æ¦‚å¿µæ†ç»‘ï¼Œå±•ç¤ºäº†ä¸¤è€…åœ¨æŸç§æ„ä¹‰ä¸Šçš„äº’é€†æ€§ã€‚  \n\n# All of this is only a high level view. What follows in this series are the details for both these big ideas, and more. And let me reiterate, at all points in this series I want you to feel like you could have invented calculus yourself, that if you drew the right pictures and played with each idea in the right way, all of the formulas, rules, and constructs could pop out quite naturally.\nä»¥ä¸Šä»…ä¸ºé«˜å±‚æ¬¡æ¦‚è§ˆã€‚ç³»åˆ—åŽç»­å°†è¯¦è¿°è¿™äº›ç†å¿µåŠå…¶æ‹“å±•ã€‚é‡ç”³ï¼šå¸Œæœ›ä½ åœ¨å­¦ä¹ ä¸­å§‹ç»ˆæœ‰\"æœ¬å¯è‡ªåˆ›å¾®ç§¯åˆ†\"ä¹‹æ„Ÿâ€”â€”è‹¥ç»˜åˆ¶æ­£ç¡®å›¾è¡¨å¹¶ä»¥æ°å½“æ–¹å¼æŽ¢ç´¢æ¯ä¸ªæ¦‚å¿µï¼Œæ‰€æœ‰å…¬å¼ã€è§„åˆ™å’Œç»“æž„çš†å¯è‡ªç„¶æµ®çŽ°ã€‚  \n\n# Calculus is littered with expressions like dA, dr, dx, etc, which show up in the notation for both derivatives and integrals. Despite their front-and-center role, there is a surprising amount of ambiguity and conflicting instruction on what these terms really mean.\nå¾®ç§¯åˆ†å……æ–¥å¦‚dAã€drã€dxç­‰ç¬¦å·ï¼ˆè§äºŽå¯¼æ•°ä¸Žç§¯åˆ†è®°æ³•ï¼‰ã€‚å°½ç®¡æ ¸å¿ƒåœ°ä½æ˜¾è‘—ï¼Œè¿™äº›æœ¯è¯­çš„çœŸå®žå«ä¹‰å´å­˜åœ¨æƒŠäººæ­§ä¹‰ä¸ŽçŸ›ç›¾è§£é‡Šã€‚  \n\n# In this chapter I encouraged you to think of dr as the difference in the radius of our circles from one to the next, prompting you to take a very literal interpretation and imagine an actual number, like dr = 0.01. Likewise, I encouraged you to think of dA as an actual number, specifically the amount that the area under a graph changes as we nudge the right endpoint by dx, again an actual number. A more common convention would be to call these changes Î”r, Î”A, and Î”x, reserving the dx notation for changes that are â€œinfinitely smallâ€.\næœ¬ç« ä¸­æˆ‘é¼“åŠ±ä½ å°†drè§†ä¸ºç›¸é‚»çŽ¯é—´åŠå¾„å·®ï¼ˆå¦‚å…·ä½“æ•°å€¼dr=0.01ï¼‰ï¼Œå¹¶åŒæ ·å°†dAçœ‹ä½œå…·ä½“æ•°å€¼ï¼ˆå³å³ç«¯ç‚¹å˜åŠ¨dxæ—¶æ›²çº¿ä¸‹é¢ç§¯çš„å˜åŒ–é‡ï¼‰ã€‚æ›´å¸¸è§æƒ¯ä¾‹æ˜¯ç”¨Î”rã€Î”Aã€Î”xè¡¨ç¤ºæ­¤ç±»å˜åŒ–ï¼Œè€Œdxä¿ç•™ç»™\"æ— ç©·å°\"çš„å˜åŒ–ã€‚  \n\n# Infinitely small?\næ— ç©·å°ï¼Ÿ  \n\n# The issue with this is that phrases like â€œinfinitely smallâ€ and â€œinfinitesimalâ€ run the risk of, well, not actually making any sense. What is an infinitesimal change? Is it a number? If so, which number? It shouldn\'t be zero, but it also shouldn\'t be anything bigger than zero. If it\'s not a number then what is it, exactly?\né—®é¢˜åœ¨äºŽï¼š\"æ— ç©·å°\"å’Œ\"æ— é™å°\"è¿™ç±»è¡¨è¿°å®žåˆ™éš¾ä»¥è‡ªæ´½ã€‚ä½•ä¸ºæ— ç©·å°å˜åŒ–ï¼Ÿæ˜¯æ•°å­—å—ï¼Ÿè‹¥æ˜¯ï¼Œä½•ç§æ•°å­—ï¼Ÿå®ƒä¸åº”ä¸ºé›¶ï¼Œåˆä¸å¯å¤§äºŽé›¶ã€‚è‹¥éžæ•°å­—ï¼Œåˆ™å…¶æœ¬è´¨ä¸ºä½•ï¼Ÿ  \n\n# In acknowledgment of this conundrum, one common sentiment is to encourage students The typical dictum is that derivatives, despite being written as dx/df, are not really fractions, and that integrals, written as âˆ«f(x)dx, are not really sums, despite the fact that the symbol âˆ« literally derives from the letter \"S\" for \"Sum\". Instead, the student is told, each of these constructs has a separate more complicated definition. But! If a student wishes for loose intuition before stepping up their game to a real analysis class, they might find it helpful to think of dx as an infinitely small change in x, even if that phrase doesn\'t quite make sense. It is, after all, only intuition.\né¢å¯¹æ­¤å›°å¢ƒï¼Œå¸¸é—»æ­¤è¨€ï¼šå¯¼æ•°è™½å†™ä½œdx/dfï¼Œå®žåˆ™éžåˆ†æ•°ï¼›ç§¯åˆ†è™½è®°ä½œâˆ«f(x)dxä¸”ç¬¦å·âˆ«æºäºŽ\"æ±‚å’Œ\"é¦–å­—æ¯Sï¼Œå®žåˆ™éžæ±‚å’Œã€‚å­¦ç”Ÿè¢«å‘ŠçŸ¥ï¼šæ¯ä¸ªç»“æž„å‡æœ‰å„è‡ªå¤æ‚å®šä¹‰ã€‚ä½†è‹¥å­¦ç”Ÿåœ¨è¿›å…¥å®žåˆ†æžè¯¾å‰å¯»æ±‚ç›´è§‚ç†è§£ï¼Œå¯å°†dxè§†ä¸ºxçš„æ— ç©·å°å˜åŒ–ï¼ˆå³ä½¿æ¦‚å¿µæœ¬èº«æ¬ ä¸¥è°¨ï¼‰â€”â€”æ¯•ç«Ÿä»…ä¸ºç›´è§‰è¾…åŠ©ã€‚  \n\n# To my taste, this sentiment is well-intentioned but ultimately misleading. The rigorous underpinnings of calculus only ever involve real, finitely sized numbers, often followed by questions of what happens as these values get closer to 0. The proposal of this series is that when we interpret the notation of calculus, we can do so in a way which maps closely to this rigorous underpinning. If you\'re anything like I am, this actually makes calculus much more tangible and understandable than any â€œintuitionâ€ resting on the idea of an infinitesimal.\næˆ‘è®¤ä¸ºæ­¤è§‚ç‚¹è™½å–„æ„ä½†è¯¯å¯¼ã€‚å¾®ç§¯åˆ†çš„ä¸¥æ ¼åŸºç¡€ä»…æ¶‰åŠå®žæœ‰é™æ•°ï¼Œå¹¶æŽ¢ç©¶è¿™äº›æ•°è¶‹è¿‘0æ—¶çš„æƒ…å½¢ã€‚æœ¬ç³»åˆ—æè®®ï¼šè§£é‡Šå¾®ç§¯åˆ†ç¬¦å·æ—¶å¯ç´§å¯†æ˜ å°„åˆ°ä¸¥æ ¼åŸºç¡€ã€‚å¯¹ä¸Žæˆ‘ç›¸ä¼¼è€…ï¼Œæ­¤æ–¹æ³•ä½¿å¾®ç§¯åˆ†æ¯”åŸºäºŽæ— ç©·å°çš„\"ç›´è§‰\"æ›´å…·ä½“å¯ç†è§£ã€‚  \n\n# In a nutshell, here\'s the philosophy for this series: When you see expressions with terms like dx, think of those terms as representing actual small numbers, say dx=0.001. When you do this, the equations you write will become wrong, but they get less wrong as the choice for dx approaches 0. Think of the circular rings from earlier in this chapter. If you thought of dr as an actual number, say dr=0.001, none of these rings actually had an area of 2Ï€rdr. However, the percent error between the true area and 2Ï€rÂ·dr gets smaller and smaller as the choice for dr does as well.\nç®€è¨€ä¹‹ï¼Œæœ¬ç³»åˆ—å“²å­¦ï¼šå½“è§å«dxç±»é¡¹çš„è¡¨è¾¾å¼æ—¶ï¼Œå°†å…¶è§†ä¸ºå…·ä½“å°æ•°ï¼ˆå¦‚dx=0.001ï¼‰ã€‚å¦‚æ­¤å¤„ç†æ—¶ï¼Œä½ å†™å‡ºçš„ç­‰å¼ä¼šæœ‰è¯¯å·®ï¼Œä½†éšç€dxè¶‹è¿‘0è¯¯å·®å‡å°ã€‚å›žæƒ³æœ¬ç« æ—©å…ˆçš„çŽ¯ï¼šè‹¥è§†drä¸ºå®žé™…æ•°ï¼ˆå¦‚0.001ï¼‰ï¼Œæ— çŽ¯é¢ç§¯ç²¾ç¡®ç­‰äºŽ2Ï€rdrï¼Œä½†çœŸå®žé¢ç§¯ä¸Ž2Ï€rÂ·drçš„ç™¾åˆ†æ¯”è¯¯å·®éšdrå‡å°è€Œå‡å°‘ã€‚  \n\n# There are more details and rules of thumb for how you replace terms like df and dx with small numbers, and we\'ll address them as the series unfolds, but that\'s the main idea.\nå¦‚ä½•ç”¨å°æ•°æ›¿ä»£dfã€dxç­‰é¡¹æœ‰æ›´å¤šç»†åˆ™ä¸Žç»éªŒæ³•åˆ™ï¼Œå°†åœ¨åŽç»­ç¯‡ç« é˜è¿°ï¼Œä½†æ ¸å¿ƒç†å¿µå·²åœ¨æ­¤ã€‚  \n\n# I mention all this now so that you know this is not the universal convention; again finite changes are more typically written with the greek letter Î”, as in an expression like Î”x=0.001. I also want to assure you that the spirit of this notational philosophy is not to compromise rigor for intuition, but to do the exact opposite.\næåŠè¿™äº›æ„åœ¨è¯´æ˜Žï¼šæ­¤éžé€šç”¨æƒ¯ä¾‹â€”â€”æœ‰é™å˜åŒ–é€šå¸¸å†™ä½œÎ”x=0.001ã€‚ä½†ç¡®ä¿ä½ ç†è§£ï¼šæ­¤è®°æ³•å“²å­¦çš„ç²¾ç¥žå¹¶éžä¸ºç›´è§‰ç‰ºç‰²ä¸¥è°¨ï¼Œåæ˜¯è¿½æ±‚ä¸¥è°¨ã€‚  \n\n# And now, without further philosophical delay, let\'s dive into what exactly a derivative is.\nçŽ°åœ¨æ”¾ä¸‹å“²å­¦è®¨è®ºï¼Œè®©æˆ‘ä»¬æ·±å…¥è§£æžä½•ä¸ºå¯¼æ•°ã€‚','PLAIN_TEXT','PLAIN_TEXT','PLAIN_TEXT','PLAIN_TEXT','PLAIN_TEXT'),(532,1008,'32892347','My goal is for you to come away from this series feeling like you could have invented calculus. That is, cover these core ideas, but in a way that makes clear where they actually come from, and what they really mean, using an all-around visual approach.\nInventing math is no joke, and there is a difference between being told why something makes sense and actually generating it from scratch. But at all points I want you to think to yourself if you were an early mathematician pondering these ideas and drawing the right diagrams, does it feel reasonable that you could have stumbled upon these truths yourself?\n\nIn this chapter, I want to show how you might stumble into the core ideas of calculus by thinking deeply about one very specific bit of geometry: The area of a circle. Maybe you know that the area is Ï€ multiplied by the radius R squared, but why? Is there a way to think about where this formula comes from?\n\nThis figure illustrates the area of a circle with radius R and the formula that calculates its area.\n\nContemplating this problem and leaving yourself open to generalizing the tools you use along the way can actually lead you to a glimpse of three big ideas in calculus: Integrals, derivatives, and the fact that they are opposites.\n\nBut the story of finding the area starts more simply: Just you, and a circle. To be concrete, let\'s say the radius is 1 unit. If you weren\'t worried about the exact area, and just wanted to estimate it, one way you might go about it is to chop the circle into smaller pieces whose areas are easier to approximate, then add up the results.\n\nThere are many ways you might go about this, each of which may lead to its own interesting line of reasoning.\n\nThis figure illustrates different ways to divide the area of the circle with a radius 1.','','TEXT','My goal is for you to finish these lessons feeling you could have created calculus yourself. That means teaching key ideas but showing their true origins and meanings using clear visuals.  \n\nCreating math isn\'t easy. Thereâ€™s a difference between learning why something works and building it from nothing. But as you read, ask: If I were an early mathematician studying these ideas and drawing helpful diagrams, could I have found these truths myself?  \n\nIn this part, Iâ€™ll show how exploring one geometry problemâ€”the area of a circleâ€”could lead you to calculusâ€™ core ideas. You may know the area formula (Ï€ multiplied by radius R squared), but why? Can we discover where this formula comes from?  \n\nThis image shows a circle with radius R and the area formula.  \n\nThinking about this problem, and being open to expanding the methods you use, might help you understand three big calculus concepts: integrals, derivatives, and how they connect as opposites.  \n\nTo begin, imagine trying to find a circleâ€™s area. Letâ€™s say the radius is 1 unit. If you donâ€™t need the exact area, you can estimate it. One way is to cut the circle into smaller, easier-to-measure pieces and add their areas.  \n\nMany methods exist for dividing the circle, each sparking different paths of discovery.  \n\nThis image shows different ways to divide the area of a circle with radius 1.','My objective is for readers to conclude this series with the conviction that they could have conceived calculus themselves. That is, to elucidate fundamental concepts by clarifying their origins and true significance through comprehensive visual methodologies.  \n\nFormulating mathematical principles is no trivial endeavor; comprehending existing proofs differs fundamentally from constructing theories *ab initio*. Nevertheless, I aim to consistently provoke introspection: Had you been an early mathematician contemplating these notions while sketching pertinent diagrams, would discovering these principles independently seem plausible?  \n\nThis chapter demonstrates how examining a singular geometric premiseâ€”the area of a circleâ€”might naturally unveil calculusâ€™ cornerstone concepts. Conventionally, the area equals Ï€ multiplied by the squared radius (RÂ²), but what underlies this formula? Can its derivation be intuitively grasped?  \n\nThe accompanying illustration depicts a circle of radius R alongside its area formula.  \n\nInterrogating this problem while remaining receptive to generalizing methodological tools may unveil three fundamental calculus concepts: integrals, derivatives, and their inverse relationship.  \n\nOur exploration commences modestly: merely you and a circle. Assuming a unit radius for simplicity, one might estimate its area by partitioning the circle into manageable segments, approximating each fragmentâ€™s area, then aggregating these valuesâ€”eschewing precision for conceptual clarity.  \n\nDiverse partitioning strategies exist, each potentially fostering distinct logical pathways.  \n\nThe subsequent figure exemplifies varied approaches to segmenting the unit circleâ€™s area.','The pedagogical objective of this series is to facilitate an epistemological foundation wherein learners might plausibly reconstruct the intellectual genesis of calculus. This entails elucidating fundamental concepts while explicitly articulating their ontological provenance and semantic significance through comprehensive visual methodologies. Mathematical discovery constitutes a significant intellectual endeavor, and whereas apprehending the logical coherence of theorems differs fundamentally from deriving them ab initio, the instructional approach herein consistently invites reflective inquiry: would a historically-situated mathematician, contemplating these principles through appropriate diagrammatic representations, find their autonomous discovery intellectually accessible?\n\nThe present chapter examines how core calculus principles might emerge organically from sustained engagement with a specific geometric problem: circular area determination. Conventional knowledge posits the area as Ï€rÂ², yet this investigation probes beyond rote memorization to foundational reasoning. The accompanying geometric schematic illustrates this classical problem, featuring a circle of radius r with associated area formula.\n\nThrough methodological contemplation of this problem while maintaining openness to conceptual generalization, one may derive insights into three principal calculus constructs: integral summation, differential calculus, and their fundamental reciprocity as formalized in the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus. Initial investigation, however, commences with elementary geometric analysis. Consider a unit circle (r=1) as our demonstrative paradigm. An estimative approach to area quantification involves partitioning the figure into elementary regions amenable to individual approximation, subsequently aggregating these constituent areas.\n\nDivergent decomposition strategies present themselves, each potentially yielding distinctive analytical pathways and mathematical insights, as visually represented in the accompanying multivariate partitioning schematic. Such methodological diversity underscores the heuristic fertility inherent in this fundamental geometric inquiry.',NULL,'','MEDIUM',0,NULL,'',0,0,1,'APPROVED',1,'2026-02-05 16:39:30',NULL,'My goal is for you to come away from this series feeling like you could have invented calculus. That is, cover these core ideas, but in a way that makes clear where they actually come from, and what they really mean, using an all-around visual approach.\n\næˆ‘çš„ç›®æ ‡æ˜¯ï¼Œé€šè¿‡è¿™ä¸ªç³»åˆ—è®©ä½ è§‰å¾—ä»¿ä½›æ˜¯è‡ªå·±å‘æ˜Žäº†å¾®ç§¯åˆ†ã€‚ä¹Ÿå°±æ˜¯è¯´ï¼Œæˆ‘ä»¬ä¼šæ¶µç›–è¿™äº›æ ¸å¿ƒæ€æƒ³ï¼Œä½†é‡‡ç”¨ä¸€ç§å…¨é¢å¯è§†åŒ–çš„æ–¹å¼ï¼Œæ¸…æ™°å±•ç¤ºå®ƒä»¬çš„çœŸå®žæ¥æºå’Œæœ¬è´¨å«ä¹‰ã€‚\n\nInventing math is no joke, and there is a difference between being told why something makes sense and actually generating it from scratch. But at all points I want you to think to yourself if you were an early mathematician pondering these ideas and drawing the right diagrams, does it feel reasonable that you could have stumbled upon these truths yourself?\n\nå‘æ˜Žæ•°å­¦ç»éžå„¿æˆï¼Œ\"ç†è§£æŸä¸ªç†è®ºçš„åˆç†æ€§\"ä¸Ž\"ä»Žé›¶å»ºæž„å®ƒ\"å®Œå…¨ä¸åŒã€‚ä½†è‡ªå§‹è‡³ç»ˆï¼Œæˆ‘å¸Œæœ›ä½ è®¾æƒ³è‡ªå·±æ˜¯ä¸€ä½æ—©æœŸæ•°å­¦å®¶ï¼šå½“ä½ æ²‰æ€è¿™äº›æ€æƒ³å¹¶ç»˜åˆ¶æ­£ç¡®å›¾è¡¨æ—¶ï¼Œä½ æ˜¯å¦ä¼šè§‰å¾—è‡ªå·±ä¹Ÿæœ‰å¯èƒ½å¶ç„¶å‘çŽ°è¿™äº›çœŸç†ï¼Ÿ\n\nIn this chapter, I want to show how you might stumble into the core ideas of calculus by thinking deeply about one very specific bit of geometry: The area of a circle. Maybe you know that the area is Ï€ multiplied by the radius R squared, but why? Is there a way to think about where this formula comes from?\n\næœ¬ç« å°†é€šè¿‡æ·±å…¥æŽ¢è®¨ä¸€ä¸ªå…·ä½“çš„å‡ ä½•é—®é¢˜â€”â€”åœ†çš„é¢ç§¯ï¼Œå±•ç¤ºä½ å¦‚ä½•èƒ½è‡ªç„¶åœ°è§¦åŠå¾®ç§¯åˆ†çš„æ ¸å¿ƒæ€æƒ³ã€‚æˆ–è®¸ä½ çŸ¥é“åœ†é¢ç§¯å…¬å¼æ˜¯Ï€ä¹˜ä»¥åŠå¾„Rçš„å¹³æ–¹ï¼Œä½†ä¸ºä»€ä¹ˆï¼Ÿæ˜¯å¦å­˜åœ¨ç†è§£è¿™ä¸ªå…¬å¼æ¥æºçš„æ€è€ƒæ–¹å¼ï¼Ÿ\n\nThis figure illustrates the area of a circle with radius R and the formula that calculates its area.\n\næ­¤å›¾å±•ç¤ºäº†åŠå¾„ä¸ºRçš„åœ†çš„é¢ç§¯åŠå…¶è®¡ç®—å…¬å¼ã€‚\n\nContemplating this problem and leaving yourself open to generalizing the tools you use along the way can actually lead you to a glimpse of three big ideas in calculus: Integrals, derivatives, and the fact that they are opposites.\n\né€šè¿‡æ€è€ƒè¿™ä¸ªé—®é¢˜å¹¶ä¿æŒå¼€æ”¾å¿ƒæ€åŽ»æŽ¨å¹¿æ‰€ä½¿ç”¨çš„å·¥å…·ï¼Œä½ å°†èƒ½çª¥è§å¾®ç§¯åˆ†çš„ä¸‰å¤§æ ¸å¿ƒç†å¿µï¼šç§¯åˆ†ã€å¯¼æ•°ä»¥åŠå®ƒä»¬äº’ä¸ºé€†è¿ç®—çš„æœ¬è´¨ã€‚\n\nBut the story of finding the area starts more simply: Just you, and a circle. To be concrete, let\'s say the radius is 1 unit. If you weren\'t worried about the exact area, and just wanted to estimate it, one way you might go about it is to chop the circle into smaller pieces whose areas are easier to approximate, then add up the results.\n\nä½†æŽ¢å¯»é¢ç§¯çš„æ•…äº‹å§‹äºŽæ›´ç®€å•çš„èµ·ç‚¹ï¼šåªéœ€è¦ä½ å’Œä¸€å¼ åœ†ã€‚å…·ä½“æ¥è¯´ï¼Œå‡è®¾åŠå¾„é•¿åº¦ä¸º1å•ä½ã€‚è‹¥ä½ ä¸éœ€è¦ç²¾ç¡®é¢ç§¯è€Œåªéœ€ä¼°ç®—ï¼Œå¯ä»¥å°†åœ†åˆ‡å‰²æˆä¾¿äºŽä¼°ç®—çš„å°å—ï¼Œå†å°†ç»“æžœç´¯åŠ ã€‚\n\nThere are many ways you might go about this, each of which may lead to its own interesting line of reasoning.\n\nå®žçŽ°è¿™ä¸€ç›®æ ‡æœ‰å¤šç§æ–¹å¼ï¼Œæ¯ç§æ–¹å¼éƒ½å¯èƒ½å¼•å‘å‡ºç‹¬ç‰¹çš„æŽ¨ç†è·¯å¾„ã€‚\n\nThis figure illustrates different ways to divide the area of the circle with a radius 1.\n\nè¯¥å›¾å±•ç¤ºäº†åŠå¾„ä¸º1çš„åœ†çš„é¢ç§¯çš„ä¸åŒåˆ’åˆ†æ–¹æ³•ã€‚',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'PLAIN_TEXT','PLAIN_TEXT','PLAIN_TEXT','PLAIN_TEXT','PLAIN_TEXT'),(533,1008,'The Essence of Calculus','','\"The art of doing mathematics is finding that special case that contains all the germs of generality.\"-David Hilbert\nMy goal is for you to come away from this series feeling like you could have invented calculus. That is, cover these core ideas, but in a way that makes clear where they actually come from, and what they really mean, using an all-around visual approach.\nInventing math is no joke, and there is a difference between being told why something makes sense and actually generating it from scratch. But at all points I want you to think to yourself if you were an early mathematician pondering these ideas and drawing the right diagrams, does it feel reasonable that you could have stumbled upon these truths yourself?\nQuestions of area\nIn this chapter, I want to show how you might stumble into the core ideas of calculus by thinking deeply about one very specific bit of geometry: The area of a circle. Maybe you know that the area is Ï€ multiplied by the radius R squared, but why? Is there a way to think about where this formula comes from?\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_2.svg](/api/media/17/download)\n\n\nThis figure illustrates the area of a circle with radius R and the formula that calculates its area.\nContemplating this problem and leaving yourself open to generalizing the tools you use along the way can actually lead you to a glimpse of three big ideas in calculus: Integrals, derivatives, and the fact that they are opposites.\nBut the story of finding the area starts more simply: Just you, and a circle. To be concrete, let\'s say the radius is 1 unit. If you weren\'t worried about the exact area, and just wanted to estimate it, one way you might go about it is to chop the circle into smaller pieces whose areas are easier to approximate, then add up the results.\nThere are many ways you might go about this, each of which may lead to its own interesting line of reasoning.\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_3.svg](/api/media/18/download)\n\n\nThis figure illustrates different ways to divide the area of the circle with a radius 1.\nMath has a tendency to reward you when you respect symmetry, so among the divisions shown above, either the bottom left one dividing into pizza slices or the bottom right which divides into concentric rings will be likely to lead us down a fruitful path. For now, let\'s think about the division into concentric rings.\nUnraveling rings\nFocus on just one of those concentric rings, and let\'s call its radius r, which will be some number between 0 and 1. If we can find a nice expression for the area of one such ring, and if we find a nice way to add them all up, it might lead us to an understanding of the full circle\'s area.\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_4.svg](/api/media/19/download)\n\n\nThis figure highlights a band of area formed from dividing the circle into concentric rings.\nIn the spirit of using what will come to be standard calculus notations, let\'s call the thickness of one of these rings â€œdrâ€, which you can read as meaning â€œthe tiny difference in radius from one ring to the nextâ€. In the drawing above, for example, dr = 0.1.\nYou should know that some people, perhaps even most people, would object to our using the notation dr to represent a specific not-infinitely-small size like this, suggesting instead we use some other notation, like Î”r. Well, it\'s my house and my rules, and I have my reasons, which we\'ll get into later. For now, I want you to think about dr as simply being some number without worrying about what phrases like â€œinfinitely smallâ€ would mean.\nWhich of the following is closest to the area of one of these rings with inner radius r and thickness dr?\nÏ€rÂ²â‹…dr\n2Ï€rÂ²â‹…dr\n2Ï€râ‹…dr\nÏ€râ‹…dr\nYou might imagine straightening out this ring, into a shape whose width is the inner circumference of the ring, which is 2Ï€r. This is the very definition of Ï€, relating the circumference of a circle to its diameter, or radius. The thickness of this unwrapped shape would be dr.\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_5.svg](/api/media/20/download)\n\n\nThis figure illustrates a concentric ring with radius r straightened out.\nIf this unwrapped shape is meant to perfectly match the area of the ring, it will be approximately, but not exactly, a rectangle. Because the outer circumference of the ring will be slightly larger than the inner circumference, the bottom of our unwrapped shape will be slightly wider than the top. However, if we\'re comfortable beginning the exploration by only approximating the area of each small piece, we could consider this to be approximately a rectangle with a width of 2Ï€r and a height of dr.\nThe difference between the true area of the ring and the area of the rectangular approximation introduces some small error.\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_6.svg](/api/media/21/download)\n\nThis figure illustrates how approximating a ring\'s area as a rectangle gets better and better for smaller choices of dr.\nHowever, and this will be a key idea,this error becomes tiny compared to the overall area when dr is small. In other words, approximating the area of each ring as 2Ï€râ‹…dr is wrong, but as we chop up the circle into finer and finer rings, it becomes less and less wrong.\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_7.svg](/api/media/22/download)\n\nThis figure illustrates small and smaller choices of dr when dividing the circle into rings.\nThink to Graph\nSo you\'ve broken up the area of this circle into all these rings, and you\'re approximating the area of each one as 2Ï€â‹…râ‹…dr. You might think that actually adding all those areas together will be a nightmare, especially if you\'re seeking more accurate approximations with finer and finer divisions of the circle. However, being the bold mathematician that you are, you might have a hunch that taking this process to the utmost extreme may actually make things easier rather than harder.\nThe size of that inner radius for the rings ranges from 0 for the smallest ring up to 1 for the largest, spaced out by whatever thickness we chose for dr, like 0.1. Or rather, they range from 0 up to 1âˆ’dr, but as dr gets smaller that upper bound looks more and more like 1.\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_8.svg](/api/media/23/download)\n\nThis figure shows how the area of the circle is equal to the sum of rings\' areas, where the area of each ring is given by 2Ï€â‹…râ‹…dr.\nTo help draw what adding those areas looks like, think about adding together your rectangular approximations. A nice way to visualize this is to fit all those rectangles upright, side-by-side standing on a horizontal axis. We can think of this horizontal axis as representing all the values of r ranging from 0 up to 1.\nEach rectangle has a thickness of dr, which is also the spacing between different values of r, which is why they fit so snugly right together. The height of any rectangle above a value of r, is exactly 2Ï€â‹…r. This is the circumference of the corresponding ring.\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_9.svg](/api/media/24/download)\n\nThis figure plots the radius of each of the circle\'s rings on a numberline, where the spacing between each radius is equal to dr.\nWhat is the height of the rectangle whose left corner touches r=0.3?\nNote: This is the rectangle highlighted in the still figure above.\n6Ï€\n3/10 Ï€\n2Ï€\n3/5 Ï€\nA nice way to think of this setup is to draw the graph of 2Ï€r, which is this straight line with a slope of 2Ï€. Each of these rectangles extends up to the point where it just barely touches the graph.\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_10.svg](/api/media/25/download)\n\nThe unraveled rings under the graph of 2Ï€r.\nAgain, we\'re being approximate here. Each of those rectangles only approximates the area of their corresponding ring from the circle. But remember, the approximation of 2Ï€â‹…râ‹…dr for the area of each ring will get less and less wrong as the size of dr gets smaller and smaller.\nAgain, concretely adding up the areas of all these rectangles would be a royal pain, but that\'s when you get a crazy thought: Maybe, just maybe, asking what this sum approaches as the choice of dr gets smaller will be easier than ever actually computing the sum for any specific value of dr.\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_11.svg](/api/media/26/download)\n\nThe unraveled rings under the graph of 2Ï€r.\nThis has a very beautiful meaning when looking at the sum of the areas of all these rectangles. For smaller and smaller choices of dr, notice how all their area in aggregate simply looks like the area under this graph. The smaller this value dr is, the closer that aggregate area of the rectangles is to being precisely the area under the graph.\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_12.svg](/api/media/27/download)\n\n\nThis portion under the graph is a triangle, whose base is 1, and whose height is 2Ï€â‹…1. So the area, which is Â½ base times height, comes out to be Ï€â‹…1Â².\nOr, if the radius of our original circle had been some other value, capital R, that area would be 1/2 â‹… R â‹… 2Ï€R.\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_13.svg](/api/media/28/download)\n\nThis is the formula for the area of a circle. It doesn\'t matter who you are or what you typically think of math; that right there is beautiful.\nGeneralizing the Approach\nBeing the mathematician that you are, you don\'t just care about finding the answer, you care about developing general problem-solving tools and techniques. So take a moment to meditate on what just happened, and why that worked, because the way we transitioned from something approximate to something precise is actually pretty subtle, and cuts deep to what calculus is all about.\nYou had a problem that could be approximated with the sum of many small numbers, each of which looked like 2Ï€â‹…râ‹…dr for values of r ranging between 0 and 1. Each of those approximates the area of one of these thin rings.\nThe small number dr represents our choice for the thickness of each ring, for example 0.1, and there are two important things to note. First, not only is dr a factor in the quantity we\'re adding up, 2Ï€â‹…râ‹…dr, it also gives the spacing between the different values of r. The smaller the choice of dr, the better the approximation.\nAdding all those numbers could be seen in a different clever way as adding up the areas of many thin rectangles sitting under a graph; in this example that graph was of the function 2Ï€â‹…r.\nSo on the one hand the sum of the areas of these slices approaches the area of the circle for smaller and smaller choices of dr. But on the other hand, that sum also approaches the area under this graph. This is how we concluded that our original hard problem had an answer equal to the area under a certain graph; not just approximately equal, but precisely equal. I\'ll emphasize again what a big theme in calculus this is: The purpose of approximating a question by subdividing it like this is not that we don\'t care about precision, it\'s that the approximation using many smaller pieces gives us the flexibility to reframe our original hard question into something simpler.\n\n\nIntegrals\nA lot of other hard problems in math and science can be broken down and approximated as the sum of many small quantities. For example if you want to figure out how far a car has gone based on its velocity at each point in time, you might range through many many points in time, and multiply the velocity at each time t by some tiny change in time dt to get the corresponding bit of distance traveled in the little time.\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_15.png](/api/media/29/download)\n\n\nI\'ll talk through the details of examples like this later in the series, but at a high level, many of these types of problems turn out to be equivalent to finding the area under some graph, in much the same way that our circle problem did.\nThis happens whenever the quantities that you\'re adding up, the ones whose sum approximates your original problem, can be thought of as the area of many thin rectangles sitting side-by-side like this. If finer and finer approximations of your original problem correspond to thinner and thinner rectangles, the original problem will be equivalent to finding the area under some graph.\nIn the same way velocity measures the change in position over the change in time, acceleration measures the change in velocity over the change in time. Can the velocity of a car be modeled as the area under the graph of acceleration?\nYes\nNo\nAgain, notice that the purpose of the small approximations is not that we intend to use them directly, per se, but that the two separate ways to think about what these approximations approach lets us reframe the question of how far the car has traveled into the question of finding the area under a certain curve.\nWe\'ll see this idea in more detail later in the series, so don\'t worry if it\'s not 100% clear right now. The point is that you, as the mathematician having just solved a problem by reframing it as an area under a graph, might start thinking about how to find the area under other graphs.\nWe were lucky in our circle problem that the relevant area turned out to be a triangle, but imagine instead something like a parabola, the graph of the function xÂ². What\'s the area under this curve, say between the values of x=0 and x=3? It\'s hard to think about, isn\'t it?\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_16.png](/api/media/30/download)\n\n\nLet me frame that question a different way: Fix that left endpoint in place at 0, and let that right endpoint vary: Can you find a function, A(x), that gives you the area under this parabola between 0 and x?\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_17.png](/api/media/31/download)\n\nIn calculus, you might call this function A(x) an â€œintegralâ€ of xÂ². Well, more precisely we\'d say this is the integral of xÂ² from 0 up to x. Or rather, to disambiguate whether x represents the variable for the function, or if it represents the right endpoint, it would be even better to describe this area as the integral of the function f(t)=tÂ² between the values 0 and x. In the lingo, you\'d see it written like this:\nBut here we\'re getting ahead of ourselves. All that matters now is that you, as the mathematician, find yourself wondering about this mystery function A(x) which gives the area under the graph of xÂ² between some fixed left point, and some variable right point. If you can find a way to compute this explicitly, you will be inventing a big part of calculus.\nAgain, the reason we care about this kind of question is not just for the sake of asking hard geometry questions; it\'s because many practical problems that can be approximated by adding up a large number of small things can be reframed as a question about the area under a certain curve.\nDerivatives\nFinding the area represented by this integral function is genuinely hard. And whenever you come across a genuinely hard question in math, a good policy is to not try too hard to get to the answer directly, since you often just end up banging your head against a wall.\nInstead, play around with this idea. Build up some familiarity with the interplay between the function defining a graph, in this case xÂ², and the function giving the area, our unknown function A(x), and see if there are any other questions you can ask about the setup.\nIn that playful spirit, if you\'re lucky, here\'s something you might notice. When you slightly increase x by some tiny nudge, dx, look at the resulting change in area, represented by this sliver which I\'m going to call dA, for a tiny difference in area.\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_18.png](/api/media/32/download)\n\nThat sliver can be pretty well approximated with a rectangle, one whose height is xÂ² and whose width is dx. Well, for larger choices of dx the approximation may not be that good, but for smaller and smaller choices of dx the error between the area of that sliver and the area of the approximating rectangle will become tiny compared to the area of the rectangle.\nThis may prompt you to think about how A(x) is related to xÂ² in a pretty fun way. A change to the output of A, this little dA, is about equal to the height of the rectangle times its width.\nHere, x is the input where you started and dx is the size of the little nudge the input that caused A to change.\nWe could also rearrange that slightly:\nThis says the ratio of a tiny change in A to the tiny change in x that caused it equals the height of our graph, xÂ², at that point. Or rather, this is only approximately true, but it\'s an approximation that should get better and better for smaller choices of dx.\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_19.png](/api/media/33/download)\n\nFor example, think about two nearby inputs, like 3 and 3.001. The change to x, then, is dx=0.001. The change dA would be the difference between the mystery function evaluated at 3.001 and evaluated at 3. Even though we don\'t know what that mystery function is, we do know something about this change, namely that this change divided by dx=0.001 is approximately 3Â².\nAnd this relationship between tiny changes to the mystery function and the value of xÂ² is true at all inputs, not just 3. For example, we can see the same relationship at the point x=2 on the graph of xÂ².\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_20.png](/api/media/34/download)\n\nThat doesn\'t immediately tell us how to find A(x), but it provides a very strong clue to work with. And there\'s nothing special about the graph xÂ² here. For any function f(x), if we call the area under its graph A(x), then this area function has the property that dA/dxâ‰ˆf(x), a slight nudge to the output of A divided by a slight nudge to the input that caused it is about equal to the height of the graph at that point, f(x). Again, that\'s an approximation that gets better and better for smaller choices of dx.\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_21.png](/api/media/35/download)\n\nThis figure illustrates the property of dA/dx for a more general function f(x).\nHere we\'re stumbling onto another big idea from calculus: Derivatives. This ratio dA/dx is called the â€œderivative of Aâ€. Or, more technically, the derivative is whatever value this ratio approaches as dx gets smaller and smaller.\nYou and I will dive more deeply into the idea of a derivative next chapter, but loosely speaking it\'s a measure of how sensitive a function is to small changes in its input. You\'ll see as the series goes on that there are many different ways to visualize a derivative, depending on what function you\'re looking at and how you think about tiny nudges to its output.\nFundamental Theorem\nWe care about derivatives because they help us solve problems, and in our little exploration here we have a slight glimpse of one way they\'re used: They are the key to solving integral problems; problems that require finding an area under a curve. When you gain enough familiarity with computing derivatives, you\'ll be able to look at a situation like the one below, where you don\'t know what a function is, but you do know that its derivative should be xÂ², and from that reverse engineer what the function must be.\nThis back and forth between integrals and derivatives, where the derivative of the function for an area under a graph gives you back the function defining the graph itself, is called the fundamental theorem of calculus. It ties together the two big ideas of integrals and derivatives, and shows how in some sense, each one is the inverse of the other.\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_22.png](/api/media/36/download)\n\nThis figure illustrates the property of dA/dx for a more general function f(x).\nAll of this is only a high level view. What follows in this series are the details for both these big ideas, and more. And let me reiterate, at all points in this series I want you to feel like you could have invented calculus yourself, that if you drew the right pictures and played with each idea in the right way, all of the formulas, rules, and constructs could pop out quite naturally.\nBonus section: So what is dx anyway?\nCalculus is littered with expressions like dA, dr, dx, etc, which show up in the notation for both derivatives and integrals. Despite their front-and-center role, there is a surprising amount of ambiguity and conflicting instruction on what these terms really mean.\nIn this chapter I encouraged you to think of dr as the difference in the radius of our circles from one to the next, prompting you to take a very literal interpretation and imagine an actual number, like dr=0.01. Likewise, I encouraged you to think of dA as an actual number, specifically the amount that the area under a graph changes as we nudge the right endpoint by dx, again an actual number. A more common convention would be to call these changes Î”r, Î”A, and Î”x, reserving the dx notation for changes that are â€œinfinitely smallâ€.\nInfinitely small?\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_23.svg](/api/media/37/download)\n\n\nThe issue with this is that phrases like â€œinfinitely smallâ€ and â€œinfinitesimalâ€ run the risk of, well, not actually making any sense. What is an infinitesimal change? Is it a number? If so, which number? It shouldn\'t be zero, but it also shouldn\'t be anything bigger than zero. If it\'s not a number then what is it, exactly?\nIn acknowledgment of this conundrum, one common sentiment is to encourage students not to take this notation too seriously. The typical dictum is that derivatives, despite being written as df/dx, are not really fractions, and that integrals, written as âˆ«f(x)dx, are not really sums, despite the fact that the symbol âˆ« literally derives from the letter \"S\" for \"Sum\". Instead, the student is told, each of these constructs has a separate more complicated definition. But! If a student wishes for loose intuition before stepping up their game to a real analysis class, they might find it helpful to think of dx as an infinitely small change in x, even if that phrase doesn\'t quite make sense. It is, after all, only intuition.\nTo my taste, this sentiment is well-intentioned but ultimately misleading. The rigorous underpinnings of calculus only ever involve real, finitely sized numbers, often followed by questions of what happens as these values get closer to 0. The proposal of this series is that when we interpret the notation of calculus, we can do so in a way which maps closely to this rigorous underpinning. If you\'re anything like I am, this actually makes calculus much more tangible and understandable than any â€œintuitionâ€ resting on the idea of an infinitesimal.\nIn a nutshell, here\'s the philosophy for this series: When you see expressions with terms like dx, think of those terms as representing actual small numbers, say dx=0.001. When you do this, the equations you write will become wrong, but they get less wrong as the choice for dx approaches 0. Think of the circular rings from earlier in this chapter. If you thought of dr as an actual number, say dr=0.001, none of these rings actually had an area of 2Ï€rdr. However, the percent error between the true area and 2Ï€râ‹…dr gets smaller and smaller as the choice for dr does as well.\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_24.svg](/api/media/38/download)\n\nThere are more details and rules of thumb for how you replace terms like df and dx with small numbers, and we\'ll address them as the series unfolds, but that\'s the main idea.\nI mention all this now so that you know this is not the universal convention; again finite changes are more typically written with the greek letter Î”, as in an expression like Î”x=0.001. I also want to assure you that the spirit of this notational philosophy is not to compromise rigor for intuition, but to do the exact opposite.\nAnd now, without further philosophical delay, let\'s dive into what exactly a derivative is.\nThanks\nSpecial thanks to those below for supporting the original video behind this post, and to current patrons for funding ongoing projects. If you find these lessons valuable, consider joining.','MARKDOWN','\n\nPlease provide the original English article you would like rewritten, and I will return a simplified version matching the CET-4 difficulty level as requested.','The original article text you provided appears to be missing. Please share the full content of the article you\'d like rewritten to CET-6 level, and I\'ll thoughtfully adjust it using appropriate vocabulary and grammatical structures while preserving the original meaning.','**The Impact of Digital Transformation on Organizational Competitiveness in Contemporary Business Environments**  \nThe rapid proliferation of digital technologies has precipitated a paradigmatic shift in global business operations, compelling organizations to reconfigure operational frameworks to maintain competitive advantage. Contemporary scholarly discourse emphasizes that digital transformation transcends mere technological adoption, constituting a holistic_realignment_of organizational strategies, operational processes, and value-creation mechanisms.  \n\nEmpirical studies_(e.g.,_ Bharadwaj et al., 2013; Vial, 2019)_demonstrate_that enterprises exhibiting strategic digital maturity achieve superior supply chain resilience, enhanced customer engagement through data-driven personalization, and augmented operational efficiency via automation. Conversely, entities lagging in digital integration face systemic risks, including market irrelevance and diminished capacity for innovation. The assimilation of artificial intelligence, blockchain, and advanced analytics has further intensified competitive differentiation, enabling predictive decision-making and hyper-personalized service delivery.  \n\nHowever, significant challenges impede seamless digital transitions. Research by Hanelt et al. (2021)_identifies_organizational inertia, cybersecurity vulnerabilities, and talent deficits in digital literacy as critical barriers. Moreover, the ethical implications of algorithmic governance and data monopolization necessitate rigorous regulatory scrutiny to ensure equitable market participation.  \n\nLongitudinal analyses suggest that sustained competitiveness necessitates continuous investment in digital infrastructure, cross-functional workforce upskilling, and agile governance models. As digital ecosystems evolve, organizational success will increasingly hinge on the ability to leverage emergent technologies while cultivating adaptive, ethically grounded business practices._This underscores_the imperative for interdisciplinary research bridging management theory, technological innovation, and socio-economic policy frameworks.',NULL,'','MEDIUM',0,NULL,'',0,0,1,'APPROVED',0,'2026-02-06 14:24:38','2026-02-06 15:03:03','We are all living in the digital age, where technology continues to shape our daily experiences and interactions. From communication to commerce, digital innovations have fundamentally altered traditional ways of operating. This transformation brings both remarkable conveniences and complex challenges that demand thoughtful navigation.\n\næˆ‘ä»¬éƒ½ç”Ÿæ´»åœ¨æ•°å­—æ—¶ä»£ï¼Œç§‘æŠ€ä¸æ–­é‡å¡‘ç€æˆ‘ä»¬çš„æ—¥å¸¸ä½“éªŒä¸Žäº’åŠ¨æ–¹å¼ã€‚ä»Žæ²Ÿé€šåˆ°å•†ä¸šæ´»åŠ¨ï¼Œæ•°å­—åˆ›æ–°å·²ä»Žæ ¹æœ¬ä¸Šæ”¹å˜äº†ä¼ ç»Ÿè¿ä½œæ¨¡å¼ã€‚è¿™ç§å˜é©æ—¢å¸¦æ¥äº†éžå‡¡ä¾¿åˆ©ï¼Œä¹Ÿå‚¬ç”Ÿå‡ºéœ€è¦å®¡æ…Žåº”å¯¹çš„å¤æ‚æŒ‘æˆ˜ã€‚\n\nThe pace of technological advancement shows no signs of slowing down, with artificial intelligence and automation becoming increasingly sophisticated. While these developments promise greater efficiency, they also raise important questions about ethical implementation and workforce adaptation. Balancing innovation with human welfare remains a critical global conversation.\n\næŠ€æœ¯è¿›æ­¥çš„èŠ‚å¥æ¯«æ— å‡ç¼“è¿¹è±¡ï¼Œäººå·¥æ™ºèƒ½ä¸Žè‡ªåŠ¨åŒ–æŠ€æœ¯æ—¥ç›Šç²¾è¿›ã€‚å°½ç®¡è¿™äº›å‘å±•æ„å‘³ç€æ›´é«˜æ•ˆçŽ‡ï¼Œä½†ä¹Ÿå¼•å‘äº†æœ‰å…³ä¼¦ç†å®žæ–½å’ŒåŠ³åŠ¨åŠ›é€‚åº”çš„é‡è¦é—®é¢˜ã€‚å¦‚ä½•åœ¨æŠ€æœ¯åˆ›æ–°ä¸Žäººç±»ç¦ç¥‰ä¹‹é—´å–å¾—å¹³è¡¡ï¼Œä¾ç„¶æ˜¯å…¨çƒèŒƒå›´å†…çš„é‡è¦è®®é¢˜ã€‚\n\nIn this rapidly evolving landscape, digital literacy has emerged as an essential life skill for individuals of all ages. Understanding how to responsibly engage with technology, protect personal data, and evaluate online information are becoming prerequisites for full participation in modern society. Educational systems worldwide are consequently adapting their curricula to meet these new demands.\n\nåœ¨è¿™ä¸ªå¿«é€Ÿæ¼”å˜çš„çŽ¯å¢ƒä¸­ï¼Œæ•°å­—ç´ å…»å·²æˆä¸ºæ‰€æœ‰å¹´é¾„æ®µäººç¾¤çš„å¿…å¤‡ç”Ÿæ´»æŠ€èƒ½ã€‚ç†è§£å¦‚ä½•è´Ÿè´£ä»»åœ°ä½¿ç”¨æŠ€æœ¯ã€ä¿æŠ¤ä¸ªäººæ•°æ®ä»¥åŠè¯„ä¼°ç½‘ç»œä¿¡æ¯ï¼Œæ­£é€æ¸æˆä¸ºå…¨é¢å‚ä¸ŽçŽ°ä»£ç¤¾ä¼šçš„å…ˆå†³æ¡ä»¶ã€‚å› æ­¤ï¼Œå…¨çƒæ•™è‚²ä½“ç³»æ­£åœ¨è°ƒæ•´è¯¾ç¨‹è®¾ç½®ä»¥æ»¡è¶³è¿™äº›æ–°éœ€æ±‚ã€‚','\"The art of doing mathematics is finding that special case that contains all the germs of generality.\" - David Hilbert  \nMy goal is for you to finish this series feeling that you could have discovered calculus yourself. That means teaching these main ideas while making clear where they come from and what they really mean, using pictures to explain everything.  \n\nCreating math is not easy, and understanding why something works is different from inventing it yourself. But I want you to ask yourself: If you were an early mathematician studying these ideas and drawing the right pictures, could you have found these truths?  \n\n**Questions about area**  \nIn this chapter, I want to show how you might discover calculus by thinking deeply about one simple geometry problem: the area of a circle. You may know the formula is Ï€ times the radius (R) squared, but why? Is there a way to understand where this formula comes from?  \n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_2.svg](/api/media/17/download)  \n\n*This picture shows a circle with radius R and its area formula.*  \n\nThinking about this problem and generalizing your tools can lead you to three big calculus ideas: integrals, derivatives, and how they are opposites.  \nBut the story starts simply: Just you and a circle. Letâ€™s say the radius is 1 unit. If you want to estimate the area (not find the exact number), one way is to cut the circle into small parts with easy-to-estimate areas, then add them.  \nThere are many ways to do this, each leading to interesting thoughts.  \n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_3.svg](/api/media/18/download)  \n\n*This picture shows different ways to cut a circle with radius 1.*  \n\nMath often rewards those who use symmetry. From the divisions above, the bottom-left (pizza slices) or bottom-right (concentric rings) will be helpful. For now, we will use concentric rings.  \n\n**Opening the rings**  \nFocus on one ring. Letâ€™s call its radius r (a number between 0 and 1). If we find an easy way to write the area of one ring and add all areas neatly, we can understand the full circleâ€™s area.  \n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_4.svg](/api/media/19/download)  \n\n*This picture highlights a single ring formed by cutting the circle.*  \n\nUsing standard calculus terms, letâ€™s call the thickness of a ring *dr* (meaning â€œa small change in radiusâ€). In the drawing, dr = 0.1.  \n(Note: Some people prefer to use Î”r instead of dr for small-but-not-zero sizes. But for now, think of dr as a small number.)  \n\nWhich of these is closest to the area of a ring with inner radius r and thickness dr?  \n- Ï€rÂ²â‹…dr  \n- 2Ï€rÂ²â‹…dr  \n- 2Ï€râ‹…dr  \n- Ï€râ‹…dr  \n\nImagine straightening the ring. Its width becomes the inner circumference (2Ï€r), by the definition of Ï€. The thickness is dr.  \n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_5.svg](/api/media/20/download)  \n\n*This picture shows a straightened ring.*  \n\nThe straightened shape is almost a rectangleâ€”but not exactly. Because the outer circumference is slightly longer than the inner one, the bottom is wider than the top. However, if we accept an approximate answer, we can consider it a rectangle with width 2Ï€r and height dr.  \nThe difference between the real ring area and the rectangle causes some error.  \n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_6.svg](/api/media/21/download)  \n\n*This picture shows how the rectangle approximation improves when dr is smaller.*  \n\nKey idea: This error becomes tiny when dr is small. Approximating each ringâ€™s area as 2Ï€râ‹…dr is wrong, but becomes less wrong as dr shrinks.  \n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_7.svg](/api/media/22/download)  \n\n*This picture shows smaller dr values dividing the circle.*  \n\n**Turning to graphs**  \nYou split the circleâ€™s area into rings and approximate each area as 2Ï€râ‹…dr. Adding all these sounds hardâ€”especially for more accurate small dr. But you might guess that using very small dr could make the math easier.  \nThe ringsâ€™ radii range from 0 (smallest) to 1 (largest), with spacing dr (like 0.1). Or almost to 1â€”as dr shrinks, the upper limit gets closer to 1.  \n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_8.svg](/api/media/23/download)  \n\n*This picture shows the area sum of all rings (each 2Ï€râ‹…dr).*  \n\nTo visualize adding the rectangle areas, stack them side-by-side on a horizontal axis showing r from 0 to 1. Each rectangle has thickness dr (same as spacing between r values) and height 2Ï€r (the ringâ€™s circumference).  \n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_9.svg](/api/media/24/download)  \n\n*This picture plots the ringsâ€™ radii on a number line, spaced by dr.*  \n\nWhat is the height of the rectangle touching r=0.3?  \n(Note: This rectangle is highlighted in the picture above.)  \n- 6Ï€  \n- 3/10 Ï€  \n- 2Ï€  \n- 3/5 Ï€  \n\nA good way to see this is to draw the graph of 2Ï€r (a straight line with slope 2Ï€). Each rectangle touches the graph.  \n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_10.svg](/api/media/25/download)  \n\n*The straightened rings under the 2Ï€r graph.*  \n\nAgain, we are approximating. Each rectangle only roughly matches its ringâ€™s area. But the approximation improves as dr shrinks.  \n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_11.svg](/api/media/26/download)  \n\n*The rings under the 2Ï€r graph.*  \n\nAdding all rectangles equals the total approximate area. As dr approaches 0, this total approaches the area under the graphâ€”which is a triangle with base 1 and height 2Ï€. Area = Â½ Ã— base Ã— height = Ï€ Ã— 1Â².  \nIf the circleâ€™s radius was R, the area would be Â½ Ã— R Ã— 2Ï€R = Ï€RÂ².  \n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_13.svg](/api/media/28/download)  \n\n*This is the circle area formula. Itâ€™s beautiful.*  \n\n**Generalizing the method**  \nAs a mathematician, you want universal tools. So think about what happened:  \n- You approximated a hard problem by adding small numbers (2Ï€râ‹…dr) for r from 0 to 1.  \n- Each *dr* affected both the area pieces and the spacing between r values. Smaller *dr* means better approximation.  \n- Adding the areas is like finding the area under the graph of 2Ï€r.  \nAs *dr* shrinks, the sum of ring areas approaches both the circle area *and* the graph area. This reframes a hard problem as an easier oneâ€”a key calculus theme.  \n\n**Integrals**  \nMany hard problems can be approximated by adding small pieces. For example, measuring a carâ€™s total distance from its speed-time graph: Take many time points, multiply speed at time *t* by small time change *dt*, then add.  \n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_15.png](/api/media/29/download)  \n\nWeâ€™ll see details later, but such problems often equal the area under a graphâ€”just like the circle problem.  \nIf your approximating pieces can be seen as thin rectangle areas, then finer pieces mean solving the original problem equals finding the graph area.  \n\nIn physics, velocity is how position changes over time (*Î”position / Î”time*). Acceleration is how velocity changes over time (*Î”velocity / Î”time*). Can velocity be modeled as the area under an acceleration graph?  \n- Yes  \n- No  \n\nAgain, small approximations help reframe problemsâ€”like distance traveled becoming the area under a speed graph.  \nWe were lucky that the circle problem became a triangle area. But for a parabola (graph of xÂ²), whatâ€™s the area between x=0 and x=3?  \n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_16.png](/api/media/30/download)  \n\nAnother view: Fix the left end at 0, let the right end vary (*x*). Can you find a function A(x) for the parabolaâ€™s area between 0 and x?  \n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_17.png](/api/media/31/download)  \n\nIn calculus, A(x) is called the **integral** of xÂ² from 0 to x. More carefully, if we write f(t)=tÂ², the area is:  \n$$\\int_{0}^{x} t^2 \\, dt$$  \nBut here, you are wondering: What is this mystery function A(x)? Calculating it would mean inventing calculus!  \n\n**Derivatives**  \nFinding integrals is hard. When math is hard, donâ€™t force the answerâ€”play with the ideas. Notice: If you nudge x by a tiny change *dx*, the area change *dA* is a sliver under the graph.  \n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_18.png](/api/media/32/download)  \n\nThis sliver is roughly a rectangle with height xÂ² and width *dx*. The error becomes tiny as *dx* shrinks. So:  \n$$ dA \\approx x^2 \\cdot dx $$  \nor  \n$$ \\frac{dA}{dx} \\approx x^2 $$  \nThis ratio (*dA/dx*) is the height of the graph at that x. It holds for all x, like x=3 or x=2.  \n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_19.png](/api/media/33/download)  \n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_20.png](/api/media/34/download)  \n\nFor any function f(x), if A(x) is the area under its graph, then:  \n$$ \\frac{dA}{dx} \\approx f(x) $$  \nAs *dx* shrinks, the ratio (*dA/dx*) equals the derivative of Aâ€”a measure of how A changes with tiny input changes.  \n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_21.png](/api/media/35/download)  \n\n*This picture shows dA/dx for a general function f(x).*  \n\n**Fundamental Theorem**  \nDerivatives help solve integrals (area problems). Knowing a functionâ€™s derivative is xÂ² helps find the original function. This link between integrals and derivativesâ€”each being the inverse of the otherâ€”is the **fundamental theorem of calculus**.  \n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_22.png](/api/media/36/download)  \n\n*This picture shows the derivative-area relationship.*  \n\nThis is a high-level view. Later, weâ€™ll cover detailsâ€”but remember, you could have invented this!  \n\n**Bonus: What is *dx*?**  \nCalculus uses terms like *dx*, *dA*, *dr* in derivatives and integrals. But what do they mean?  \nIn this chapter, I called *dr* a small radius change (like 0.01). But some say *dx* should mean â€œinfinitely small.â€ However, â€œinfinitely smallâ€ is confusingâ€”is it zero or not?  \n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_23.svg](/api/media/37/download)  \n\nMany teachers say derivatives (written *df/dx*) arenâ€™t fractions, and integrals (written *âˆ«f(x)dx*) arenâ€™t sumsâ€”though âˆ« comes from â€œSum.â€ Instead, think of *dx* as a very small number (like 0.001). The equations are not perfectly accurate, but the error shrinks as *dx* approaches 0.  \n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_24.svg](/api/media/38/download)  \n\nThis approach keeps rigorâ€”real math uses limits as values approach zero, not â€œinfinite smallness.â€ In this series, when you see *dx*, think of small numbers!  \n\nNow, letâ€™s explore what a derivative really is.  \n*Special thanks to supporters of the original video and current patrons. If you find this helpful, consider joining.*','\n\n\"The art of doing mathematics is finding that special case that contains all the germs of generality.\" - David Hilbert\n\nMy goal is for you to finish this series feeling you could have invented calculus yourself. That is, to present key ideas while showing their origins and meanings through visual explanations. Creating math isn\'t easy - understanding why something works differs from deriving it yourself. But throughout, ask: If you were an early mathematician drawing diagrams, could you have discovered these truths?\n\n## Questions of Area\nThis chapter explores how calculus concepts emerge from studying a circle\'s area. We know the formula Ï€ times radius R squared, but why? Let\'s trace its origin.\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_2.svg](/api/media/17/download)\n\n_Illustration: Circle with radius R and area formula_\n\nBy deeply examining this problem, you\'ll encounter three core calculus ideas: integrals, derivatives, and their inverse relationship. We start simply: You and a circle (radius=1 unit). To estimate its area, divide it into manageable pieces and total their approximate areas. Various division methods offer different insights.\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_3.svg](/api/media/18/download)\n\n_Illustration: Different circle division methods_\n\nMathematics rewards symmetry. The bottom-left (pizza slices) or bottom-right (concentric rings) divisions are promising. We\'ll focus on concentric rings.\n\n## Unraveling Rings\nExamine one ring with radius r (between 0 and 1). Finding its area and summing all rings could reveal the circle\'s total area.\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_4.svg](/api/media/19/download)\n\n_Illustration: Highlighted ring in concentric division_\n\nUsing standard calculus notation, let the ring\'s thickness be _dr_ (\"tiny radius difference\"). In our example, _dr_ = 0.1. While some prefer Î”r for finite differences, we\'ll use _dr_ as a concrete number for now.\n\n**Question:** Which best approximates a ring\'s area (inner radius _r_, thickness _dr_)?  \n\\* Ï€rÂ²Â·dr  \n\\* 2Ï€rÂ²Â·dr  \n\\* 2Ï€rÂ·dr  \n\\* Ï€rÂ·dr  \n\nImagine straightening the ring. Its width becomes the inner circumference 2Ï€r (Ï€ links circumference to diameter/radius). Its height becomes _dr_.\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_5.svg](/api/media/20/download)\n\n_Illustration: Unwrapped ring_\n\nThe unwrapped shape approximates a rectangle but isn\'t perfect - the outer circumference exceeds the inner. Starting with approximations is fine; consider a rectangle with width 2Ï€r and height _dr_. The real area differs slightly, but...\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_6.svg](/api/media/21/download)\n\n_Illustration: Approximation improves with smaller dr_\n\n**Crucial point:** This error becomes insignificant when _dr_ is small. Though 2Ï€rÂ·dr isn\'t exact, it becomes nearly correct as rings get thinner.\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_7.svg](/api/media/22/download)\n\n_Illustration: Smaller dr values refine the division_\n\n## Thinking Graphically\nYou\'ve approximated each ring\'s area as 2Ï€rÂ·dr. Summing these seems tedious, but intuition suggests extreme refinement simplifies things. Ring radii range from 0 to 1 (spaced by _dr_).\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_8.svg](/api/media/23/download)\n\n_Illustration: Total area as sum of ring areas_\n\nVisualize stacking rectangles upright along a horizontal axis (r from 0 to 1). Each rectangle\'s width is _dr_ (r-spacing), height 2Ï€r (ring circumference).\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_9.svg](/api/media/24/download)\n\n_Illustration: Radial values plotted with spacing dr_\n\n**Question:** What\'s the height at r=0.3?  \n\\* 6Ï€  \n\\* 3/10 Ï€  \n\\* 2Ï€  \n\\* 3/5 Ï€  \n\nPlotting 2Ï€r gives a straight line (slope 2Ï€). The rectangles nearly touch this line.\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_10.svg](/api/media/25/download)\n\n_Illustration: Rectangles under 2Ï€r graph_\n\nWhile adding areas is hard, consider what happens as _dr_ approaches zero. The aggregate rectangle area increasingly matches the graph\'s area below the curve.\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_11.svg](/api/media/26/download)\n\n_Reiteration: Rectangles approaching the graph area_\n\nWith infinitesimal _dr_, the total area equals the shaded region - a triangle! Base=1, height=2Ï€Ã—1. Area=Â½Ã—1Ã—2Ï€=Ï€. For general radius R: Â½Ã—RÃ—2Ï€R = Ï€RÂ².\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_13.svg](/api/media/28/download)\n\n_Result: The circle area formula Ï€RÂ²_\n\n## Generalizing the Approach\nBeyond finding answers, mathematicians develop problem-solving methods. Let\'s analyze our approach\'s deeper calculus implications:\n\n1. Problem approximated by summing small terms 2Ï€rÂ·dr (r from 0 to 1)  \n2. _dr_ serves dual roles:  \n   - Factor in each term  \n   - Spacing between r-values  \n   - Smaller _dr_ improves accuracy  \n3. Summing terms equals summing rectangle areas under 2Ï€r graph  \n4. Two interpretations converge:  \n   - Circle area = Rectangle sum limit  \n   - Rectangle sum limit = Graph area  \n\nThis dual perspective solves hard problems by reframing them as area calculations. This key calculus theme recurs often.\n\n## Integrals\nMany problems approximate sums of small quantities. Example: Distance traveled = âˆ‘ (velocity Ã— tiny time interval dt).\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_15.png](/api/media/29/download)\n\n_Future example: Distance as velocity-time area_\n\nMany such problems equate to finding graph areas. Similar to our circle problem, finer approximations use thinner rectangles.\n\nAcceleration measures velocity change over time. Is velocity the area under acceleration\'s graph?  \n\\* Yes  \n\\* No  \n\nAgain, small approximations let us reframe distance questions as area-under-curve problems.\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_16.png](/api/media/30/download)\n\n_Challenge: Area under parabola xÂ² between 0 and 3?_\n\nConsider left endpoint fixed at 0. Can we find A(x) = area under xÂ² from 0 to x?\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_17.png](/api/media/31/download)\n\n_Visualizing A(x) for general x_\n\nCalculus terms: A(x) is the \"integral\" of xÂ² from 0 to x, written:  \n$$\nA(x) = \\int_0^x t^2 dt\n$$\n\nFinding A(x) exemplifies calculus\'s powerâ€”many problems reduce to area-under-curve questions.\n\n## Derivatives\nIntegrals are hard. When stuck, explore relationships. Notice: Increasing x by tiny dx adds area slice dA â‰ˆ xÂ²Â·dx.\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_18.png](/api/media/32/download)\n\n_Illustration: dA as area slice when x increases by dx_\n\nFor small dx, dA â‰ˆ xÂ²Â·dx. Rearranging:  \n$$\n\\frac{dA}{dx} \\approx x^2\n$$\n\nThis ratioâ€”tiny output change over input changeâ€”approaches xÂ² as dx shrinks. This is the \"derivative\" of A.  \n\nExample: At x=3, dx=0.001:  \ndA/dx â‰ˆ 3Â² = 9\n\nSimilarly at x=2:\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_20.png](/api/media/34/download)\n\n_Illustration: Derivative relation at x=2_\n\nThis generalizes: For any f(x), if A(x) is the area under its graph, then  \n$$\n\\frac{dA}{dx} \\approx f(x)\n$$\n\nThis derivative measures function sensitivity to input changes.  \n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_21.png](/api/media/35/download)\n\n_General case: dA/dx â‰ˆ f(x)_\n\n## Fundamental Theorem\nDerivatives solve integral problems. Knowing that A\'(x) = xÂ² lets us reconstruct A(x). This interplayâ€”where derivatives invert integralsâ€”is calculus\'s **Fundamental Theorem**.\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_22.png](/api/media/36/download)\n\n_Graphical representation of Fundamental Theorem_\n\nThis theorem unites calculus\'s two pillars: derivatives and integrals as inverses.\n\n## Bonus: What exactly is dx?\nExpressions like dx appear in derivatives and integrals. Their meanings often confuse learners. Many interpret dx as \"infinitely small,\" but what does that mean?\n\nI suggest viewing dx as actual small numbers (e.g., dx=0.001), recognizing that:  \n1. Equations become \"approximately\" true  \n2. Accuracy improves as dx approaches zero  \n\nThis aligns with calculus\'s rigorous foundations using limits of finite quantities. Though typically Î”x denotes finite changes, using dx this way builds intuitive yet precise understanding.\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_24.svg](/api/media/38/download)\n\n_Conceptual visualization of dx as finite difference_\n\n## Next Steps\nNext chapter explores derivatives thoroughly. Remember: At each stage, imagine how these concepts could emerge naturally from thoughtful diagrams and explorations.','\n\n```markdown\n\"The art of mathematics lies in discerning that particular instance which encapsulates all seeds of generality.\" â€“ David Hilbert  \nThe pedagogical objective of this series is to facilitate an intellectual journey whereby readers emerge feeling capable of reconstructing calculus through first principles. That is, to explicate foundational concepts while elucidating their epistemological origins and substantive interpretations, employing a comprehensive visual methodology.  \nMathematical innovation represents a formidable intellectual undertaking, necessitating a distinction between comprehending justifications for established truths and generating theoretical constructs ex nihilo. At each juncture, readers are implored to engage in epistemological reflection: Were you an early mathematical pioneer contemplating these concepts while constructing appropriate diagrams, would the emergence of these truths feel experientially plausible?  \n\n## Questions of Area\nThis chapter endeavors to demonstrate how one might organically discover calculus\' core tenets through rigorous examination of a specific geometric problem: determining the area of a circular region. While one might recall the area formula as Ï€ multiplied by the squared radius (R), what is the ontological basis for this relationship? Does a methodological approach exist that reveals the formula\'s provenance?  \n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_2.svg](/api/media/17/download)  \n\n*This illustration depicts a circular region with radius R accompanied by its area formula.*  \n\nContemplating this problem while remaining receptive to generalizing the employed methodologies may lead to profound insights regarding three principal calculus concepts: integrals, derivatives, and their inverse relationship. However, our exposition commences with greater simplicity: consider merely a circle whose radius we shall standardize as 1 unit. When absolute precision proves unnecessary, an efficacious estimation strategy involves partitioning the circular area into elementary components whose measurements prove more tractable, subsequently aggregating these partial quantities.  \nDiverse partitioning methods invite consideration, each potentially yielding distinct and fruitful analytical pathways.  \n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_3.svg](/api/media/18/download)  \n\n*Illustration presenting various decomposition strategies for a unit circle.*  \n\nMathematical systems frequently yield more tractable solutions when symmetrical configurations are exploited. Among the illustrated partitions, both the sectoral division (lower left) and concentric annular decomposition (lower right) present promising analytical avenues. Our present focus centers upon the concentric annular approach.  \n\n## Unraveling Rings\nConsider an individual annular element within this configuration, designated as having radius r (where 0 â‰¤ r â‰¤ 1). Should we derive an exact expression for the area of such an annular segment and develop a systematic summation technique, this framework may facilitate comprehension of the complete circular area.  \n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_4.svg](/api/media/19/download)  \n\n*Highlighted annular region resulting from concentric partition.*  \n\nAdopting standard calculus notation, we denote an annular segment\'s radial thickness as \"dr\" (interpreted as an incremental radial difference). In the accompanying diagram, dr = 0.1. While conventional mathematical pedagogy often prefers \"Î”r\" for non-infinitesimal quantities, the present exposition employs \"dr\" deliberately, with justification to follow. Presently, conceptualize dr as a finite positive quantity.  \n\nWhich expression most accurately approximates the area of an annular segment with inner radius r and thickness dr?  \n- Ï€rÂ²â‹…dr  \n- 2Ï€rÂ²â‹…dr  \n- 2Ï€râ‹…dr  \n- Ï€râ‹…dr  \n\nOne might visualize transforming this annular segment into a quadrilateral whose width corresponds to the inner circumference 2Ï€r (directly deriving from Ï€\'s definition relating circumference to radius). The quadrilateral\'s height would then equal dr.  \n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_5.svg](/api/media/20/download)  \n\n*Geometric transformation of annular segment into quadrilateral.*  \n\nThis topological transformation produces an approximate rectangular form rather than an exact rectangle, since the outer circumference exceeds the inner circumference by 2Ï€(dr). However, initiating our investigation with approximate elementary areas permits conceptualizing this as a rectangle of width 2Ï€r and height dr. The discrepancy between true annular area and rectangular approximation constitutes a quantifiable error term.  \n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_6.svg](/api/media/21/download)  \n\n*Illustration demonstrating approximation refinement with decreasing dr.*  \n\nA pivotal observation emerges: this error becomes negligible relative to total area as drâ†’0. That is, the approximation 2Ï€râ‹…dr for annular area contains inherent inaccuracy, but this inaccuracy diminishes asymptotically with finer partitions.  \n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_7.svg](/api/media/22/download)  \n\n*Visualization of increasingly refined annular partitions.*  \n\n## Think to Graph\nHaving partitioned the circular area into annular elements approximated by 2Ï€râ‹…dr, one might anticipate summation challenges, particularly considering refined partitions. However, the intrepid mathematical mind conjectures that extreme refinement may paradoxically simplify computation rather than complicate it.  \n\nThe inner radius parameter r ranges from 0 to 1-dr (with dr=0.1 in our exemplar), with the upper bound approaching 1 as drâ†’0.  \n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_8.svg](/api/media/23/download)  \n\n*Summation of annular areas visualized as rectangular areas.*  \n\nVisualization enhancement occurs when arranging these rectangular approximations orthogonally along a horizontal axis representing râˆˆ[0,1]. Each rectangle possesses thickness dr (simultaneously representing r-value spacing) and height 2Ï€r (the corresponding circumference).  \n\nCrucially, all rectangles align contiguously along the r-axis, establishing a correspondence between partition spacing and rectangle width.  \n\nWhat height characterizes the rectangle positioned at r=0.3?  \n- 6Ï€  \n- 3/10 Ï€  \n- 2Ï€  \n- 3/5 Ï€  \n\nThis configuration naturally suggests the linear graph 2Ï€r with slope 2Ï€. Each rectangle\'s upper boundary contacts the graph at its rightmost point.  \n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_10.svg](/api/media/25/download)  \n\n*Graphical representation of annular approximation under 2Ï€r.*  \n\nAgain noting approximation: each rectangular area imperfectly estimates its corresponding annular segment. However, as previously established, approximation accuracy improves monotonically with decreasing dr.  \n\nThough direct summation proves computationally prohibitive, an insightful conjecture emerges: perhaps determining the limiting behavior of these sums as drâ†’0 provides computational tractability compared to explicit finite summation.  \n\nReconceptualizing the aggregate rectangular area exposes a profound geometric interpretation: as drâ†’0, the collective rectangular area converges to the area beneath the 2Ï€r graph.  \n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_12.svg](/api/media/27/download)  \n\n*Triangular region under 2Ï€r from r=0 to r=1.*  \n\nThis subgraphical region constitutes a right triangle with base 1 and height 2Ï€Â·1, yielding area Â½Â·1Â·2Ï€ = Ï€Â·1Â². Generalizing to radius R, the area becomes Â½Â·RÂ·2Ï€R = Ï€RÂ².  \n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_13.svg](/api/media/28/download)  \n\nRegardless of mathematical predisposition, the elegance of this derivation remains incontrovertible.  \n\n## Generalizing the Approach\nThe mathematical enterprise extends beyond particular solutions to general methodological development. We thus pause to consider the deeper implications of our approach, particularly the transition from approximation to exactitude â€“ a transition embodying the fundamental subtlety and power of calculus.  \n\nOur problem admitted approximation through sums comprising terms 2Ï€rÂ·dr over râˆˆ[0,1]. Here, dr serves dual roles: as a multiplicative factor in summation terms (2Ï€rÂ·dr) and as parameter spacing. Diminishing dr improves approximation fidelity.  \n\nThis summation framework permits geometric reinterpretation as rectangular areas under the function graph f(r)=2Ï€r. Consequently:  \n1. The sum converges to the circular area as drâ†’0  \n2. Simultaneously, it converges to the area under f(r)=2Ï€r  \n\nThis dual convergence establishes exact equivalence between our initial geometric problem and a definite integral (area under a graph). This exemplifies a central calculus paradigm: approximations via partitioning create analytical flexibility, enabling reformulation of intractable problems into more soluble forms.  \n\n## Integrals\nNumerous mathematical and scientific problems admit decomposition into summations of infinitesimal quantities. For instance, determining displacement from time-dependent velocity involves temporal partitioning, multiplying velocity v(t) by time differential dt to obtain incremental displacement.  \n\nThough subsequent chapters will elaborate such applications, broad conceptual parallels emerge: many such problems prove equivalent to determining areas under characteristic graphs, analogous to our circular area derivation.  \n\nThis equivalence manifests whenever approximating sums can be geometrized as adjacent rectangular areas. As approximations refine through diminution of partition sizes, original problems transmute into questions of graphical areas.  \n\nConsidering the relationship between acceleration (dt/dv) and velocity (dt/ds), does vehicular velocity modeling require area computation under an acceleration graph?  \n- Yes  \n- No  \n\nReiterating: approximations serve not as endpoints but as bridges connecting disparate problem formulations. We\'ve seen how displacement computation equivalates to graphical area determination â€“ a concept to be expanded subsequently.  \n\nPresently, having solved our initial problem through integral reformulation, mathematical curiosity naturally extends to area determination under arbitrary graphs. For f(x)=xÂ², what area lies beneath the curve between x=0 and x=3?  \n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_16.png](/api/media/30/download)  \n\nReformulating: fixing the left endpoint at x=0, let the right endpoint vary. Can we determine a function A(x) yielding the parabolic area from 0 to x?  \n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_17.png](/api/media/31/download)  \n\nThis function A(x) constitutes the integral of xÂ², specifically the integral from 0 to x. Formalized:  \n\nâˆ«â‚€Ë£ tÂ² dt  \n\nOur immediate concern lies in comprehending this enigmatic function A(x). Its explicit determination would constitute a significant calculus innovation.  \n\nOur interest extends beyond particular geometric solutions: numerous problems admitting approximation through summation mechanisms can be transduced into graphical area determination.  \n\n## Derivatives\nIntegral determination presents substantial analytical challenges. Confronting mathematical complexity, one should avoid direct frontal assault, preferring instead exploratory engagement with conceptual interrelationships.  \n\nConsider the functional interplay between f(x)=xÂ² and A(x). A subtle but critical observation emerges: slight input perturbation dx produces area variation dA.  \n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_18.png](/api/media/32/download)  \n\nThis incremental area dA is well-approximated by a rectangle of height f(x) and width dx. Approximation quality improves as dxâ†’0.  \n\nThis implies a functional relationship:  \ndA â‰ˆ f(x)Â·dx  \nRearranged:  \ndA/dx â‰ˆ f(x)  \n\nThis ratio of output variation to causative input variation approximates the graph\'s height at x.  \n\nThe relationship holds universally, not merely at particular x-values:  \n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_20.png](/api/media/34/download)  \n\nThis relationship â€“ while not immediately solving for A(x) â€“ provides vital analytical traction. Generalizing: for any function f(x), if A(x) gives the area beneath f, then dA/dx â‰ˆ f(x), with approximation improving as dxâ†’0.  \n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_21.png](/api/media/35/download)  \n\nHere we encounter another calculus cornerstone: derivatives. The limit of dA/dx as dxâ†’0 constitutes A\'s derivative â€“ a measure of functional sensitivity to input perturbations. Subsequent chapters will elaborate derivative visualization and computation.  \n\n## Fundamental Theorem\nDerivatives acquire importance through problem-solving utility. Our exploration has revealed their pivotal role in resolving integral problems â€“ those requiring graphical area determination. With derivative computation proficiency, one can reverse-engineer functions from their derivatives.  \n\nThis interrelation between integration and differentiation â€“ whereby an area function\'s derivative regenerates the original function â€“ comprises the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus. It unifies calculus\' two principal operations, establishing their inverse relationship.  \n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_22.png](/api/media/36/download)  \n\nThis theorem constitutes the conceptual keystone of calculus. Subsequent exposition will develop rigorous foundations for these ideas. Reaffirming our pedagogical philosophy: readers should perceive all developments as natural consequences of appropriate diagrammatic reasoning and conceptual exploration.  \n\n## Bonus Section: The Ontology of dx\nCalculus notation abounds with differential terms (dA, dr, dx) employed ubiquitously in derivative and integral expressions. Despite their centrality, considerable ambiguity surrounds their precise interpretation.  \n\nThis chapter promotes interpreting dx as representing actual numerical increments (e.g., dx=0.001). Contemporary convention often prefers Î”x for finite changes, reserving dx for abstract \"infinitesimals\" â€“ problematic conceptual entities lacking rigorous definition. Terms like \"infinitesimal\" risk semantic incoherence: are these numerical entities? If so, how do they differ from zero?  \n\nConventional pedagogical responses frequently dismiss dx notation\'s literal interpretation, asserting that derivatives (df/dx) are not true fractions and integrals (âˆ«f(x)dx) are not actual summations â€“ notwithstanding the integral symbol\'s etymological origin as a stylized \"S\" for \"sum\". Such approaches relegate dx to heuristic status pending advanced real analysis courses.  \n\nContrarily, this series proposes interpreting dx as concretely representing finite values (e.g., dx=0.001), despite introducing mathematical inaccuracy. The justification emerges through limiting behavior: errors become negligible as dxâ†’0. Our annular area approximation exemplifies this â€“ 2Ï€rÂ·dr becomes asymptotically exact as drâ†’0.  \n\nThis philosophical stance does not sacrifice rigor for intuition, but rather aligns notation with rigorous (Ïµ-Î´) foundations. Finite changes might conventionally be denoted Î”x, but our approach maintains mathematical integrity while enhancing conceptual accessibility. Subsequent chapters will elaborate differential manipulation rules within this framework.  \n\nWith these epistemological considerations addressed, we proceed to derivative formalization.  \n\n## Acknowledgments  \nGratitude is extended to supporters of the original video material and current patrons enabling continued educational endeavors. Those deriving value from these pedagogical resources are encouraged to consider patronage participation.  \n```','\"The art of doing mathematics is finding that special case that contains all the germs of generality.\"-David Hilbert\nâ€œæ•°å­¦çš„è‰ºæœ¯åœ¨äºŽæ‰¾åˆ°é‚£ä¸ªåŒ…å«æ‰€æœ‰æ™®éæ€§èŒèŠ½çš„ç‰¹ä¾‹ã€‚â€â€”â€”å¤§å«Â·å¸Œå°”ä¼¯ç‰¹\n\nMy goal is for you to come away from this series feeling like you could have invented calculus. That is, cover these core ideas, but in a way that makes clear where they actually come from, and what they really mean, using an all-around visual approach.\næˆ‘çš„ç›®æ ‡æ˜¯è®©ä½ åœ¨å­¦å®Œè¿™ä¸ªç³»åˆ—åŽï¼Œæ„Ÿè§‰è‡ªå·±æœ¬å¯ä»¥å‘æ˜Žå¾®ç§¯åˆ†ã€‚ä¹Ÿå°±æ˜¯è¯´ï¼Œæˆ‘ä»¬ä¼šæ¶µç›–è¿™äº›æ ¸å¿ƒç†å¿µï¼Œä½†é‡‡ç”¨ä¸€ç§å…¨è§†è§‰åŒ–çš„æ–¹å¼ï¼Œæ¸…æ™°åœ°å±•ç¤ºå®ƒä»¬çš„å®žé™…æ¥æºå’ŒçœŸæ­£å«ä¹‰ã€‚\n\nInventing math is no joke, and there is a difference between being told why something makes sense and actually generating it from scratch. But at all points I want you to think to yourself if you were an early mathematician pondering these ideas and drawing the right diagrams, does it feel reasonable that you could have stumbled upon these truths yourself?\nå‘æ˜Žæ•°å­¦ç»éžå„¿æˆï¼Œç†è§£äº‹ç‰©çš„åˆç†æ€§ä¸Žå…¶ä»Žé›¶æž„å»ºå­˜åœ¨æœ¬è´¨åŒºåˆ«ã€‚ä½†åœ¨æ•´ä¸ªè¿‡ç¨‹ä¸­ï¼Œæˆ‘å¸Œæœ›ä½ è®¾æƒ³è‡ªå·±æ˜¯ä¸€ä½æ—©æœŸæ•°å­¦å®¶ï¼šå½“ä½ æ·±æ€è¿™äº›æ¦‚å¿µå¹¶ç»˜åˆ¶æ­£ç¡®çš„å›¾è¡¨æ—¶ï¼Œæ˜¯å¦å®Œå…¨æœ‰å¯èƒ½è‡ªå·±å‘çŽ°è¿™äº›çœŸç†å‘¢ï¼Ÿ\n\nQuestions of area\nå…³äºŽé¢ç§¯çš„æ€è€ƒ\n\nIn this chapter, I want to show how you might stumble into the core ideas of calculus by thinking deeply about one very specific bit of geometry: The area of a circle. Maybe you know that the area is Ï€ multiplied by the radius R squared, but why? Is there a way to think about where this formula comes from?\næœ¬ç« ä¸­ï¼Œæˆ‘å°†å±•ç¤ºå¦‚ä½•é€šè¿‡å¯¹ä¸€ä¸ªå…·ä½“å‡ ä½•é—®é¢˜â€”â€”åœ†çš„é¢ç§¯â€”â€”çš„æ·±åº¦æ€è€ƒï¼Œè‡ªç„¶é¢†æ‚Ÿå¾®ç§¯åˆ†çš„æ ¸å¿ƒç†å¿µã€‚æˆ–è®¸ä½ çŸ¥é“é¢ç§¯å…¬å¼æ˜¯Ï€ä¹˜ä»¥åŠå¾„Rçš„å¹³æ–¹ï¼Œä½†ä¸ºä½•å¦‚æ­¤ï¼Ÿæ˜¯å¦å­˜åœ¨ç†è§£è¯¥å…¬å¼èµ·æºçš„é€”å¾„ï¼Ÿ\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_2.svg](/api/media/17/download)\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_2.svg](/api/media/17/download)\n\nThis figure illustrates the area of a circle with radius R and the formula that calculates its area.\næ­¤å›¾å±•ç¤ºäº†åŠå¾„ä¸ºRçš„åœ†é¢ç§¯åŠå…¶è®¡ç®—å…¬å¼ã€‚\n\nContemplating this problem and leaving yourself open to generalizing the tools you use along the way can actually lead you to a glimpse of three big ideas in calculus: Integrals, derivatives, and the fact that they are opposites.\næ·±å…¥æ€è€ƒè¿™ä¸ªé—®é¢˜å¹¶å¼€æ”¾æ‹“å±•ä½¿ç”¨å·¥å…·çš„æ–¹æ³•ï¼Œå®žé™…ä¸Šèƒ½è®©ä½ çª¥è§å¾®ç§¯åˆ†çš„ä¸‰å¤§æ ¸å¿ƒæ€æƒ³ï¼šç§¯åˆ†ã€å¯¼æ•°ï¼Œä»¥åŠå®ƒä»¬äº’ä¸ºé€†è¿ç®—çš„æœ¬è´¨ã€‚\n\nBut the story of finding the area starts more simply: Just you, and a circle. To be concrete, let\'s say the radius is 1 unit. If you weren\'t worried about the exact area, and just wanted to estimate it, one way you might go about it is to chop the circle into smaller pieces whose areas are easier to approximate, then add up the results.\nä½†æŽ¢ç´¢é¢ç§¯çš„æ•…äº‹å§‹äºŽæ›´ç®€å•çš„èµ·ç‚¹ï¼šä»…ä½ ä¸Žä¸€ä¸ªåœ†ã€‚å…·ä½“åŒ–å‡è®¾ï¼Œè®¾åŠå¾„ä¸º1å•ä½ã€‚è‹¥ä¸è‹›æ±‚ç²¾ç¡®é¢ç§¯åªæ±‚ä¼°ç®—ï¼Œå¯å°†åœ†åˆ†å‰²ä¸ºæ›´æ˜“ä¼°ç®—é¢ç§¯çš„å°ç‰‡ï¼Œå†ç´¯åŠ ç»“æžœã€‚\n\nThere are many ways you might go about this, each of which may lead to its own interesting line of reasoning.\nå®žçŽ°åˆ†å‰²çš„æ–¹å¼å¤šæ ·ï¼Œæ¯ç§éƒ½å¯èƒ½å¼•å‘ç‹¬ç‰¹çš„æŽ¨ç†è·¯å¾„ã€‚\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_3.svg](/api/media/18/download)\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_3.svg](/api/media/18/download)\n\nThis figure illustrates different ways to divide the area of the circle with a radius 1.\næ­¤å›¾å±•ç¤ºäº†åŠå¾„ä¸º1çš„åœ†çš„ä¸åŒåˆ†å‰²æ–¹æ³•ã€‚\n\nMath has a tendency to reward you when you respect symmetry, so among the divisions shown above, either the bottom left one dividing into pizza slices or the bottom right which divides into concentric rings will be likely to lead us down a fruitful path. For now, let\'s think about the division into concentric rings.\næ•°å­¦å¾€å¾€å¥–èµå°Šé‡å¯¹ç§°æ€§çš„æ€è€ƒã€‚å› æ­¤ï¼Œä¸Šå›¾ä¸­å·¦ä¸‹è§’çš„æŠ«è¨å¼åˆ†å‰²æˆ–å³ä¸‹è§’çš„åŒå¿ƒçŽ¯åˆ†å‰²æ›´å¯èƒ½å¼•å‘ä¸°ç¡•æˆæžœã€‚æˆ‘ä»¬æš‚ä»¥åŒå¿ƒçŽ¯åˆ†å‰²å±•å¼€æ€è€ƒã€‚\n\nUnraveling rings\nå±•å¼€åŒå¿ƒçŽ¯\n\nFocus on just one of those concentric rings, and let\'s call its radius r, which will be some number between 0 and 1. If we can find a nice expression for the area of one such ring, and if we find a nice way to add them all up, it might lead us to an understanding of the full circle\'s area.\nèšç„¦å•ä¸ªåŒå¿ƒçŽ¯ï¼Œè®¾å…¶åŠå¾„ä¸ºrï¼ˆå–å€¼0è‡³1ï¼‰ã€‚è‹¥èƒ½æ‰¾åˆ°æ­¤ç±»çŽ¯å½¢é¢ç§¯çš„ç®€æ´è¡¨è¾¾å¼ï¼Œå¹¶å‘å±•å‡ºé«˜æ•ˆçš„ç´¯åŠ æ–¹æ³•ï¼Œå°±å¯èƒ½ç†è§£æ•´ä¸ªåœ†çš„é¢ç§¯ã€‚\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_4.svg](/api/media/19/download)\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_4.svg](/api/media/19/download)\n\nThis figure highlights a band of area formed from dividing the circle into concentric rings.\næ­¤å›¾çªå‡ºæ˜¾ç¤ºäº†åŒå¿ƒçŽ¯åˆ†å‰²å½¢æˆçš„ä¸€ä¸ªçŽ¯å½¢åŒºåŸŸã€‚\n\nIn the spirit of using what will come to be standard calculus notations, let\'s call the thickness of one of these rings â€œdrâ€, which you can read as meaning â€œthe tiny difference in radius from one ring to the nextâ€. In the drawing above, for example, dr = 0.1.\nä¸ºå¥‘åˆæœªæ¥æ ‡å‡†çš„å¾®ç§¯åˆ†ç¬¦å·ä½“ç³»ï¼Œæˆ‘ä»¬ç§°çŽ¯çš„åŽšåº¦ä¸ºâ€œdrâ€ï¼Œæ„ä¸ºâ€œç›¸é‚»çŽ¯é—´çš„å¾®å°åŠå¾„å·®â€ã€‚ä¾‹å¦‚ä¸Šå›¾ä¸­dr = 0.1ã€‚\n\nYou should know that some people, perhaps even most people, would object to our using the notation dr to represent a specific not-infinitely-small size like this, suggesting instead we use some other notation, like Î”r. Well, it\'s my house and my rules, and I have my reasons, which we\'ll get into later. For now, I want you to think about dr as simply being some number without worrying about what phrases like â€œinfinitely smallâ€ would mean.\néœ€è¯´æ˜Žï¼šéƒ¨åˆ†äººï¼ˆæˆ–å¤šæ•°äººï¼‰ä¼šåå¯¹ç”¨drè¡¨ç¤ºå…·ä½“æœ‰é™å°çš„å°ºå¯¸ï¼ˆå¦‚æœ¬ä¾‹ï¼‰ï¼Œå»ºè®®æ”¹ç”¨Î”rç­‰ç¬¦å·ã€‚ä½†æ­¤å¤„éµå¾ªæˆ‘çš„å‡†åˆ™ï¼Œç†ç”±åŽç»­é˜æ˜Žã€‚çŽ°åœ¨åªéœ€å°†drè§†ä¸ºæ™®é€šæ•°å€¼ï¼Œæš‚ä¸æ·±ç©¶â€œæ— é™å°â€çš„å“²å­¦å«ä¹‰ã€‚\n\nWhich of the following is closest to the area of one of these rings with inner radius r and thickness dr?\nä»¥ä¸‹å“ªé¡¹æœ€æŽ¥è¿‘å†…åŠå¾„ä¸ºrã€åŽšåº¦ä¸ºdrçš„çŽ¯å½¢é¢ç§¯ï¼Ÿ\nÏ€rÂ²â‹…dr\n2Ï€rÂ²â‹…dr\n2Ï€râ‹…dr\nÏ€râ‹…dr\n\nYou might imagine straightening out this ring, into a shape whose width is the inner circumference of the ring, which is 2Ï€r. This is the very definition of Ï€, relating the circumference of a circle to its diameter, or radius. The thickness of this unwrapped shape would be dr.\nä¸å¦¨è®¾æƒ³å°†æ­¤çŽ¯å±•å¼€ä¸ºæ‰å¹³å½¢çŠ¶ï¼Œå…¶å®½åº¦ç­‰äºŽå†…åœ†å‘¨2Ï€rï¼ˆè¿™æ­£æ˜¯Ï€çš„å®šä¹‰ï¼šåœ†å‘¨é•¿ä¸Žç›´å¾„æˆ–åŠå¾„çš„æ¯”å€¼ï¼‰ã€‚å±•å¼€å½¢æ€çš„åŽšåº¦å³drã€‚\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_5.svg](/api/media/20/download)\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_5.svg](/api/media/20/download)\n\nThis figure illustrates a concentric ring with radius r straightened out.\næ­¤å›¾å±•ç¤ºäº†åŠå¾„ä¸ºrçš„åŒå¿ƒçŽ¯è¢«æ‹‰ç›´åŽçš„å½¢æ€ã€‚\n\nIf this unwrapped shape is meant to perfectly match the area of the ring, it will be approximately, but not exactly, a rectangle. Because the outer circumference of the ring will be slightly larger than the inner circumference, the bottom of our unwrapped shape will be slightly wider than the top. However, if we\'re comfortable beginning the exploration by only approximating the area of each small piece, we could consider this to be approximately a rectangle with a width of 2Ï€r and a height of dr.\nè‹¥è¦ä½¿å±•å¼€å½¢æ€ç²¾ç¡®åŒ¹é…çŽ¯å½¢é¢ç§¯ï¼Œå®ƒè¿‘ä¼¼ä½†ä¸å®Œå…¨ç­‰äºŽçŸ©å½¢ã€‚ç”±äºŽçŽ¯çš„å¤–å‘¨ç•¥é•¿äºŽå†…å‘¨ï¼Œå±•å¼€å½¢æ€åº•éƒ¨ç•¥å®½äºŽé¡¶éƒ¨ã€‚ä½†è‹¥æŽ¥å—ä»¥è¿‘ä¼¼æ³•å¯åŠ¨æŽ¢ç´¢ï¼Œå¯å°†å…¶è§†ä¸ºå®½2Ï€rã€é«˜drçš„è¿‘ä¼¼çŸ©å½¢ã€‚\n\nThe difference between the true area of the ring and the area of the rectangular approximation introduces some small error.\nçœŸå®žçŽ¯å½¢é¢ç§¯ä¸ŽçŸ©å½¢è¿‘ä¼¼é¢ç§¯ä¹‹é—´å­˜åœ¨çš„å·®å¼‚ä¼šå¯¼è‡´å°è¯¯å·®ã€‚\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_6.svg](/api/media/21/download)\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_6.svg](/api/media/21/download)\n\nThis figure illustrates how approximating a ring\'s area as a rectangle gets better and better for smaller choices of dr.\næ­¤å›¾è¯´æ˜Žï¼šå½“drå–å€¼è¶Šå°æ—¶ï¼Œä»¥çŸ©å½¢è¿‘ä¼¼çŽ¯å½¢é¢ç§¯çš„æ•ˆæžœè¶Šå¥½ã€‚\n\nHowever, and this will be a key idea,this error becomes tiny compared to the overall area when dr is small. In other words, approximating the area of each ring as 2Ï€râ‹…dr is wrong, but as we chop up the circle into finer and finer rings, it becomes less and less wrong.\nç„¶è€Œå…³é”®åœ¨äºŽï¼šå½“drè¶³å¤Ÿå°æ—¶ï¼Œè¯¥è¯¯å·®ç›¸å¯¹äºŽæ€»é¢ç§¯å¯å¿½ç•¥ä¸è®¡ã€‚æ¢è¨€ä¹‹ï¼Œç”¨2Ï€râ‹…drè¿‘ä¼¼å•çŽ¯é¢ç§¯è™½æœ‰è¯¯å·®ï¼Œä½†éšç€åœ†è¢«åˆ†å‰²ä¸ºæ›´ç»†å¯†çš„çŽ¯ï¼Œè¯¯å·®å½±å“è¶Šæ¥è¶Šå°ã€‚\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_7.svg](/api/media/22/download)\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_7.svg](/api/media/22/download)\n\nThis figure illustrates small and smaller choices of dr when dividing the circle into rings.\næ­¤å›¾å±•ç¤ºäº†å°†åœ†åˆ†å‰²ä¸ºçŽ¯æ—¶drä¸æ–­å‡å°çš„è¿‡ç¨‹ã€‚\n\nThink to Graph\nè½¬å‘å›¾å½¢æ€è€ƒ\n\nSo you\'ve broken up the area of this circle into all these rings, and you\'re approximating the area of each one as 2Ï€â‹…râ‹…dr. You might think that actually adding all those areas together will be a nightmare, especially if you\'re seeking more accurate approximations with finer and finer divisions of the circle. However, being the bold mathematician that you are, you might have a hunch that taking this process to the utmost extreme may actually make things easier rather than harder.\nè‡³æ­¤ï¼Œä½ å·²å°†æ‰€æœ‰çŽ¯çš„é¢ç§¯è¿‘ä¼¼ä¸º2Ï€â‹…râ‹…drï¼Œç²¾ç¡®æ±‚å’Œä¼¼ä¹Žæ˜¯ä¸ªå™©æ¢¦â€”â€”å°¤å…¶è¿½æ±‚æ›´ç»†åˆ†å‰²æ›´é«˜ç²¾åº¦æ—¶ã€‚ç„¶è€Œä½œä¸ºå‹‡æ•¢çš„æ•°å­¦å®¶ï¼Œä½ å¯èƒ½æœ‰ç›´è§‰ï¼šå°†è¿™ä¸€è¿‡ç¨‹æŽ¨å‘æžè‡´åè€Œä¼šåŒ–éš¾ä¸ºæ˜“ã€‚\n\nThe size of that inner radius for the rings ranges from 0 for the smallest ring up to 1 for the largest, spaced out by whatever thickness we chose for dr, like 0.1. Or rather, they range from 0 up to 1âˆ’dr, but as dr gets smaller that upper bound looks more and more like 1.\nçŽ¯çš„å†…åŠå¾„ä»Žæœ€å°çŽ¯çš„0åˆ°æœ€å¤§çŽ¯çš„1ï¼ŒæŒ‰æ‰€é€‰drï¼ˆå¦‚0.1ï¼‰æ­¥è¿›ã€‚ä¸¥æ ¼æ¥è¯´èŒƒå›´æ˜¯0è‡³1âˆ’drï¼Œä½†éšç€drå‡å°ï¼Œä¸Šé™è¶‹è¿‘äºŽ1ã€‚\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_8.svg](/api/media/23/download)\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_8.svg](/api/media/23/download)\n\nThis figure shows how the area of the circle is equal to the sum of rings\' areas, where the area of each ring is given by 2Ï€â‹…râ‹…dr.\næ­¤å›¾å±•ç¤ºäº†åœ†é¢ç§¯ç­‰äºŽå„çŽ¯é¢ç§¯ä¹‹å’Œï¼ˆæ¯çŽ¯é¢ç§¯ç”±2Ï€â‹…râ‹…drç»™å‡ºï¼‰ã€‚\n\nTo help draw what adding those areas looks like, think about adding together your rectangular approximations. A nice way to visualize this is to fit all those rectangles upright, side-by-side standing on a horizontal axis. We can think of this horizontal axis as representing all the values of r ranging from 0 up to 1.\nä¸ºå…·è±¡åŒ–é¢ç§¯ç´¯åŠ ï¼Œè®¾æƒ³å°†çŸ©å½¢è¿‘ä¼¼ç›´ç«‹æŽ’åˆ—åœ¨æ°´å¹³è½´ä¸Šã€‚è¯¥è½´ä»£è¡¨rä»Ž0åˆ°1çš„æ‰€æœ‰å–å€¼ã€‚\n\nEach rectangle has a thickness of dr, which is also the spacing between different values of r, which is why they fit so snugly right together. The height of any rectangle above a value of r, is exactly 2Ï€â‹…r. This is the circumference of the corresponding ring.\næ¯ä¸ªçŸ©å½¢åŽšdrï¼ˆå³rå€¼çš„é—´éš”ï¼‰ï¼Œæ•…ç´§å¯†æŽ’åˆ—ã€‚rå¯¹åº”çŸ©å½¢çš„é«˜åº¦æ°å¥½æ˜¯2Ï€â‹…rï¼ˆå¯¹åº”çŽ¯çš„å‘¨é•¿ï¼‰ã€‚\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_9.svg](/api/media/24/download)\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_9.svg](/api/media/24/download)\n\nThis figure plots the radius of each of the circle\'s rings on a numberline, where the spacing between each radius is equal to dr.\næ­¤å›¾å°†å„çŽ¯çš„åŠå¾„ç»˜åˆ¶åœ¨æ•°è½´ä¸Šï¼Œé—´è·ç­‰äºŽdrã€‚\n\nWhat is the height of the rectangle whose left corner touches r=0.3?\nå·¦è§’æŽ¥è§¦r=0.3çš„çŸ©å½¢é«˜åº¦æ˜¯å¤šå°‘ï¼Ÿ\næ³¨æ„ï¼šæ­¤æŒ‡ä¸Šå›¾ä¸­é«˜äº®æ˜¾ç¤ºçš„çŸ©å½¢ã€‚\n6Ï€\n3/10 Ï€\n2Ï€\n3/5 Ï€\n\nNote: This is the rectangle highlighted in the still figure above.\n\nA nice way to think of this setup is to draw the graph of 2Ï€r, which is this straight line with a slope of 2Ï€. Each of these rectangles extends up to the point where it just barely touches the graph.\nç†è§£æ­¤ç»“æž„çš„ä¸€ç§ä¼˜é›…æ–¹å¼ï¼Œæ˜¯ç»˜åˆ¶2Ï€rçš„å›¾åƒâ€”â€”æ–œçŽ‡ä¸º2Ï€çš„ç›´çº¿ã€‚æ¯ä¸ªçŸ©å½¢é¡¶ç«¯æ°å¥½è§¦åŠè¯¥å›¾åƒã€‚\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_10.svg](/api/media/25/download)\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_10.svg](/api/media/25/download)\n\nThe unraveled rings under the graph of 2Ï€r.\n2Ï€rå›¾åƒä¸‹å±•å¼€çš„çŽ¯ã€‚\n\nAgain, we\'re being approximate here. Each of those rectangles only approximates the area of their corresponding ring from the circle. But remember, the approximation of 2Ï€â‹…râ‹…dr for the area of each ring will get less and less wrong as the size of dr gets smaller and smaller.\né‡ç”³ï¼šæˆ‘ä»¬ä»åœ¨è¿‘ä¼¼ä¸­ã€‚æ¯ä¸ªçŸ©å½¢ä»…è¿‘ä¼¼å¯¹åº”çŽ¯çš„é¢ç§¯ã€‚ä½†éšç€drå‡å°ï¼Œ2Ï€â‹…râ‹…drçš„è¿‘ä¼¼è¯¯å·®ä¼šè¶Šæ¥è¶Šå°ã€‚\n\nAgain, concretely adding up the areas of all these rectangles would be a royal pain, but that\'s when you get a crazy thought: Maybe, just maybe, asking what this sum approaches as the choice of dr gets smaller will be easier than ever actually computing the sum for any specific value of dr.\nå½“ç„¶ï¼Œå…·ä½“è®¡ç®—æ‰€æœ‰çŸ©å½¢é¢ç§¯å’Œéžå¸¸éº»çƒ¦ã€‚æ­¤æ—¶ä½ èŒç”Ÿä¸€ä¸ªç–¯ç‹‚æƒ³æ³•ï¼šæˆ–è®¸å½“drè¶‹è¿‘äºŽ0æ—¶ï¼Œæ±‚æ­¤å’Œçš„æžé™æ¯”è®¡ç®—å…·ä½“drä¸‹çš„å’Œæ›´ç®€å•ã€‚\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_11.svg](/api/media/26/download)\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_11.svg](/api/media/26/download)\n\nThe unraveled rings under the graph of 2Ï€r.\n2Ï€rå›¾åƒä¸‹å±•å¼€çš„çŽ¯ã€‚\n\nThis has a very beautiful meaning when looking at the sum of the areas of all these rectangles. For smaller and smaller choices of dr, notice how all their area in aggregate simply looks like the area under this graph. The smaller this value dr is, the closer that aggregate area of the rectangles is to being precisely the area under the graph.\nè§‚å¯ŸçŸ©å½¢é¢ç§¯å’Œæ—¶ï¼Œè¿™è•´å«ç€æžç¾Žçš„å«ä¹‰ï¼šéšç€drå‡å°ï¼ŒçŸ©å½¢æ€»é¢ç§¯æ„ˆå‘æŽ¥è¿‘å›¾åƒä¸‹çš„é¢ç§¯ã€‚drè¶Šå°ï¼ŒçŸ©å½¢æ€»é¢ç§¯ä¸Žå›¾åƒä¸‹é¢ç§¯è¶Šç²¾ç¡®ä¸€è‡´ã€‚\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_12.svg](/api/media/27/download)\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_12.svg](/api/media/27/download)\n\nThis portion under the graph is a triangle, whose base is 1, and whose height is 2Ï€â‹…1. So the area, which is Â½ base times height, comes out to be Ï€â‹…1Â².\næ­¤å›¾åƒä¸‹æ–¹çš„åŒºåŸŸæ˜¯åº•ä¸º1ã€é«˜ä¸º2Ï€â‹…1çš„ä¸‰è§’å½¢ã€‚é¢ç§¯ä¸ºÂ½ Ã— åº• Ã— é«˜ = Ï€â‹…1Â²ã€‚\n\nOr, if the radius of our original circle had been some other value, capital R, that area would be 1/2 â‹… R â‹… 2Ï€R.\nè‹¥åŽŸå§‹åœ†åŠå¾„ä¸ºRï¼Œé¢ç§¯åˆ™ä¸º1/2 Ã— R Ã— 2Ï€Rã€‚\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_13.svg](/api/media/28/download)\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_13.svg](/api/media/28/download)\n\nThis is the formula for the area of a circle. It doesn\'t matter who you are or what you typically think of math; that right there is beautiful.\næ­¤å³åœ†çš„é¢ç§¯å…¬å¼ã€‚æ— è®ºä½ æ˜¯è°æˆ–å¯¹æ•°å­¦æŒä½•è§‚ç‚¹ï¼Œæ­¤å¤„çš„ç®€æ´ç¾Žä¸å¯å¦è®¤ã€‚\n\nGeneralizing the Approach\næŽ¨å¹¿æ–¹æ³•\n\nBeing the mathematician that you are, you don\'t just care about finding the answer, you care about developing general problem-solving tools and techniques. So take a moment to meditate on what just happened, and why that worked, because the way we transitioned from something approximate to something precise is actually pretty subtle, and cuts deep to what calculus is all about.\nä½œä¸ºæ•°å­¦å®¶ï¼Œä½ ä¸ä»…å…³æ³¨ç­”æ¡ˆï¼Œæ›´å…³æ³¨å‘å±•é€šç”¨è§£é¢˜å·¥å…·ä¸ŽæŠ€æœ¯ã€‚è¯·èŠ±ç‰‡åˆ»æ²‰æ€ï¼šæˆ‘ä»¬å¦‚ä½•ä»Žè¿‘ä¼¼è¿‡æ¸¡åˆ°ç²¾ç¡®ï¼Ÿè¿™è¿‡ç¨‹ç²¾å¦™ä¸”ç›´å‡»å¾®ç§¯åˆ†æœ¬è´¨ã€‚\n\nYou had a problem that could be approximated with the sum of many small numbers, each of which looked like 2Ï€â‹…râ‹…dr for values of r ranging between 0 and 1. Each of those approximates the area of one of these thin rings.\nåŽŸå§‹é—®é¢˜å¯ç”¨å¤šä¸ªå°æ•°ä¹‹å’Œè¿‘ä¼¼ï¼Œæ¯ä¸ªæ•°å½¢å¦‚2Ï€â‹…râ‹…drï¼ˆrä»Ž0åˆ°1ï¼‰ã€‚å®ƒä»¬è¿‘ä¼¼å„ç»†çŽ¯é¢ç§¯ã€‚\n\nThe small number dr represents our choice for the thickness of each ring, for example 0.1, and there are two important things to note. First, not only is dr a factor in the quantity we\'re adding up, 2Ï€â‹…râ‹…dr, it also gives the spacing between the different values of r. The smaller the choice of dr, the better the approximation.\nå°æ•°å€¼drï¼ˆå¦‚0.1ï¼‰ä»£è¡¨çŽ¯çš„åŽšåº¦ã€‚éœ€æ³¨æ„ä¸¤ç‚¹ï¼šé¦–å…ˆï¼Œdrä¸ä»…æ˜¯æ±‚å’Œé¡¹2Ï€â‹…râ‹…drä¸­çš„å› å­ï¼Œå®ƒè¿˜å†³å®šäº†rå€¼é—´çš„é—´è·ã€‚drè¶Šå°ï¼Œè¿‘ä¼¼æ•ˆæžœè¶Šå¥½ã€‚\n\nAdding all those numbers could be seen in a different clever way as adding up the areas of many thin rectangles sitting under a graph; in this example that graph was of the function 2Ï€â‹…r.\næ±‚å’Œçš„å¦ä¸€ç§å·§å¦™ç†è§£æ˜¯ï¼šç´¯åŠ å›¾åƒä¸‹ä¼—å¤šç»†çŸ©å½¢çš„é¢ç§¯ã€‚æœ¬ä¾‹ä¸­å›¾åƒå¯¹åº”å‡½æ•°2Ï€â‹…rã€‚\n\nSo on the one hand the sum of the areas of these slices approaches the area of the circle for smaller and smaller choices of dr. But on the other hand, that sum also approaches the area under this graph. This is how we concluded that our original hard problem had an answer equal to the area under a certain graph; not just approximately equal, but precisely equal. I\'ll emphasize again what a big theme in calculus this is: The purpose of approximating a question by subdividing it like this is not that we don\'t care about precision, it\'s that the approximation using many smaller pieces gives us the flexibility to reframe our original hard question into something simpler.\nå› æ­¤ï¼Œéšç€drå‡å°ï¼Œåˆ‡ç‰‡é¢ç§¯å’Œæ—¢é€¼è¿‘åœ†é¢ç§¯ï¼Œåˆé€¼è¿‘å›¾åƒä¸‹é¢ç§¯ã€‚ç”±æ­¤æˆ‘ä»¬å¾—å‡ºç»“è®ºï¼šåŽŸå§‹éš¾é¢˜çš„ç­”æ¡ˆç²¾ç¡®ç­‰äºŽæŸå›¾åƒä¸‹çš„é¢ç§¯ã€‚é‡ç”³å¾®ç§¯åˆ†çš„ä¸€å¤§ä¸»é¢˜ï¼šé€šè¿‡ç»†åˆ†è¿‘ä¼¼é—®é¢˜çš„ç›®çš„å¹¶éžå¿½ç•¥ç²¾åº¦ï¼Œè€Œæ˜¯åˆ©ç”¨å¤šå°ç‰‡çš„è¿‘ä¼¼å¸¦æ¥çµæ´»æ€§ï¼Œå°†åŽŸé—®é¢˜é‡æž„ä¸ºæ›´ç®€å•çš„å½¢å¼ã€‚\n\nIntegrals\nç§¯åˆ†\n\nA lot of other hard problems in math and science can be broken down and approximated as the sum of many small quantities. For example if you want to figure out how far a car has gone based on its velocity at each point in time, you might range through many many points in time, and multiply the velocity at each time t by some tiny change in time dt to get the corresponding bit of distance traveled in the little time.\næ•°å­¦ä¸Žç§‘å­¦ä¸­è®¸å¤šéš¾é¢˜å¯åˆ†è§£è¿‘ä¼¼ä¸ºå¤šä¸ªå°é‡ä¹‹å’Œã€‚ä¾‹å¦‚ï¼šè‹¥è¦æ ¹æ®æ¯æ—¶åˆ»çš„é€Ÿåº¦è®¡ç®—æ±½è½¦è¡Œé©¶è·ç¦»ï¼Œå¯å–ä¼—å¤šæ—¶é—´ç‚¹tï¼Œå°†å„ç‚¹é€Ÿåº¦ä¹˜ä»¥å¾®å°æ—¶é—´å˜åŒ–dtï¼Œå¾—åˆ°è¯¥å¾®å°æ—¶é—´æ®µå†…çš„è¡Œç¨‹ã€‚\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_15.png](/api/media/29/download)\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_15.png](/api/media/29/download)\n\nI\'ll talk through the details of examples like this later in the series, but at a high level, many of these types of problems turn out to be equivalent to finding the area under some graph, in much the same way that our circle problem did.\næœ¬ç³»åˆ—åŽç»­å°†è¯¦è¿°æ­¤ç±»ä¾‹å­ã€‚ä½†é«˜å±‚æ¬¡åœ°çœ‹ï¼Œè®¸å¤šé—®é¢˜æœ€ç»ˆç­‰ä»·äºŽæ±‚æŸå›¾åƒä¸‹çš„é¢ç§¯ï¼Œæ­£å¦‚åœ†é¢ç§¯é—®é¢˜ã€‚\n\nThis happens whenever the quantities that you\'re adding up, the ones whose sum approximates your original problem, can be thought of as the area of many thin rectangles sitting side-by-side like this. If finer and finer approximations of your original problem correspond to thinner and thinner rectangles, the original problem will be equivalent to finding the area under some graph.\nå½“æ±‚å’Œé¡¹ï¼ˆå³è¿‘ä¼¼åŽŸé—®é¢˜çš„å„é¡¹ï¼‰å¯è§†ä¸ºå¹¶åˆ—ç»†çŸ©å½¢é¢ç§¯æ—¶ï¼Œæ­¤ç±»å…³ç³»å³æˆç«‹ã€‚è‹¥æ›´ä¼˜è¿‘ä¼¼å¯¹åº”æ›´ç»†çš„çŸ©å½¢ï¼Œåˆ™åŽŸé—®é¢˜ç­‰ä»·äºŽæ±‚æŸå›¾åƒä¸‹çš„é¢ç§¯ã€‚\n\nIn the same way velocity measures the change in position over the change in time, acceleration measures the change in velocity over the change in time. Can the velocity of a car be modeled as the area under the graph of acceleration?\nå¦‚åŒé€Ÿåº¦è¡¡é‡ä½ç½®éšæ—¶é—´å˜åŒ–çŽ‡ï¼ŒåŠ é€Ÿåº¦è¡¡é‡é€Ÿåº¦éšæ—¶é—´å˜åŒ–çŽ‡ã€‚æ±½è½¦é€Ÿåº¦èƒ½å¦å»ºæ¨¡ä¸ºåŠ é€Ÿåº¦å›¾åƒä¸‹çš„é¢ç§¯ï¼Ÿ\næ˜¯\nå¦\n\nYes\nNo\n\nAgain, notice that the purpose of the small approximations is not that we intend to use them directly, per se, but that the two separate ways to think about what these approximations approach lets us reframe the question of how far the car has traveled into the question of finding the area under a certain curve.\nå†æ¬¡æ³¨æ„ï¼šå¾®å°è¿‘ä¼¼çš„ç›®çš„å¹¶éžç›´æŽ¥ä½¿ç”¨å®ƒä»¬ï¼Œè€Œæ˜¯é€šè¿‡ä¸¤ç§è§†è§’çœ‹å¾…è¿‘ä¼¼ç»“æžœçš„æžé™ï¼Œå°†æ±½è½¦è¡Œé©¶è·ç¦»é—®é¢˜è½¬åŒ–ä¸ºæ±‚æŸæ›²çº¿ä¸‹é¢ç§¯çš„é—®é¢˜ã€‚\n\nWe\'ll see this idea in more detail later in the series, so don\'t worry if it\'s not 100% clear right now. The point is that you, as the mathematician having just solved a problem by reframing it as an area under a graph, might start thinking about how to find the area under other graphs.\nåŽç»­ç« èŠ‚å°†è¯¦ç»†æŽ¢è®¨æ­¤æ¦‚å¿µï¼Œç›®å‰æ— éœ€å®Œå…¨ç†è§£ã€‚å…³é”®åœ¨äºŽï¼šä½œä¸ºåˆšé€šè¿‡é‡æž„ä¸ºå›¾åƒä¸‹é¢ç§¯è§£å†³éš¾é¢˜çš„æ•°å­¦å®¶ï¼Œä½ å¯èƒ½ä¼šå¼€å§‹æ€è€ƒå¦‚ä½•æ±‚å…¶ä»–å›¾åƒä¸‹çš„é¢ç§¯ã€‚\n\nWe were lucky in our circle problem that the relevant area turned out to be a triangle, but imagine instead something like a parabola, the graph of the function xÂ². What\'s the area under this curve, say between the values of x=0 and x=3? It\'s hard to think about, isn\'t it?\nåœ†é¢ç§¯é—®é¢˜ä¸­æˆ‘ä»¬å¹¸è¿åœ°å¾—åˆ°ä¸‰è§’å½¢ï¼Œä½†è¯•æƒ³æŠ›ç‰©çº¿ï¼ˆå‡½æ•°xÂ²å›¾åƒï¼‰ã€‚åœ¨x=0åˆ°x=3åŒºé—´å†…ï¼Œå…¶æ›²çº¿ä¸‹é¢ç§¯æ˜¯å¤šå°‘ï¼Ÿè¿™ä¼¼ä¹Žéš¾ä»¥æž„æƒ³ã€‚\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_16.png](/api/media/30/download)\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_16.png](/api/media/30/download)\n\nLet me frame that question a different way: Fix that left endpoint in place at 0, and let that right endpoint vary: Can you find a function, A(x), that gives you the area under this parabola between 0 and x?\næ¢ç§æé—®æ–¹å¼ï¼šå›ºå®šå·¦ç«¯ç‚¹ä¸º0ï¼Œç§»åŠ¨å³ç«¯ç‚¹ã€‚èƒ½å¦æ‰¾åˆ°å‡½æ•°A(x)è¡¨ç¤º0åˆ°xé—´æŠ›ç‰©çº¿ä¸‹çš„é¢ç§¯ï¼Ÿ\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_17.png](/api/media/31/download)\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_17.png](/api/media/31/download)\n\nIn calculus, you might call this function A(x) an â€œintegralâ€ of xÂ². Well, more precisely we\'d say this is the integral of xÂ² from 0 up to x. Or rather, to disambiguate whether x represents the variable for the function, or if it represents the right endpoint, it would be even better to describe this area as the integral of the function f(t)=tÂ² between the values 0 and x. In the lingo, you\'d see it written like this:\nå¾®ç§¯åˆ†ä¸­ï¼Œå¯ç§°A(x)ä¸ºxÂ²çš„â€œç§¯åˆ†â€ã€‚æ›´å‡†ç¡®åœ°è¯´ï¼Œè¿™æ˜¯ä»Ž0åˆ°xçš„xÂ²ç§¯åˆ†ã€‚ä¸ºé¿å…æ··æ·†å˜é‡xä¸Žå³ç«¯ç‚¹xï¼Œæ›´ä½³æè¿°æ˜¯ï¼šè¯¥é¢ç§¯æ˜¯å‡½æ•°f(t)=tÂ²åœ¨0åˆ°xé—´çš„ç§¯åˆ†ã€‚ç¬¦å·åŒ–è¡¨ç¤ºå¦‚ä¸‹ï¼š\n\n$$\nA(x) = \\int_{0}^{x} tÂ² dt\n$$\n\nBut here we\'re getting ahead of ourselves. All that matters now is that you, as the mathematician, find yourself wondering about this mystery function A(x) which gives the area under the graph of xÂ² between some fixed left point, and some variable right point. If you can find a way to compute this explicitly, you will be inventing a big part of calculus.\nä½†æ­¤å¤„è¶…çº²ã€‚é‡è¦çš„æ˜¯ï¼šä½œä¸ºæ•°å­¦å®¶ï¼Œä½ å¯¹è¿™ä¸ªç¥žç§˜å‡½æ•°A(x)å……æ»¡å¥½å¥‡â€”â€”å®ƒç»™å‡ºå›ºå®šå·¦ç«¯ç‚¹åˆ°å¯å˜å³ç«¯ç‚¹é—´xÂ²å›¾åƒä¸‹çš„é¢ç§¯ã€‚è‹¥èƒ½æ‰¾åˆ°æ˜Žç¡®è®¡ç®—æ–¹æ³•ï¼Œä½ å°†å‘æ˜Žå¾®ç§¯åˆ†çš„é‡è¦éƒ¨åˆ†ã€‚\n\nAgain, the reason we care about this kind of question is not just for the sake of asking hard geometry questions; it\'s because many practical problems that can be approximated by adding up a large number of small things can be reframed as a question about the area under a certain curve.\næˆ‘ä»¬å…³æ³¨æ­¤ç±»é—®é¢˜ä¸ä»…å› å‡ ä½•éš¾é¢˜æœ¬èº«ï¼Œè€Œæ˜¯ç”±äºŽè®¸å¤šå¯è¿‘ä¼¼ä¸ºå¤§é‡å°é‡ä¹‹å’Œçš„å®žé™…é—®é¢˜ï¼Œéƒ½èƒ½é‡æž„ä¸ºæŸæ›²çº¿ä¸‹é¢ç§¯çš„æ±‚è§£ã€‚\n\nDerivatives\nå¯¼æ•°\n\nFinding the area represented by this integral function is genuinely hard. And whenever you come across a genuinely hard question in math, a good policy is to not try too hard to get to the answer directly, since you often just end up banging your head against a wall.\næ±‚æ­¤ç§¯åˆ†å‡½æ•°è¡¨ç¤ºçš„é¢ç§¯ä¸ºçœŸæ­£éš¾é¢˜ã€‚æ•°å­¦ä¸­é­é‡çœŸæ­£éš¾é¢˜æ—¶ï¼Œè‰¯ç­–æ˜¯å‹¿å¼ºè¡Œç›´æŽ¥æ±‚è§£ï¼ˆå¸¸å¾’åŠ³æ— åŠŸï¼‰ï¼Œè€Œåº”ä¸Žé—®é¢˜å»ºç«‹è”ç³»ã€‚\n\nInstead, play around with this idea. Build up some familiarity with the interplay between the function defining a graph, in this case xÂ², and the function giving the area, our unknown function A(x), and see if there are any other questions you can ask about the setup.\nè½¬è€ŒæŠŠçŽ©è¯¥æ€æƒ³ï¼šç†Ÿæ‚‰å®šä¹‰å›¾åƒçš„å‡½æ•°ï¼ˆæ­¤å¤„xÂ²ï¼‰ä¸Žé¢ç§¯å‡½æ•°ï¼ˆæœªçŸ¥A(x)ï¼‰é—´çš„äº¤äº’ä½œç”¨ï¼Œå°è¯•æå‡ºå…³äºŽæ­¤æ¡†æž¶çš„å…¶ä»–é—®é¢˜ã€‚\n\nIn that playful spirit, if you\'re lucky, here\'s something you might notice. When you slightly increase x by some tiny nudge, dx, look at the resulting change in area, represented by this sliver which I\'m going to call dA, for a tiny difference in area.\nå€Ÿæ­¤æŽ¢ç´¢ç²¾ç¥žï¼Œå¹¸è¿çš„è¯ä½ å¯èƒ½å‘çŽ°ï¼šå½“å°†xå¾®å¢ždxæ—¶ï¼Œè§‚å¯Ÿé¢ç§¯çš„ç›¸åº”å˜åŒ–ï¼ˆè®°ä¸ºdAï¼Œå¾®å°é¢ç§¯å·®ï¼‰ã€‚\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_18.png](/api/media/32/download)\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_18.png](/api/media/32/download)\n\nThat sliver can be pretty well approximated with a rectangle, one whose height is xÂ² and whose width is dx. Well, for larger choices of dx the approximation may not be that good, but for smaller and smaller choices of dx the error between the area of that sliver and the area of the approximating rectangle will become tiny compared to the area of the rectangle.\næ­¤ç»†é•¿åŒºåŸŸå¯è¾ƒå¥½è¿‘ä¼¼ä¸ºé«˜xÂ²ã€å®½dxçš„çŸ©å½¢ã€‚è¾ƒå¤§çš„dxå¯èƒ½ä½¿è¿‘ä¼¼æ¬ ä½³ï¼Œä½†éšç€dxå‡å°ï¼Œç»†é•¿åŒºåŸŸé¢ç§¯ä¸ŽçŸ©å½¢é¢ç§¯çš„è¯¯å·®ç›¸å¯¹äºŽçŸ©å½¢é¢ç§¯æœ¬èº«å°†å˜å¾—å¾®ä¸è¶³é“ã€‚\n\nThis may prompt you to think about how A(x) is related to xÂ² in a pretty fun way. A change to the output of A, this little dA, is about equal to the height of the rectangle times its width.\nè¿™æç¤ºä½ ä»¥æœ‰è¶£æ–¹å¼æ€è€ƒA(x)ä¸ŽxÂ²çš„å…³ç³»ï¼šAçš„è¾“å‡ºå˜åŒ–dAçº¦ç­‰äºŽçŸ©å½¢é«˜ä¹˜å®½ã€‚\n\n$$\ndA â‰ˆ xÂ² dx\n$$\n\nHere, x is the input where you started and dx is the size of the little nudge the input that caused A to change.\næ­¤å¤„çš„xæ˜¯èµ·å§‹è¾“å…¥å€¼ï¼Œdxæ˜¯å¯¼è‡´Aå˜åŒ–çš„è¾“å…¥å¾®å°è°ƒæ•´é‡ã€‚\n\nWe could also rearrange that slightly:\nå¯ç¨ä½œå˜å½¢ï¼š\n\n$$\n\\frac{dA}{dx} \\approx xÂ²\n$$\n\nThis says the ratio of a tiny change in A to the tiny change in x that caused it equals the height of our graph, xÂ², at that point. Or rather, this is only approximately true, but it\'s an approximation that should get better and better for smaller choices of dx.\nè¿™è¡¨æ˜Žï¼šAçš„å¾®å°å˜åŒ–ä¸Žå¼•å‘è¯¥å˜åŒ–çš„xå¾®å°å˜åŒ–ä¹‹æ¯”ç­‰äºŽå›¾åƒåœ¨è¯¥ç‚¹çš„é«˜åº¦xÂ²ã€‚å°½ç®¡ä»…ä¸ºè¿‘ä¼¼ç­‰å¼ï¼Œéšç€dxå‡å°ï¼Œè¿‘ä¼¼ç²¾åº¦ä¼šè¶Šæ¥è¶Šé«˜ã€‚\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_19.png](/api/media/33/download)\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_19.png](/api/media/33/download)\n\nFor example, think about two nearby inputs, like 3 and 3.001. The change to x, then, is dx=0.001. The change dA would be the difference between the mystery function evaluated at 3.001 and evaluated at 3. Even though we don\'t know what that mystery function is, we do know something about this change, namely that this change divided by dx=0.001 is approximately 3Â².\nä¾‹å¦‚ï¼šå–é‚»è¿‘è¾“å…¥3å’Œ3.001ï¼Œxçš„å˜åŒ–é‡dx=0.001ã€‚dAå³ç¥žç§˜å‡½æ•°åœ¨3.001ä¸Ž3å¤„çš„å€¼å·®ã€‚å°½ç®¡æœªçŸ¥å‡½æ•°å½¢å¼ï¼Œä½†æˆ‘ä»¬çŸ¥é“æ­¤å˜åŒ–é‡é™¤ä»¥dxâ‰ˆ3Â²ã€‚\n\nAnd this relationship between tiny changes to the mystery function and the value of xÂ² is true at all inputs, not just 3. For example, we can see the same relationship at the point x=2 on the graph of xÂ².\nç¥žç§˜å‡½æ•°å¾®å°å˜åŒ–ä¸ŽxÂ²å€¼çš„æ­¤å…³ç³»å¯¹æ‰€æœ‰è¾“å…¥æˆç«‹ï¼Œä¸ä»…é™3ã€‚ä¾‹å¦‚åœ¨x=2å¤„åŒæ ·å¯è§æ­¤å…³ç³»ã€‚\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_20.png](/api/media/34/download)\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_20.png](/api/media/34/download)\n\nThat doesn\'t immediately tell us how to find A(x), but it provides a very strong clue to work with. And there\'s nothing special about the graph xÂ² here. For any function f(x), if we call the area under its graph A(x), then this area function has the property that dA/dxâ‰ˆf(x), a slight nudge to the output of A divided by a slight nudge to the input that caused it is about equal to the height of the graph at that point, f(x). Again, that\'s an approximation that gets better and better for smaller choices of dx.\nè™½æœªç›´æŽ¥ç»™å‡ºA(x)ï¼Œä½†æä¾›äº†æžå¼ºçº¿ç´¢ã€‚æ­¤å…³ç³»ä¸ä»…é™äºŽxÂ²å›¾åƒï¼šå¯¹ä»»æ„å‡½æ•°f(x)ï¼Œè‹¥ç§°å…¶å›¾åƒä¸‹é¢ç§¯ä¸ºA(x)ï¼Œåˆ™é¢ç§¯å‡½æ•°æ»¡è¶³dA/dxâ‰ˆf(x)ï¼ˆAè¾“å‡ºå¾®å°å˜åŒ–é™¤ä»¥å¼•å‘è¯¥å˜åŒ–çš„è¾“å…¥å¾®å°å˜åŒ–â‰ˆå›¾åƒåœ¨è¯¥ç‚¹é«˜åº¦f(x)ï¼‰ã€‚éšç€dxå‡å°ï¼Œè¿‘ä¼¼æ€§æ„ˆå‘ç²¾ç¡®ã€‚\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_21.png](/api/media/35/download)\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_21.png](/api/media/35/download)\n\nThis figure illustrates the property of dA/dx for a more general function f(x).\næ­¤å›¾å±•ç¤ºäº†ä¸€èˆ¬å‡½æ•°f(x)çš„dA/dxå±žæ€§ã€‚\n\nHere we\'re stumbling onto another big idea from calculus: Derivatives. This ratio dA/dx is called the â€œderivative of Aâ€. Or, more technically, the derivative is whatever value this ratio approaches as dx gets smaller and smaller.\næˆ‘ä»¬ç”±æ­¤é‚‚é€…å¾®ç§¯åˆ†çš„å¦ä¸€æ ¸å¿ƒæ€æƒ³ï¼šå¯¼æ•°ã€‚æ¯”å€¼dA/dxç§°ä¸ºâ€œAçš„å¯¼æ•°â€ã€‚æ›´æŠ€æœ¯åŒ–åœ°è¯´ï¼Œå¯¼æ•°æ˜¯è¯¥æ¯”å€¼éšdxè¶‹è¿‘äºŽ0æ—¶çš„æžé™å€¼ã€‚\n\nYou and I will dive more deeply into the idea of a derivative next chapter, but loosely speaking it\'s a measure of how sensitive a function is to small changes in its input. You\'ll see as the series goes on that there are many different ways to visualize a derivative, depending on what function you\'re looking at and how you think about tiny nudges to its output.\nä¸‹ç« æˆ‘ä»¬å°†æ·±å…¥å¯¼æ•°æ¦‚å¿µã€‚ç²—ç•¥è®²ï¼Œå¯¼æ•°è¡¡é‡å‡½æ•°å¯¹è¾“å…¥å¾®å°å˜åŒ–çš„æ•æ„Ÿåº¦ã€‚éšç€ç³»åˆ—æŽ¨è¿›ï¼Œä½ å°†çœ‹åˆ°å¯¼æ•°å¯è§†åŒ–çš„å¤šæ ·æ–¹å¼ï¼ˆå–å†³äºŽå‡½æ•°ç±»åž‹åŠå¯¹å…¶è¾“å‡ºå¾®å°å˜åŒ–çš„æ€è€ƒæ–¹å¼ï¼‰ã€‚\n\nFundamental Theorem\nå¾®ç§¯åˆ†åŸºæœ¬å®šç†\n\nWe care about derivatives because they help us solve problems, and in our little exploration here we have a slight glimpse of one way they\'re used: They are the key to solving integral problems; problems that require finding an area under a curve. When you gain enough familiarity with computing derivatives, you\'ll be able to look at a situation like the one below, where you don\'t know what a function is, but you do know that its derivative should be xÂ², and from that reverse engineer what the function must be.\nå¯¼æ•°ä¹‹æ‰€ä»¥é‡è¦å› å…¶åŠ©è§£éš¾é¢˜ã€‚æ­¤æŽ¢ç´¢ä¸­æˆ‘ä»¬çž¥è§å…¶ç”¨é€”ä¹‹ä¸€ï¼šä½œä¸ºè§£å†³ç§¯åˆ†é—®é¢˜ï¼ˆæ±‚æ›²çº¿ä¸‹é¢ç§¯ï¼‰çš„é’¥åŒ™ã€‚ç†Ÿç»ƒæŽŒæ¡å¯¼æ•°è®¡ç®—åŽï¼Œé¢å¯¹æœªçŸ¥å‡½æ•°ä½†çŸ¥å…¶å¯¼æ•°åº”ä¸ºxÂ²çš„åœºæ™¯ï¼Œä½ èƒ½é€†å‘æŽ¨å¯¼å‡ºè¯¥å‡½æ•°ã€‚\n\nThis back and forth between integrals and derivatives, where the derivative of the function for an area under a graph gives you back the function defining the graph itself, is called the fundamental theorem of calculus. It ties together the two big ideas of integrals and derivatives, and shows how in some sense, each one is the inverse of the other.\nç§¯åˆ†ä¸Žå¯¼æ•°é—´çš„æ­¤äº’åŠ¨å…³ç³»ï¼ˆå›¾åƒä¸‹é¢ç§¯å‡½æ•°çš„å¯¼æ•°è¿”å›žå®šä¹‰å›¾åƒæœ¬èº«çš„å‡½æ•°ï¼‰ç§°ä¸ºå¾®ç§¯åˆ†åŸºæœ¬å®šç†ã€‚å®ƒå°†ç§¯åˆ†ä¸Žå¯¼æ•°ä¸¤å¤§æ€æƒ³ç»“åˆï¼Œå±•ç¤ºäº†ä¸¤è€…åœ¨æŸç§æ„ä¹‰ä¸Šçš„äº’é€†æ€§ã€‚\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_22.png](/api/media/36/download)\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_22.png](/api/media/36/download)\n\nThis figure illustrates the property of dA/dx for a more general function f(x).\næ­¤å›¾å±•ç¤ºäº†ä¸€èˆ¬å‡½æ•°f(x)çš„dA/dxå±žæ€§ã€‚\n\nAll of this is only a high level view. What follows in this series are the details for both these big ideas, and more. And let me reiterate, at all points in this series I want you to feel like you could have invented calculus yourself, that if you drew the right pictures and played with each idea in the right way, all of the formulas, rules, and constructs could pop out quite naturally.\nä»¥ä¸Šä»…ä¸ºé«˜å±‚æ¬¡æ¦‚è§ˆã€‚æœ¬ç³»åˆ—åŽç»­å°†è¯¦è¿°è¿™äº›æ ¸å¿ƒç†å¿µä¹ƒè‡³æ›´å¤šã€‚é‡ç”³ï¼šæˆ‘å§‹ç»ˆå¸Œæœ›è®©ä½ æ„Ÿè§‰æœ¬å¯è‡ªåˆ›å¾®ç§¯åˆ†â€”â€”è‹¥ç»˜åˆ¶æ­£ç¡®å›¾ç¤ºå¹¶ä»¥æ°å½“æ–¹å¼æŠŠçŽ©æ€æƒ³ï¼Œæ‰€æœ‰å…¬å¼ã€è§„åˆ™ä¸Žç»“æž„çš†èƒ½è‡ªç„¶æ¶ŒçŽ°ã€‚\n\nBonus section: So what is dx anyway?\nåŠ é¤ï¼šdxç©¶ç«Ÿæ˜¯ä»€ä¹ˆï¼Ÿ\n\nCalculus is littered with expressions like dA, dr, dx, etc, which show up in the notation for both derivatives and integrals. Despite their front-and-center role, there is a surprising amount of ambiguity and conflicting instruction on what these terms really mean.\nå¾®ç§¯åˆ†å……æ»¡å¦‚dAã€drã€dxç­‰ç¬¦å·ï¼Œè§äºŽå¯¼æ•°ä¸Žç§¯åˆ†ã€‚å°½ç®¡åœ°ä½æ ¸å¿ƒï¼Œå…³äºŽå…¶çœŸå®žå«ä¹‰å´å­˜åœ¨æƒŠäººæ­§ä¹‰ä¸ŽçŸ›ç›¾è§£é‡Šã€‚\n\nIn this chapter I encouraged you to think of dr as the difference in the radius of our circles from one to the next, prompting you to take a very literal interpretation and imagine an actual number, like dr=0.01. Likewise, I encouraged you to think of dA as an actual number, specifically the amount that the area under a graph changes as we nudge the right endpoint by dx, again an actual number. A more common convention would be to call these changes Î”r, Î”A, and Î”x, reserving the dx notation for changes that are â€œinfinitely smallâ€.\næœ¬ç« æˆ‘é¼“åŠ±ä½ å°†drè§†ä¸ºç›¸é‚»çŽ¯çš„åŠå¾„å·®ï¼Œå­—é¢ç†è§£ä¸ºå®žé™…æ•°å­—å¦‚dr=0.01ã€‚åŒæ ·ï¼ŒdAè§†ä¸ºå®žé™…æ•°å€¼ï¼ˆç§»åŠ¨å³ç«¯ç‚¹dxæ—¶å›¾åƒä¸‹é¢ç§¯çš„å˜åŒ–é‡ï¼‰ã€‚æ›´æƒ¯ä¾‹æ˜¯ç”¨Î”rã€Î”Aã€Î”xè¡¨ç¤ºæœ‰é™å˜åŒ–ï¼Œè€Œdxä¿ç•™ç»™â€œæ— é™å°â€å˜åŒ–ã€‚\n\nInfinitely small?\næ— é™å°ï¼Ÿ\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_23.svg](/api/media/37/download)\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_23.svg](/api/media/37/download)\n\nThe issue with this is that phrases like â€œinfinitely smallâ€ and â€œinfinitesimalâ€ run the risk of, well, not actually making any sense. What is an infinitesimal change? Is it a number? If so, which number? It shouldn\'t be zero, but it also shouldn\'t be anything bigger than zero. If it\'s not a number then what is it, exactly?\né—®é¢˜åœ¨äºŽï¼šâ€œæ— é™å°â€â€œæ— ç©·å°â€ç­‰çŸ­è¯­å¯èƒ½å¹¶æ— å®žé™…æ„ä¹‰ã€‚æ— ç©·å°å˜åŒ–æ˜¯æ•°å—ï¼Ÿè‹¥æ˜¯ï¼Œæ˜¯å“ªä¸ªæ•°ï¼Ÿå®ƒä¸åº”ä¸ºé›¶ï¼Œå´ä¹Ÿä¸åº”å¤§äºŽé›¶ã€‚è‹¥éžæ•°ï¼Œå®ƒç©¶ç«Ÿæ˜¯ä»€ä¹ˆï¼Ÿ\n\nIn acknowledgment of this conundrum, one common sentiment is to encourage students not to take this notation too seriously. The typical dictum is that derivatives, despite being written as df/dx, are not really fractions, and that integrals, written as âˆ«f(x)dx, are not really sums, despite the fact that the symbol âˆ« literally derives from the letter \"S\" for \"Sum\". Instead, the student is told, each of these constructs has a separate more complicated definition. But! If a student wishes for loose intuition before stepping up their game to a real analysis class, they might find it helpful to think of dx as an infinitely small change in x, even if that phrase doesn\'t quite make sense. It is, after all, only intuition.\né‰´äºŽæ­¤å›°å¢ƒï¼Œå¸¸è§å»ºè®®æ˜¯è®©å­¦ç”Ÿå‹¿è¿‡åº¦ä¸¥è‚ƒå¯¹å¾…ç¬¦å·ã€‚å…¸åž‹è®­è¯«æ˜¯ï¼šå°½ç®¡å¯¼æ•°å†™ä½œdf/dxï¼Œå®ƒä»¬å®žéžåˆ†æ•°ï¼›ç§¯åˆ†å†™ä½œâˆ«f(x)dxäº¦éžçœŸæ­£çš„å’Œï¼ˆå°½ç®¡âˆ«æºäºŽæ±‚å’Œé¦–å­—æ¯\"S\"ï¼‰ã€‚å­¦ä¹ è€…è¢«å‘ŠçŸ¥è¿™äº›ç»“æž„å„æœ‰æ›´å¤æ‚çš„å®šä¹‰ã€‚ä½†!è‹¥å­¦ç”Ÿåœ¨å­¦ä¹ å®žåˆ†æžå‰å¯»æ±‚ç›´è§‚ç†è§£ï¼Œå¯å…ˆè¡Œå°†dxè§†ä¸ºxçš„æ— ç©·å°å˜åŒ–â€”â€”å³ä¾¿æ­¤è¯´æ³•æ¬ ä¸¥è°¨ï¼Œç»ˆå½’ä»…ä¸ºç›´è§‚ã€‚\n\nTo my taste, this sentiment is well-intentioned but ultimately misleading. The rigorous underpinnings of calculus only ever involve real, finitely sized numbers, often followed by questions of what happens as these values get closer to 0. The proposal of this series is that when we interpret the notation of calculus, we can do so in a way which maps closely to this rigorous underpinning. If you\'re anything like I am, this actually makes calculus much more tangible and understandable than any â€œintuitionâ€ resting on the idea of an infinitesimal.\nä¾æˆ‘ä¹‹è§ï¼Œæ­¤å»ºè®®å–„æ„ä½†æœ€ç»ˆè¯¯å¯¼ã€‚å¾®ç§¯åˆ†çš„ä¸¥æ ¼åŸºç¡€ä»…æ¶‰å®žæ•°ä¸Žæœ‰é™å€¼ï¼Œå¸¸ä¼´éšâ€œå½“è¿™äº›å€¼è¶‹è¿‘äºŽ0æ—¶â€çš„æé—®ã€‚æœ¬ç³»åˆ—æè®®ï¼šè§£é‡Šå¾®ç§¯åˆ†ç¬¦å·æ—¶å¯ç´§å¯†æ˜ å°„äºŽæ­¤ä¸¥æ ¼åŸºç¡€ã€‚è‹¥ä½ å¦‚æˆ‘ä¸€èˆ¬ï¼Œè¿™å®žé™…ä¸Šä½¿å¾®ç§¯åˆ†æ¯”ä»»ä½•åŸºäºŽæ— ç©·å°çš„â€œç›´è§‚â€æ›´å¯è§¦å¯ç†è§£ã€‚\n\nIn a nutshell, here\'s the philosophy for this series: When you see expressions with terms like dx, think of those terms as representing actual small numbers, say dx=0.001. When you do this, the equations you write will become wrong, but they get less wrong as the choice for dx approaches 0. Think of the circular rings from earlier in this chapter. If you thought of dr as an actual number, say dr=0.001, none of these rings actually had an area of 2Ï€rdr. However, the percent error between the true area and 2Ï€râ‹…dr gets smaller and smaller as the choice for dr does as well.\nç®€è¨€ä¹‹ï¼Œæœ¬ç³»åˆ—å“²å­¦æ˜¯ï¼šé‡dxç­‰ç¬¦å·æ—¶ï¼Œå°†å…¶è§†ä¸ºå®žé™…å°æ•°ï¼ˆå¦‚dx=0.001ï¼‰ã€‚æ­¤ä¸¾å°†ä½¿ç­‰å¼å­˜åœ¨è¯¯å·®ï¼Œä½†éšç€dxè¶‹è¿‘0ï¼Œè¯¯å·®è¶Šæ¥è¶Šå°ã€‚å›žæƒ³æœ¬ç« åŒå¿ƒçŽ¯ï¼šè‹¥è§†drä¸º0.001ï¼Œæ‰€æœ‰çŽ¯çš„çœŸå®žé¢ç§¯â‰ 2Ï€rdrï¼Œä½†çœŸå®žé¢ç§¯ä¸Ž2Ï€râ‹…drçš„ç™¾åˆ†æ¯”è¯¯å·®éšç€drå‡å°è€Œé™ä½Žã€‚\n\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_24.svg](/api/media/38/download)\n![Chapter 1The Essence of Calculus_24.svg](/api/media/38/download)\n\nThere are more details and rules of thumb for how you replace terms like df and dx with small numbers, and we\'ll address them as the series unfolds, but that\'s the main idea.\nå…³äºŽå¦‚ä½•ç”¨å°æ•°å€¼æ›¿ä»£dfä¸Ždxç­‰ç¬¦å·ï¼Œè¿˜æœ‰æ›´å¤šç»†èŠ‚ä¸Žç»éªŒæ³•åˆ™ï¼Œç³»åˆ—åŽç»­å°†æŽ¢è®¨ï¼Œä½†ä¸»æ—¨å³æ­¤ã€‚\n\nI mention all this now so that you know this is not the universal convention; again finite changes are more typically written with the greek letter Î”, as in an expression like Î”x=0.001. I also want to assure you that the spirit of this notational philosophy is not to compromise rigor for intuition, but to do the exact opposite.\næ­¤åˆ»æåŠæ˜¯ä¸ºè¯´æ˜Žæ­¤éžæ™®é€‚æƒ¯ä¾‹ï¼šæœ‰é™å˜åŒ–é€šå¸¸ç”¨Î”è¡¨ç¤ºï¼ˆå¦‚Î”x=0.001ï¼‰ã€‚æˆ‘è¿˜éœ€ç¡®ä¿ä½ ç†è§£ï¼šæ­¤ç¬¦å·å“²å­¦çš„ç²¾ç¥žå¹¶éžä¸ºç›´è§‚ç‰ºç‰²ä¸¥æ ¼æ€§ï¼Œè€Œæ˜¯ç›¸åã€‚\n\nAnd now, without further philosophical delay, let\'s dive into what exactly a derivative is.\nçŽ°åœ¨ï¼Œæ— æ›´å¤šå“²å­¦å»¶å®•ï¼Œæˆ‘ä»¬æ·±å…¥æŽ¢è®¨å¯¼æ•°çš„ç¡®åˆ‡å«ä¹‰ã€‚\n\nThanks\nè‡´è°¢\n\nSpecial thanks to those below for supporting the original video behind this post, and to current patrons for funding ongoing projects. If you find these lessons valuable, consider joining.\nç‰¹åˆ«æ„Ÿè°¢ä»¥ä¸‹æ”¯æŒåŽŸå§‹è§†é¢‘åˆ¶ä½œçš„äººå‘˜ï¼Œä»¥åŠå½“å‰èµžåŠ©æŒç»­é¡¹ç›®çš„èµ„åŠ©è€…ã€‚è‹¥è§‰è¯¾ç¨‹æœ‰ä»·å€¼ï¼Œè¯·è€ƒè™‘åŠ å…¥ã€‚','PLAIN_TEXT','PLAIN_TEXT','PLAIN_TEXT','PLAIN_TEXT','PLAIN_TEXT');
